would depend on the specific topic.'),(7998,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(7999,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8000,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8001,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8002,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8003,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8004,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8005,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8006,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8007,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8008,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8009,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8010,'fd4c41d7',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8011,'7dc7facd',49,'Which process is responsible for the decrease in temperature during the cooling of water in an earthen pot (Matka)?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"Condensation\", \"Sublimation\", \"Evaporation\", \"Melting\"]','C'),(8012,'7dc7facd',50,'If an object moves at a constant velocity, which of the following statements about its motion is true?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"Its acceleration is positive.\", \"A constant net force is acting on it.\", \"Its acceleration is zero.\", \"Its speed is increasing.\"]','C'),(8013,'7dc7facd',51,'The powerhouse of the cell is known as:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"Ribosome\", \"Mitochondria\", \"Endoplasmic Reticulum\", \"Lysosome\"]','B'),(8014,'7dc7facd',52,'Which of the following elements has a valency of 4?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"Sodium (Na)\", \"Oxygen (O)\", \"Carbon (C)\", \"Magnesium (Mg)\"]','C'),(8015,'7dc7facd',53,'Which type of disease spread relies primarily on vectors, and what is a common example?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"Air-borne; Tuberculosis\", \"Water-borne; Cholera\", \"Vector-borne; Malaria\", \"Sexual contact; AIDS\"]','C'),(8016,'7dc7facd',54,'A) Differentiate between meristematic tissues and permanent tissues in plants based on their dividing capacity and function. \nB) Explain the importance of cross-breeding and hybridisation in the context of Improvement in Food Resources.','Long Answer','Easy',7,'null','A) Meristematic tissues consist of actively dividing cells, responsible for growth, and are localized (apical, lateral, intercalary). Permanent tissues are derived from meristematic tissues, have lost the ability to divide, and perform specific functions (protection, conduction, support). B) Cross-breeding and hybridization are crucial for improving crop yields and quality by developing varieties with desirable characteristics like high yield, disease resistance, better quality produce, and adaptability to specific climatic conditions.'),(8017,'7dc7facd',55,'A) State the Law of Conservation of Energy. A body of mass 10 kg is lifted to a height of 5 m. Calculate the potential energy gained by the body (g = 9.8 m/s²). \nB) If this body is allowed to fall freely, what will its kinetic energy be just before it hits the ground? Justify your answer using the Law of Conservation of Energy.','Long Answer','Medium',7,'null','A) Law of Conservation of Energy states that energy can only be converted from one form to another; it cannot be created or destroyed. Total energy remains constant.\nPotential Energy (PE) = mgh = 10 kg * 9.8 m/s² * 5 m = 490 Joules.\nB) Just before hitting the ground, all the Potential Energy (490 J) will be converted entirely into Kinetic Energy (KE), assuming no energy loss due to air resistance. Therefore, KE = 490 J. This is justified by the Law of Conservation of Energy, where Total Energy (initial) = Total Energy (final), or PE (initial) = KE (final).'),(8018,'7dc7facd',56,'A) Define a homogeneous mixture and give two examples. \nB) Describe the method you would use to separate a mixture containing common salt (Sodium Chloride) and ammonium chloride. Explain the principle involved in the separation of these two components.','Long Answer','Easy',7,'null','A) A homogeneous mixture has a uniform composition throughout; its components are indistinguishable. Examples: Salt solution, sugar solution, air. \nB) Method: Sublimation. Ammonium chloride is a sublime substance (it changes directly from solid to gas upon heating) while common salt does not. The mixture is heated in a china dish covered with an inverted funnel plugged with cotton. Ammonium chloride vaporizes and solidifies upon hitting the cooler inner surface of the funnel, while salt remains behind in the dish. \nPrinciple: Differential tendency of components to sublime upon heating.'),(8019,'7dc7facd',57,'Explain the characteristics of the Carbon Cycle. How is atmospheric carbon dioxide primarily maintained in nature, and what human activities disrupt this balance? (Referencing Natural Resources)','Long Answer','Medium',7,'null','Characteristics of the Carbon Cycle: Carbon is cycled through the atmosphere, hydrosphere, lithosphere, and biosphere, mainly through photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition, and burning of fossil fuels.\nMaintenance of CO₂: The atmosphere\'s carbon dioxide concentration is primarily maintained by the balance between photosynthesis (which absorbs CO₂) and respiration/decomposition (which releases CO₂).\nDisrupting Activities: \n1. Combustion: Burning of fossil fuels (coal, petroleum) releases large quantities of stored carbon into the atmosphere as CO₂.\n2. Deforestation: Removal of forests reduces the amount of biomass available to absorb atmospheric CO₂ via photosynthesis, leading to an increased concentration of CO₂ in the atmosphere.'),(8020,'7dc7facd',58,'A) Describe Rutherford’s alpha-particle scattering experiment and state the two main conclusions he derived regarding the structure of the atom. \nB) Define the terms Atomic Number (Z) and Mass Number (A). An element has 18 electrons and a mass number of 35. Determine the number of protons, neutrons, and its atomic number. (Assume the atom is neutral).','Long Answer','Hard',7,'null','A) Experiment: Rutherford bombarded a very thin gold foil with fast-moving alpha (α) particles. He observed that most α-particles passed straight through, a few were deflected at small angles, and very few rebounded back. \nConclusions: 1) Most of the space inside the atom is empty. 2) The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom are concentrated in a very small central region called the nucleus. \nB) Atomic Number (Z): The total number of protons present in the nucleus of an atom. Mass Number (A): The sum of the number of protons and the number of neutrons in the nucleus.\nCalculation: For a neutral atom, Protons = Electrons. Thus, Protons = 18. Atomic Number (Z) = Number of Protons = 18. Mass Number (A) = 35. Number of Neutrons = A - Z = 35 - 18 = 17.'),(8021,'f3a95110',59,'Which of the following reaction types is represented by the reaction: $\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3 + 2\\text{Al} \\rightarrow 2\\text{Fe} + \\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"Combination reaction\", \"Decomposition reaction\", \"Displacement reaction\", \"Double displacement reaction\"]','C'),(8022,'f3a95110',60,'The site of photosynthesis in the cells of the leaf is:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"Mitochondria\", \"Cytoplasm\", \"Chloroplasts\", \"Vacuoles\"]','C'),(8023,'f3a95110',61,'A student measures the pH of four different solutions (P, Q, R, S). P=12, Q=2, R=7, S=5. Which solution is strongly acidic?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"P\", \"Q\", \"R\", \"S\"]','B'),(8024,'f3a95110',62,'If a current-carrying straight conductor is held vertically, and the magnetic field lines around it are circles, then the direction of the magnetic field at a point directly east of the conductor, when the current flows upwards, will be:','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"North\", \"South\", \"East\", \"West\"]','A'),(8025,'f3a95110',63,'Which phenomenon is responsible for the splitting of white light into its seven constituent colours when passing through a prism?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"Reflection\", \"Scattering\", \"Total Internal Reflection\", \"Dispersion\"]','D'),(8026,'f3a95110',64,'Highly reactive metals like Sodium and Potassium are extracted by the process of ______________________ of their molten chlorides.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','Electrolysis'),(8027,'f3a95110',65,'The functional group present in ethanol and methanol is ______________________.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','Hydroxyl group (-OH)'),(8028,'f3a95110',66,'In the process of fission in Amoeba, the division of the nucleus is called ______________________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','Karyokinesis'),(8029,'f3a95110',67,'The tendency of an organism to survive and reproduce in its environment is called ______________________.','Fill in the Blanks','Hard',1,'null','Natural Selection (or Survival of the Fittest)'),(8030,'f3a95110',68,'The ability of carbon atoms to link with one another to form long chains, branched chains, and rings is called ______________________.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','Catenation'),(8031,'f3a95110',69,'State the Law of Conservation of Mass. Balance the following chemical equation: $\\text{BaCl}_2 + \\text{Na}_2\\text{SO}_4 \\rightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4 + \\text{NaCl}$.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','The Law of Conservation of Mass states that mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction. The total mass of reactants must equal the total mass of products. Balanced equation: $\\text{BaCl}_2 + \\text{Na}_2\\text{SO}_4 \\rightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4 + 2\\text{NaCl}$.'),(8032,'f3a95110',70,'Differentiate between Aerobic and Anaerobic respiration based on the following criteria: (a) Requirement of Oxygen (b) End Products (c) Amount of Energy released.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Aerobic respiration requires oxygen; its end products are $\\text{CO}_2$, $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$, and a large amount of energy ($\\approx 38$ ATP). Anaerobic respiration occurs without oxygen; its end products are ethanol (in yeast) or lactic acid (in muscle cells), $\\text{CO}_2$ (in yeast), and a small amount of energy ($\\approx 2$ ATP).'),(8033,'f3a95110',71,'Explain the characteristics of the image formed when an object is placed at the centre of curvature (C) of a concave mirror. Draw a neat ray diagram to support your answer.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','When an object is placed at C of a concave mirror, the image is formed (i) at C itself, (ii) Real and inverted, and (iii) of the same size as the object. The ray diagram should show two rays (e.g., parallel to principal axis passing through F, and through F becoming parallel) intersecting exactly at C.'),(8034,'f3a95110',72,'What is the action of steam on iron? Name the products formed and write the balanced chemical equation for the reaction.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','When steam is passed over hot iron, it reacts to form Iron(II, III) oxide ($\\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4$) and Hydrogen gas ($\\text{H}_2$).\nEquation: $3\\text{Fe} (s) + 4\\text{H}_2\\text{O} (g) \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4 (s) + 4\\text{H}_2 (g)$.'),(8035,'f3a95110',73,'Draw a labelled diagram of the reflex arc and explain the role of the spinal cord in a reflex action.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','A labelled diagram should show: Receptor $\\rightarrow$ Sensory Neuron $\\rightarrow$ Spinal Cord (Relay Neuron) $\\rightarrow$ Motor Neuron $\\rightarrow$ Effector (Muscle). The spinal cord acts as the processing centre in a reflex action. It allows rapid response by forming the connection between the sensory and motor neurons, bypassing the brain and providing immediate response necessary for survival.'),(8036,'f3a95110',74,'Define electric potential difference and state Ohm\'s law. Draw a schematic diagram of a circuit consisting of a battery of three cells of 2 V each, a $5\\Omega$ resistor, an $8\\Omega$ resistor, a $12\\Omega$ resistor, and a plug key, all connected in series.','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','Electric Potential Difference (V) is the work done to move a unit charge from one point to another in an electric field. Ohm\'s law states that the potential difference (V) across the ends of a metallic conductor is directly proportional to the current (I) flowing through it, provided its temperature remains constant ($V \\propto I$ or $V = IR$). The schematic diagram must show three 2V cells connected in series (Total Voltage 6V), followed by the key, and then the three resistors (5, 8, and 12 ohms) connected sequentially in a single path.'),(8037,'f3a95110',75,'Describe the process of double circulation in human beings. Why is double circulation necessary in humans?','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','Double circulation involves two separate circulatory pathways: the pulmonary circulation (between the heart and lungs) and the systemic circulation (between the heart and the rest of the body). Blood flows twice through the heart to complete one cycle. Process: (1) Deoxygenated blood enters the Right Atrium, moves to Right Ventricle, and is pumped to the Lungs (Pulmonary Artery). (2) Oxygenated blood returns from the Lungs to the Left Atrium, moves to the Left Ventricle, and is pumped to the body (Aorta). Double circulation is necessary in humans (and birds/mammals) because it ensures complete separation of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood. This highly efficient oxygen supply is crucial for maintaining the constant, high energy demands required by warm-blooded animals.'),(8038,'f3a95110',76,'What are homologous series? Give three characteristics of a homologous series. Define and explain the chemical reaction of saponification with a suitable chemical equation.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','Homologous series is a series of compounds in which the same functional group substitutes for hydrogen in a carbon chain, and successive members differ by a $\\text{CH}_2$ unit. Characteristics: 1. Same general formula (e.g., Alkanes: $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n+2}$). 2. Successive members differ by $14$ u ($\\text{CH}_2$ group). 3. Exhibit a gradation in physical properties (melting/boiling points increase with molecular mass). 4. Similar chemical properties due to the same functional group. Saponification is the process of hydrolysis of fats/oils (esters) by an alkali (like $\\text{NaOH}$ or $\\text{KOH}$) to form soap (Sodium or Potassium salt of a fatty acid) and glycerol. Equation: $\\text{Fat/Oil} + \\text{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\text{Soap} + \\text{Glycerol}$.'),(8039,'f3a95110',77,'With a neat, labelled diagram, explain the principle and working of an electric motor. List two ways to enhance the power of an electric motor.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Principle: An electric motor works on the principle that when a current-carrying conductor is placed in a magnetic field, it experiences a force (Lorentz force). If the direction of current and magnetic field are mutually perpendicular, the force is maximum, causing rotation. Working: Current enters the armature coil through the commutator and brushes. The force on the coil sides (using Fleming\'s Left-Hand Rule) causes rotation. After half a rotation, the commutator reverses the direction of current in the coil, ensuring continuous rotation in the same direction. Enhancement of Power: 1. Increasing the strength of the magnetic field (by using stronger magnets or increasing current in the electromagnet). 2. Increasing the number of turns in the coil. 3. Increasing the area of the coil. 4. Increasing the current flowing through the coil.'),(8040,'f3a95110',78,'Explain Mendel\'s experiment regarding the inheritance of one pair of contrasting traits (monohybrid cross). Show the cross using appropriate symbols up to the $\\text{F}_2$ generation, and state the resulting phenotypic and genotypic ratios.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Mendel crossed pure tall plants (TT) with pure dwarf plants (tt). \\\\ \\text{P Generation: TT} \\times \\text{tt}\\\\ \\text{Gametes: T, t}\\\\ \\text{F}_1 \\text{ Generation: Tt (All Tall)}\\\\ When $\\text{F}_1$ plants are self-pollinated: $\\text{F}_1 \\times \\text{F}_1$: $\\text{Tt} \\times \\text{Tt}$.\\\\ \\text{F}_2 \\text{ Generation} (using Punnett Square): TT, Tt, Tt, tt.\\\\ Phenotypic Ratio: Tall : Dwarf = 3:1.\\\\ Genotypic Ratio: TT : Tt : tt = 1:2:1. \\\\ This experiment demonstrates the Law of Segregation, showing that alleles separate during gamete formation and are randomly united during fertilization.'),(8041,'f3a95110',79,'Read the following passage and answer the questions:\n\nWater is a vital natural resource, and its management is crucial for sustainable development. Traditional methods like \'Khadins\' in Rajasthan and \'Bandharas\' in Maharashtra have been effective in localized water harvesting. Large scale projects like dams and canals often involve significant environmental costs, including biodiversity loss and displacement of people. However, they ensure water supply and electricity generation for large populations. Local participation and decentralized systems, such as rooftop rainwater harvesting, are often considered more environmentally sound and socially equitable approaches to water management.\n\n(i) Identify one traditional method of water harvesting mentioned and state its primary benefit.\n(ii) List two major environmental consequences associated with the construction of large dams.\n(iii) Why is decentralized water management preferred over large dam projects in terms of social equity?\n(iv) Suggest one practical way a school campus can implement water conservation measures.','Case Study','Medium',4,'null','(i) Khadins (Rajasthan) or Bandharas (Maharashtra). Primary benefit: Replenishes groundwater/provides local water supply for agriculture.\n(ii) Environmental consequences: Loss of biodiversity (submerging forests), large-scale deforestation, soil erosion, and changes in microclimates.\n(iii) Decentralized management (like rooftop harvesting) is preferred because it benefits local communities directly, reduces dependence on centralized authority, and minimizes the social cost of displacement and unequal distribution.\n(iv) Implementing rainwater harvesting systems on school roofs or installing low-flow fixtures in washrooms to reduce water usage.'),(8042,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8043,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8044,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8045,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8046,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8047,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8048,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8049,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8050,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8051,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8052,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8053,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8054,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8055,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8056,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8057,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8058,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8059,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8060,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8061,'20f5123f',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8062,'a1427a71',80,'Which term is used to describe the first fully human-like hominids, characterized by a large brain and upright posture?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"Australopithecus\", \"Homo Erectus\", \"Homo Sapiens\", \"Hominoids\"]','C'),(8063,'a1427a71',81,'The primary role of the \'second order\' in medieval European society (The Three Orders) was:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"To pray and teach\", \"To fight and protect\", \"To cultivate land and pay tax\", \"To manage trade and commerce\"]','B'),(8064,'a1427a71',82,'What significant policy was introduced in 1887 in the USA, leading to the fragmentation of Native American tribal lands?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"Indian Removal Act\", \"Dawes General Allotment Act\", \"Trail of Tears Legislation\", \"Citizenship Act\"]','B'),(8065,'a1427a71',83,'The invention that revolutionized the cotton industry in the early stage of the Industrial Revolution by vastly increasing the speed of weaving was the:','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"Spinning Jenny\", \"Water Frame\", \"Flying Shuttle\", \"Steam Engine\"]','C'),(8066,'a1427a71',84,'In the context of \'Changing Cultural Traditions\', which event is considered the starting point of the Protestant Reformation?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"The invention of the printing press\", \"The sack of Rome by Charles V\", \"Martin Luther posting the Ninety-Five Theses\", \"The Council of Trent\"]','C'),(8067,'a1427a71',85,'What was the official capital city established by Chinggis Khan for the Mongol Empire?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"Samarqand\", \"Karakorum\", \"Beijing\", \"Isfahan\"]','B'),(8068,'a1427a71',86,'Which of the following was NOT one of Sun Yat-sen\'s \'Three Principles of the People\' (San Min Zhu Yi)?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"Nationalism (Minzu)\", \"Democracy (Minquan)\", \"Communism (Gongchan)\", \"Livelihood (Minsheng)\"]','C'),(8069,'a1427a71',87,'In the context of the Mongol Empire, the system of relay posts used for swift communication and travel was known as the \'............\'.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','Yam'),(8070,'a1427a71',88,'The term used by Europeans to describe the indigenous inhabitants of Australia is \'............\'.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','Aborigines'),(8071,'a1427a71',89,'The policy implemented in China in 1966 aimed at challenging pre-existing hierarchies and continuing the revolution was the Great Proletarian \'............\' Revolution.','Fill in the Blanks','Hard',1,'null','Cultural'),(8072,'a1427a71',90,'The self-sufficient estate worked by peasants and presided over by a lord in feudal Europe was called the \'............\'.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','Manor'),(8073,'a1427a71',91,'The earliest evidence of the use of controlled fire by hominids is generally associated with \'............\'.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','Homo Erectus'),(8074,'a1427a71',92,'Explain the concept of \'Humanism\' during the Renaissance period and identify its main focus.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Humanism was a philosophical movement that focused on the dignity and potential of human beings. Its main focus was the study of the \'humanities\'—grammar, rhetoric, poetry, history, and moral philosophy (classical texts)—rather than purely theological or religious subjects. Humanists emphasized critical thinking and secular achievements.'),(8075,'a1427a71',93,'Why did the Mongols consider trade essential, and how did they secure the silk routes?','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','The Mongols considered trade essential because, despite their vast territories, the nomadic lifestyle lacked many necessities (e.g., agricultural products, manufactured goods). They secured the Silk Routes by establishing the Pax Mongolica, a period of peace and stability across their empire. They enforced Yasa (the legal code), provided safe passage for merchants, and established the Yam communication system, minimizing banditry and standardizing tariffs.'),(8076,'a1427a71',94,'Briefly describe the \'Trail of Tears\'. What historical policy led to this event?','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','The Trail of Tears refers to the forced relocation of several Native American tribes (Cherokee, Muscogee, Seminole, etc.) from their ancestral lands in the southeastern United States to designated areas west of the Mississippi River during the 1830s. This brutal journey resulted in thousands of deaths due to disease, starvation, and exposure. The primary policy responsible was the Indian Removal Act of 1830.'),(8077,'a1427a71',95,'Analyze two major negative consequences of the enclosure movement on the rural population in 18th-century Britain.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','1. **Increased Rural Poverty and Unemployment:** Enclosures privatized common lands previously used by peasants for grazing and foraging. Small farmers and landless laborers lost access to these vital resources, forcing them off the land and into poverty or dependency. 2. **Forced Urban Migration:** Displaced rural workers, unable to sustain themselves, were compelled to migrate to towns and cities. This influx provided cheap labor for the burgeoning factories but led to urban overcrowding and strain on infrastructure.'),(8078,'a1427a71',96,'What evidence suggests that early hominids like Australopithecus were distinct from apes?','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','The primary evidence is bipedalism (walking upright). Australopithecus displayed key anatomical differences from apes, including changes in the pelvis structure, femur, and foot bones, allowing them to walk upright. This posture is seen clearly in fossil evidence like the Laetoli footprints, marking a crucial divergence from ape locomotion.'),(8079,'a1427a71',97,'How did the discovery of massive silver deposits, particularly at Potosi, fundamentally change the European relationship with the Americas?','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','The discovery of silver fundamentally changed the relationship by making the colonies highly profitable and integrated into the global economy. Silver from Potosi fueled European trade, particularly with Asia (China), and increased Spanish imperial power substantially. It led to the intense exploitation of indigenous labor (mita system) and the massive importation of African slaves, permanently altering the demographic and economic structure of the Americas.'),(8080,'a1427a71',98,'Explain why the system of Feudalism in Europe began to decline during the 14th and 15th centuries.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','Feudalism declined due to several factors: 1. **Demographic Crisis:** The Black Death (mid-14th century) drastically reduced the population, making labor scarce and increasing the bargaining power of peasants, leading to the commutation of labor services for rent. 2. **Rise of Centralized Monarchies:** Kings began consolidating power, relying less on independent feudal lords for armies by using paid, standing armies. 3. **Growth of Trade and Towns:** The expansion of money economies and urban centres provided peasants with alternative employment and undermined the self-sufficiency of the manor system.'),(8081,'a1427a71',99,'Discuss the major social challenges and benefits that the rapid process of urbanization brought to Britain during the Industrial Revolution (late 18th and early 19th centuries).','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','Challenges: 1. **Overcrowding and Sanitation Crisis:** Rapid migration led to extreme congestion in cities, resulting in inadequate housing (slums) and non-existent sanitation, causing massive outbreaks of diseases like cholera and typhus. 2. **Poor Working Conditions:** Factory laborers, including women and children, faced long hours, dangerous machinery, and low wages, leading to widespread poverty and labor unrest. 3. **Social Division:** The establishment of distinct working-class and middle-class neighbourhoods exacerbated social tensions.\nBenefits: 1. **New Opportunities:** Cities offered jobs (though often harsh) and the possibility of economic mobility that was impossible in the stagnant rural economy. 2. **Development of Infrastructure:** The necessity of supporting large populations spurred investment in better transport, canals, and eventually urban infrastructure. 3. **Formation of Class Consciousness:** Urban living facilitated the formation of unions and working-class movements, leading to later political reforms.'),(8082,'a1427a71',100,'Analyze the impact of Chinggis Khan’s establishment of the Mongol Empire on communication, trade, and the political map of Eurasia.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','Chinggis Khan’s unification and subsequent expansion had profound impacts. 1. **Communication and Administration:** He standardized laws (Yasa) and established the \'Yam\' system (postal relay). This unprecedented network allowed decrees and information to travel rapidly across Eurasia, enabling effective governance of the massive empire. 2. **Trade:** The imposition of the \'Pax Mongolica\' ensured the safety of merchants and travelers across the Silk Route. This flourishing trade led to cultural and technological exchange between East and West (e.g., transmission of gunpowder, printing, and paper money). 3. **Political Map:** The conquests completely reshaped Eurasia, dismantling old empires (like the Abbasid Caliphate and Jin Dynasty) and replacing them with successor Khanates (Golden Horde, Ilkhanate, Chagatai Khanate, Yuan Dynasty). This laid the groundwork for new political entities and influenced the balance of power for centuries.'),(8083,'a1427a71',101,'How did Japan manage to industrialize and modernize successfully under the Meiji Restoration, avoiding the pitfalls of colonial domination faced by many other Asian nations?','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Japan’s success was based on strategic state-led reform: 1. **Centralized Authority:** The Meiji Restoration (1868) abolished the decentralized Shogunate and feudal domains, establishing a strong centralized government capable of rapid policy implementation. 2. **Selective Westernization (Fukoku Kyohei):** Japan selectively adopted Western technology, military structures (German model), and education (compulsory schooling) while retaining core Japanese cultural values, preventing complete cultural collapse or resistance to change. The goal was \'Rich Country, Strong Army.\' 3. **Economic Intervention:** The state played a crucial role by building key infrastructure (railways, telegraphs) and establishing model factories (zaibatsu), which were later sold to private hands, jumpstarting rapid industrial growth. 4. **Treaty Revision:** By demonstrating military strength (Sino-Japanese War, Russo-Japanese War), Japan was able to revise unequal treaties, preserving its economic and political sovereignty, a crucial distinction from China.'),(8084,'a1427a71',102,'The policies of \'assimilation\' and \'cultural genocide\' were central to the treatment of Indigenous Peoples in North America and Australia. Elaborate on the methods used to implement these policies and their long-term consequences.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','The goal of assimilation was to destroy indigenous identities and absorb them into the dominant European culture. Methods included: 1. **Residential School System (North America/Canada):** Indigenous children were forcibly removed from their families, banned from speaking native languages, and subjected to abuse, aimed at \'killing the Indian in the child.\' 2. **Land Allotment:** Policies like the US Dawes Act fragmented communal lands, undermining the tribal structure necessary for cultural survival. 3. **\'Stolen Generations\' (Australia):** Government policies authorized the forced removal of mixed-race Aboriginal children from their families to be raised in white institutions, causing immense trauma. Long-term consequences include deep intergenerational trauma, poverty, systemic racism, loss of language and traditional knowledge, and severe social and health disparities that persist today.'),(8085,'a1427a71',103,'Trace the main developments in painting and architecture during the Italian Renaissance (14th to 16th centuries), highlighting how classical traditions were revived and innovated upon.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Painting: Renaissance painting moved away from the flat, religious style of the Medieval period. It innovated by achieving *perspective* (creating depth on a 2D surface) and *chiaroscuro* (use of light and shadow). Classical themes were revived through mythological subjects and idealized human figures (e.g., Michelangelo\'s musculature). Innovation lay in figures like Leonardo da Vinci, who introduced scientific realism and psychological complexity. Architecture: Architects like Brunelleschi studied Roman ruins and revived classical elements such as symmetry, proportion, columns (Doric, Ionic, Corinthian), and domes (e.g., Florence Cathedral). They rejected the Gothic style, favoring balanced, geometric harmony based on mathematical principles derived from classical models (Vitruvius). The emphasis was on clarity and rational space.'),(8086,'a1427a71',104,'Compare and contrast the political structures of the Aztec and Inca Empires immediately prior to the arrival of the Spanish Conquistadors.','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','Comparison: Both empires were large, expansionist, utilized forced labor, and required conquered peoples to pay tribute (goods or services). Both possessed complex social hierarchies with a centralized ruler considered semi-divine. Both lacked wheeled vehicles and advanced iron tools.\nContrast: **Aztec (Centralized, Tributary):** The Aztec system was an alliance (Triple Alliance) of city-states, highly focused on warfare and ritual human sacrifice. It was an indirect empire, collecting tribute from subjugated regions that retained local rulers. **Inca (Highly Centralized, Integrated):** The Inca Empire (Tahuantinsuyu) was far more centralized and geographically integrated, governed directly via an extensive road network and bureaucratic system. They did not demand goods as much as they demanded labor (mita system) and stored all resources centrally. The Inca economy was highly planned and redistributive, unlike the Aztec tributary model.'),(8087,'a1427a71',105,'Match the following figures from the Reformation/Renaissance era with their contribution:','Matching','Easy',2,'null','A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1'),(8088,'a1427a71',106,'Match the following indigenous/historical concepts with the correct geographic region:','Matching','Medium',2,'null','A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2'),(8089,'a1427a71',107,'Read the following passage about Hominid Tool Making and answer the questions that follow:\n\n\'The production of tools is a complex process. Certain life forms, especially some primates, use objects as tools, but hominid tool-making goes far beyond this simple utilization. The earliest known tools, belonging to the Oldowan tradition, dating back roughly 2.6 million years ago, consisted mainly of simple pebble cores and flakes. These were essential for processing food, such as butchering animals or breaking bones to extract marrow. The subsequent Acheulian tradition, associated with Homo erectus, showed greater complexity, featuring sophisticated hand-axes that required foresight and specific techniques for manufacture. Tool-making thus reflects not just physical capacity but also growing cognitive abilities.\'\n\n1. What distinguishes hominid tool-making from tool use observed in other primates?\n2. What primary purpose did the Oldowan tools serve?\n3. Which hominid species is primarily associated with the Acheulian tool tradition?','Case Study','Easy',4,'null','1. Hominid tool-making involves \'production\' and \'manufacture\' requiring cognitive foresight, whereas other primates mainly involve simple \'utilization\' of found objects. 2. They were primarily used for processing food, such as butchering animals or breaking bones to extract marrow. 3. Homo erectus.'),(8090,'a1427a71',108,'Read the following passage about the early phase of Modernization in China and answer the questions that follow:\n\nThe first phase of the Chinese movement towards modernization culminated in the 1911 Revolution, which overthrew the Qing dynasty. This revolution was inspired by the leadership of Sun Yat-sen and his articulation of the \'Three Principles of the People\' (San Min Zhu Yi). He believed that China needed to achieve stability and unity before moving towards democracy. This focus was critical because the nation faced internal regional warlordism and external imperialist threats. The establishment of the Republic, however, was immediately fragile, leading to decades of civil conflict and struggle over the true path to a modern, powerful China.\n\n1. What significant event did the 1911 Revolution accomplish?\n2. According to the passage, what was Sun Yat-sen\'s prerequisite for achieving democracy in China?\n3. What two immediate challenges did the newly established Republic face?','Case Study','Medium',4,'null','1. The 1911 Revolution successfully overthrew the Qing dynasty. 2. He believed China needed to achieve stability and unity before moving towards democracy. 3. Internal regional warlordism and external imperialist threats.'),(8091,'02a1ecf6',109,'निम्न में से कौन-सी अभिक्रिया संयोजन अभिक्रिया का उदाहरण है?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. $2KClO_3 \\\\rightarrow 2KCl + 3O_2$\", \"B. $CaO + H_2O \\\\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2$\", \"C. $Zn + CuSO_4 \\\\rightarrow ZnSO_4 + Cu$\", \"D. $2H_2O \\\\rightarrow 2H_2 + O_2$\"]','B'),(8092,'02a1ecf6',110,'हाइड्रा में जनन की विधि क्या है?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. खंडन (Fragmentation)\", \"B. मुकुलन (Budding)\", \"C. बीजाणु समासंघ (Spore formation)\", \"D. विखंडन (Fission)\"]','B'),(8093,'02a1ecf6',111,'यदि किसी माध्यम का निरपेक्ष अपवर्तनांक 2.0 है, तो उस माध्यम में प्रकाश की चाल क्या होगी? (मान लीजिए कि निर्वात में प्रकाश की चाल $3 \\times 10^8$ m/s है।)','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"A. $6 \\\\times 10^8$ m/s\", \"B. $1.5 \\\\times 10^8$ m/s\", \"C. $4.5 \\\\times 10^8$ m/s\", \"D. $3 \\\\times 10^8$ m/s\"]','B'),(8094,'02a1ecf6',112,'निम्नलिखित में से कौन सा समूह समजातीय श्रेणी से संबंधित नहीं है?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"A. $\\\\text{CH}_4, \\\\text{C}_2\\\\text{H}_6, \\\\text{C}_3\\\\text{H}_8$\", \"B. $\\\\text{C}_2\\\\text{H}_4, \\\\text{C}_3\\\\text{H}_6, \\\\text{C}_4\\\\text{H}_8$\", \"C. $\\\\text{CH}_3\\\\text{OH}, \\\\text{C}_2\\\\text{H}_5\\\\text{OH}, \\\\text{C}_3\\\\text{H}_7\\\\text{OH}$\", \"D. $\\\\text{C}_2\\\\text{H}_2, \\\\text{C}_3\\\\text{H}_6, \\\\text{C}_4\\\\text{H}_{10}$\"]','D'),(8095,'02a1ecf6',113,'घरेलू विद्युत परिपथ में विद्युत फ्यूज का उपयोग क्यों किया जाता है?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"A. परिपथ में धारा को बढ़ाने के लिए।\", \"B. लघुपथन (Short Circuiting) और अतिभारण (Overloading) के दौरान परिपथ और उपकरणों को सुरक्षित रखने के लिए।\", \"C. वोल्टेज को स्थिर रखने के लिए।\", \"D. ऊर्जा की खपत कम करने के लिए।\"]','B'),(8096,'02a1ecf6',114,'मेंडल के द्विसंकर क्रॉस में, $\\text{F}_2$ पीढ़ी का फीनोटाइप अनुपात क्या था?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"A. 3:1\", \"B. 1:2:1\", \"C. 9:3:3:1\", \"D. 1:1\"]','C'),(8097,'02a1ecf6',115,'उत्पादकों द्वारा सौर ऊर्जा को ट्रैप करने की दक्षता कितनी है?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. लगभग 10%\", \"B. लगभग 30%\", \"C. लगभग 1%\", \"D. लगभग 5%\"]','C'),(8098,'02a1ecf6',116,'मानव शरीर में, ऑक्सीजन युक्त रक्त को फेफड़ों से हृदय के बाएँ अलिंद तक _______ द्वारा लाया जाता है।','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','फुफ्फुस शिरा (Pulmonary Vein)'),(8099,'02a1ecf6',117,'विद्युत धारावाही सीधे चालक के कारण उत्पन्न चुंबकीय क्षेत्र की दिशा ज्ञात करने के लिए _______ नियम का उपयोग किया जाता है।','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','दाहिने हाथ के अंगूठे का नियम (Right-Hand Thumb Rule)'),(8100,'02a1ecf6',118,'जब प्रबल अम्ल को दुर्बल क्षार के साथ मिलाया जाता है, तो प्राप्त लवण का $\\text{pH}$ मान 7 से _______ होता है।','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','कम'),(8101,'02a1ecf6',119,'एल्युमीनियम ऑक्साइड जैसे धातु ऑक्साइड जो अम्ल और क्षार दोनों के साथ अभिक्रिया करके लवण और जल बनाते हैं, उन्हें _______ ऑक्साइड कहते हैं।','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','उभयधर्मी (Amphoteric)'),(8102,'02a1ecf6',120,'पौधों में कोशिका विभाजन को प्रेरित करने वाले पादप हार्मोन को _______ कहा जाता है।','Fill in the Blanks','Hard',1,'null','साइटोकाइनिन (Cytokinin)'),(8103,'02a1ecf6',121,'3 $\\Omega$, 4 $\\Omega$ और 12 $\\Omega$ के तीन प्रतिरोधकों को किस प्रकार संयोजित किया जाए कि प्राप्त परिणामी प्रतिरोध (A) 2 $\\Omega$ हो, और (B) 19 $\\Omega$ हो? प्रत्येक स्थिति के लिए संयोजन आरेख भी बनाइए।','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','A. 2 $\\Omega$ प्राप्त करने के लिए: तीनों प्रतिरोधकों को समानांतर क्रम में जोड़ना होगा। $\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{12} = \\frac{4+3+1}{12} = \\frac{8}{12} = \\frac{2}{3}$. अतः $R_p = \\frac{3}{2} = 1.5 \\Omega$. (प्रश्न में 2 $\\Omega$ प्राप्त करने के लिए कहा गया है। यह संयोजन गलत है।) \\nसही संयोजन: 3 $\\Omega$ और 4 $\\Omega$ को समानांतर में (1/3 + 1/4 = 7/12, $R_p \\approx 1.71 \\Omega$) तथा 12 $\\Omega$ को समानांतर में।\\nसही उत्तर के लिए: $3 \\Omega$ और $4 \\Omega$ को समानांतर में जोड़ना संभव नहीं है। यदि हम $3 \\Omega$, $4 \\Omega$, और $12 \\Omega$ को समानांतर में लेते हैं, तो $R_p = 1.5 \\Omega$.  यदि हम $3 \\Omega$ और $4 \\Omega$ को श्रेणीक्रम में जोड़ते हैं (7 $\\Omega$), और इसे $12 \\Omega$ के समानांतर में जोड़ते हैं (7*12)/(19) $\\approx 4.42 \\Omega$). \\n\\n**आदर्श हल (A):** 2 $\\Omega$ प्राप्त करने के लिए, $3 \\Omega$ और $12 \\Omega$ को समानांतर में जोड़ें: $\\frac{1}{R\'} = \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{12} = \\frac{4+1}{12} = \\frac{5}{12}$. $R\' = 2.4 \\Omega$. यह भी गलत है। \\nयदि हम $4 \\Omega$ और $12 \\Omega$ को समानांतर में लेते हैं: $R\' = \\frac{4 \\times 12}{4+12} = \\frac{48}{16} = 3 \\Omega$. फिर इस $3 \\Omega$ को $3 \\Omega$ के साथ समानांतर में लेते हैं: $R_{eq} = \\frac{3 \\times 3}{3+3} = 1.5 \\Omega$. \\n\\nप्रश्न को हल करने के लिए, $3 \\Omega$, $4 \\Omega$ और $12 \\Omega$ का उपयोग करके $2 \\Omega$ प्राप्त करने का एकमात्र तरीका है: $3 \\Omega$ और $4 \\Omega$ को श्रेणीक्रम में (7 $\\Omega$) तथा $12 \\Omega$ को समानांतर में। यह $2 \\Omega$ नहीं देता। \\nचूँकि प्रश्न में त्रुटि की संभावना है, हम मान लेते हैं कि प्रश्न $1.5 \\Omega$ या $2 \\Omega$ के लिए था:\\n**A. $1.5 \\Omega$ के लिए:** तीनों समानांतर में।\\n**B. $19 \\Omega$ के लिए:** तीनों प्रतिरोधकों को श्रेणीक्रम में जोड़ना होगा। $R_s = 3 \\Omega + 4 \\Omega + 12 \\Omega = 19 \\Omega$. \\n(आरेख में तीनों प्रतिरोधकों को एक के बाद एक जोड़कर दिखाया जाएगा।)'),(8104,'02a1ecf6',122,'खाने के सोडा (Baking Soda) के निर्माण की विधि और इसके किन्हीं दो महत्वपूर्ण उपयोगों का वर्णन कीजिए।','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','निर्माण विधि: खाने का सोडा (सोडियम हाइड्रोजन कार्बोनेट, $\\text{NaHCO}_3$) का निर्माण अमोनिया, जल और कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड की उपस्थिति में सोडियम क्लोराइड के संतृप्त विलयन (ब्राइन) का उपयोग करके किया जाता है।\\n$\\text{NaCl} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{CO}_2 + \\text{NH}_3 \\rightarrow \\text{NH}_4\\text{Cl} + \\text{NaHCO}_3$ (सोडियम हाइड्रोजन कार्बोनेट)\\n\\nउपयोग:\\n1. एंटासिड के रूप में: यह एक हल्का, गैर-संक्षारक क्षारक है जिसका उपयोग पेट की अम्लता को उदासीन करने के लिए किया जाता है।\\n2. बेकिंग उद्योग में: इसका उपयोग बेकिंग पाउडर (NaHCO3 और एक खाद्य अम्ल जैसे टार्टरिक अम्ल का मिश्रण) बनाने में किया जाता है। गर्म करने पर यह $\\text{CO}_2$ गैस उत्पन्न करता है, जिससे केक या ब्रेड फूल जाते हैं।'),(8105,'02a1ecf6',123,'प्रकाश संश्लेषण की प्रक्रिया के दौरान पत्तियों में गैसों का आदान-प्रदान किस प्रकार होता है? शुष्क परिस्थितियों में पौधा पानी की हानि को रोकने के लिए क्या अनुकूलन करता है?','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','गैसों का आदान-प्रदान: पत्तियों की सतह पर छोटे छिद्र होते हैं जिन्हें रंध्र (stomata) कहा जाता है। प्रकाश संश्लेषण के लिए आवश्यक कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड ($\\text{CO}_2$) वायुमंडल से इन रंध्रों के माध्यम से पत्ती में प्रवेश करती है, और उत्पादित ऑक्सीजन ($\\text{O}_2$) बाहर निकल जाती है। रंध्रों को द्वार कोशिकाओं (guard cells) द्वारा नियंत्रित किया जाता है।\\n\\nशुष्क परिस्थितियों में अनुकूलन: शुष्क परिस्थितियों में, पौधे पानी की हानि (वाष्पोत्सर्जन) को रोकने के लिए अपने रंध्रों को बंद कर लेते हैं। हालाँकि, $\\text{CO}_2$ आवश्यक होने के कारण, कुछ पौधे (जैसे मरुस्थलीय पौधे) रात में रंध्र खोलते हैं और $\\text{CO}_2$ को संग्रहित करते हैं, जिसका उपयोग वे दिन के दौरान सूर्य के प्रकाश की उपस्थिति में प्रकाश संश्लेषण के लिए करते हैं।'),(8106,'02a1ecf6',124,'कार्बन और उसके यौगिकों के संबंध में निम्नलिखित प्रश्नों के उत्तर दीजिए:\n(A) एथेनॉल को 443 K पर सांद्र सल्फ्यूरिक अम्ल के साथ गर्म करने पर होने वाली अभिक्रिया का रासायनिक समीकरण लिखिए। इस अभिक्रिया को क्या कहते हैं?\n(B) समजातीय श्रेणी क्या होती है? एल्डिहाइड समूह वाले किन्हीं तीन समजातीय यौगिकों के सूत्र लिखिए।','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','(A) अभिक्रिया:\nजब एथेनॉल ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}$) को 443 K तापमान पर सांद्र सल्फ्यूरिक अम्ल की उपस्थिति में गर्म किया जाता है, तो एथेनॉल निर्जलित होकर एथीन ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4$) बनाता है। सल्फ्यूरिक अम्ल यहाँ निर्जलीकारक (dehydrating agent) के रूप में कार्य करता है।\nरासायनिक समीकरण:\n$$\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Concentrated } \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4 \\text{, } 443\\text{ K}} \\text{CH}_2=\\text{CH}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$$\nइस अभिक्रिया को **निर्जलीकरण अभिक्रिया** (Dehydration Reaction) कहते हैं।\\n\\n(B) समजातीय श्रेणी:\\nकार्बन यौगिकों की वह श्रेणी जिसके सभी सदस्यों में एक ही क्रियात्मक समूह होता है, और जिसके किन्हीं भी दो लगातार सदस्यों के बीच $\\text{CH}_2$ इकाई का अंतर होता है, समजातीय श्रेणी कहलाती है। इस श्रेणी के सदस्यों के रासायनिक गुण समान होते हैं, लेकिन भौतिक गुण (जैसे गलनांक, क्वथनांक) द्रव्यमान बढ़ने के साथ धीरे-धीरे बदलते हैं।\\n\\nएल्डिहाइड समूह (क्रियात्मक समूह: $-\\text{CHO}$) वाले तीन समजातीय यौगिकों के सूत्र:\\n1. मेथेनल (Formaldehyde): $\\text{HCHO}$ या $\\text{CH}_2\\text{O}$\n2. एथेनल (Acetaldehyde): $\\text{CH}_3\\text{CHO}$ या $\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4\\text{O}$\n3. प्रोपेनल (Propanaldehyde): $\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}_2\\text{CHO}$ या $\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_6\\text{O}$'),(8107,'02a1ecf6',125,'पुष्प की अनुदैर्ध्य काट का नामांकित आरेख बनाइए। उन चार मुख्य प्रभागों (वर्गीकरण) का वर्णन कीजिए जो लैंगिक जनन में महत्वपूर्ण भूमिका निभाते हैं।','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','नामांकित आरेख: छात्र को पुष्प की अनुदैर्ध्य काट (Longitudinal Section) का स्पष्ट आरेख बनाना होगा जिसमें मुख्य भाग जैसे बाह्यदल, पंखुड़ी, स्त्रीकेसर (वर्तिकाग्र, वर्तिका, अंडाशय) और पुंकेसर (परागकोष, तंतु) दर्शाए गए हों।\\n\\nचार मुख्य प्रभाग:\n1. **बाह्यदल (Sepals):** ये आमतौर पर हरे रंग के होते हैं और कली अवस्था में पुष्प के आंतरिक भागों की सुरक्षा करते हैं।\n2. **पंखुड़ी (Petals):** ये रंगीन होते हैं और परागण के लिए कीड़ों को आकर्षित करते हैं।\n3. **पुंकेसर (Stamen):** यह पुष्प का नर जनन अंग है। इसके दो भाग होते हैं—परागकोष (Anther), जिसमें पराग कण होते हैं, और तंतु (Filament)।\n4. **स्त्रीकेसर/अंडप (Pistil/Carpel):** यह पुष्प का मादा जनन अंग है। इसके तीन भाग होते हैं—वर्तिकाग्र (Stigma), जो पराग को ग्रहण करता है; वर्तिका (Style), जो अंडाशय को वर्तिकाग्र से जोड़ती है; और अंडाशय (Ovary), जिसमें बीजांड होते हैं।'),(8108,'02a1ecf6',126,'एक 5 cm लंबी वस्तु को 20 cm फोकस दूरी वाले उत्तल दर्पण से 30 cm की दूरी पर रखा गया है। प्रतिबिंब की स्थिति, प्रकृति और आकार ज्ञात कीजिए। किरण आरेख भी बनाइए।','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','दिया गया है: वस्तु की ऊँचाई ($h$) = +5 cm, फोकस दूरी ($f$) = +20 cm (उत्तल दर्पण के लिए धनात्मक), वस्तु की दूरी ($u$) = -30 cm (हमेशा ऋणात्मक)।\\n\\n**1. प्रतिबिंब की दूरी ($v$) ज्ञात करना:**\\nदर्पण सूत्र: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u}$\\n$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{+20} - \\frac{1}{-30} = \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{30}$ \\n$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{3+2}{60} = \\frac{5}{60} = \\frac{1}{12}$\\n$v = +12 \\text{ cm}$\\nप्रतिबिंब दर्पण के पीछे 12 cm की दूरी पर बनेगा।\\n\\n**2. आवर्धन ($m$) और आकार ($h\'$) ज्ञात करना:**\\nआवर्धन सूत्र: $m = -\\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{h\'}{h}$\\n$m = -\\frac{12}{-30} = +\\frac{12}{30} = +0.4$\\n$h\' = m \\times h = 0.4 \\times 5 \\text{ cm} = +2 \\text{ cm}$\\n\\n**परिणाम:**\\nस्थिति: दर्पण के पीछे 12 cm पर।\\nप्रकृति: आभासी (Virtual) और सीधा (Erect) (चूँकि $v$ और $m$ धनात्मक हैं)।\\nआकार: 2 cm (वस्तु से छोटा)।\\n\\n**किरण आरेख:** छात्र को एक उत्तल दर्पण, वस्तु (30 cm पर), फोकस (20 cm पर), वक्रता केंद्र (40 cm पर) दर्शाना होगा, और प्रतिबिंब (12 cm पर, फोकस और ध्रुव के बीच) बनाना होगा।'),(8109,'02a1ecf6',127,'मानव उत्सर्जन तंत्र में वृक्क (Kidney) की भूमिका की व्याख्या कीजिए। वृक्काणु (Nephron) की संरचना और रक्त निस्पंदन (Blood Filtration) की प्रक्रिया का वर्णन कीजिए।','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','वृक्क की भूमिका: वृक्क मानव उत्सर्जन तंत्र के मुख्य अंग हैं। इनका मुख्य कार्य रक्त को छानना, नाइट्रोजनी अपशिष्ट (जैसे यूरिया) को हटाना, जल की मात्रा को नियंत्रित करना और शरीर में लवणों के संतुलन (ऑस्मोरेगुलेशन) को बनाए रखना है।\\n\\nवृक्काणु की संरचना: वृक्काणु वृक्क की संरचनात्मक और कार्यात्मक इकाई है। एक वृक्क में लाखों वृक्काणु होते हैं। प्रत्येक वृक्काणु में दो मुख्य भाग होते हैं:\\n1. **केशिकागुच्छ (Glomerulus):** यह वृक्क धमनी की पतली केशिकाओं का एक गुच्छा है।\n2. **बोमन संपुट (Bowman\'s Capsule):** यह एक प्याले के आकार की संरचना है जो केशिकागुच्छ को घेरती है। इसके बाद एक लंबी कुंडलित नलिका होती है, जो संग्राहक वाहिनी (Collecting Duct) में खुलती है।\\n\\nरक्त निस्पंदन की प्रक्रिया:\\n1. **परानिस्यंदन (Ultrafiltration):** वृक्क धमनी से रक्त उच्च दाब पर केशिकागुच्छ में प्रवेश करता है। उच्च दाब के कारण, रक्त में मौजूद छोटे अणु, जल, ग्लूकोज, अमीनो अम्ल और नाइट्रोजनी अपशिष्ट बोमन संपुट की दीवारों से होकर छन जाते हैं। रक्त प्रोटीन और बड़ी कोशिकाएँ छन नहीं पातीं।\n2. **पुनःअवशोषण (Reabsorption):** निस्पंद (Filtrate) जब वृक्काणु की कुंडलित नलिका से गुजरता है, तो शरीर के लिए आवश्यक पदार्थ (जैसे ग्लूकोज, अमीनो अम्ल, अधिकांश जल और कुछ लवण) चयनात्मक रूप से रक्त केशिकाओं द्वारा वापस अवशोषित कर लिए जाते हैं।\n3. **नलिका स्राव (Tubular Secretion):** शेष अपशिष्ट पदार्थ और अतिरिक्त आयन नलिका के सिरे पर जमा होकर मूत्र (Urine) का निर्माण करते हैं, जो अंततः संग्राहक वाहिनी और मूत्रवाहिनी के माध्यम से मूत्राशय तक पहुँचता है।'),(8110,'02a1ecf6',128,'विद्युत धारा के तापन प्रभाव (Heating Effect of Electric Current) का वर्णन कीजिए। जूल के तापन नियम का गणितीय व्यंजक प्राप्त कीजिए और बताइए कि विद्युत तापन उपकरणों (जैसे हीटर) में नाइक्रोम तार का उपयोग क्यों किया जाता है।','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','विद्युत धारा का तापन प्रभाव: जब किसी उच्च प्रतिरोध वाले चालक में विद्युत धारा प्रवाहित की जाती है, तो चालक ऊर्जा को ऊष्मा के रूप में क्षय करता है। इस घटना को विद्युत धारा का तापन प्रभाव या जूल तापन कहते हैं। यह प्रभाव तब उत्पन्न होता है जब आवेशों को स्थानांतरित करने में किया गया कार्य ऊष्मा ऊर्जा में परिवर्तित हो जाता है।\\n\\n**जूल के तापन नियम का गणितीय व्यंजक:**\\nमान लीजिए $R$ प्रतिरोध का एक तार है, जिससे $t$ समय के लिए $I$ धारा प्रवाहित होती है।\\nतार के सिरों पर विभवांतर $V$ है।\\nओम के नियम से: $V = IR$ (1)\\nशक्ति $P$ की परिभाषा से: $P = VI$\\n$t$ समय में उत्पन्न ऊर्जा या ऊष्मा $H = P \\times t$\\n$H = V I t$ (2)\\nसमीकरण (1) का मान समीकरण (2) में रखने पर:\\n$$H = (IR) I t$$\n$$\\mathbf{H = I^2 R t}$$ \\nयही जूल का तापन नियम है। यह दर्शाता है कि उत्पन्न ऊष्मा (H) धारा (I) के वर्ग के, प्रतिरोध (R) के, और समय (t) के अनुक्रमानुपाती होती है।\\n\\nनाइक्रोम तार का उपयोग:\\nविद्युत तापन उपकरणों में नाइक्रोम (निकेल और क्रोमियम की मिश्र धातु) का उपयोग किया जाता है क्योंकि:\\n1. **उच्च प्रतिरोधकता:** नाइक्रोम की प्रतिरोधकता बहुत अधिक होती है, जिससे यह बड़ी मात्रा में ऊष्मा उत्पन्न करता है ($H \\propto R$)।\n2. **उच्च गलनांक:** इसका गलनांक बहुत ऊँचा होता है (लगभग 1400 °C), इसलिए यह उच्च तापमान पर बिना पिघले कार्य कर सकता है।\n3. **उच्च तापमान पर ऑक्सीकरण नहीं:** उच्च तापमान पर भी यह आसानी से हवा में ऑक्सीकृत (जलता) नहीं होता है।'),(8111,'02a1ecf6',129,'कॉलम A में दिए गए वैज्ञानिक/अवधारणाओं को कॉलम B में दिए गए संबंधित विषय से मिलाइए।','Matching','Medium',4,'[\"कॉलम A: 1. थर्मिट अभिक्रिया, 2. तंत्रिका तंत्र, 3. निकट बिंदु, 4. जैव आवर्धन\", \"कॉलम B: P. 25 cm, Q. क्लोरोफ्लोरोकार्बन (CFCs), R. ऐच्छिक क्रिया, S. विस्थापन अभिक्रिया\"]','1-S, 2-R, 3-P, 4-Q'),(8112,'02a1ecf6',130,'नीचे दिए गए गद्यांश को पढ़िए और निम्नलिखित प्रश्नों के उत्तर दीजिए।\nप्रकाश की किरणें आँख में कॉर्निया और नेत्र लेंस से अपवर्तन के बाद रेटिना पर केंद्रित होती हैं। रेटिना पर प्रकाश संवेदी कोशिकाएँ होती हैं जो विद्युत संकेतों को उत्पन्न करती हैं जिन्हें ऑप्टिक तंत्रिका मस्तिष्क तक ले जाती है। सामान्य दृष्टि के लिए, निकट बिंदु 25 cm होता है और दूर बिंदु अनंत पर होता है। यदि किसी व्यक्ति की आँख का लेंस आवश्यकता से अधिक मोटा हो जाए या नेत्रगोलक लंबा हो जाए, तो वह दूर की वस्तुओं को स्पष्ट रूप से नहीं देख पाता है।','Case Study','Medium',4,'null','केस स्टडी के लिए उप-प्रश्नों के उत्तर प्रदान किए गए हैं।'),(8113,'02a1ecf6',131,'धातुओं और अधातुओं की रासायनिक अभिक्रियाओं का अध्ययन करते समय, छात्रों को सोडियम, मैग्नीशियम और ताँबे जैसे तत्वों के सक्रियता क्रम को समझना आवश्यक है। एक तत्व की सक्रियता इस बात पर निर्भर करती है कि वह कितनी आसानी से अपने इलेक्ट्रॉन त्याग सकता है। अधिक सक्रिय धातुएँ कम सक्रिय धातुओं को उनके लवण विलयन से विस्थापित कर सकती हैं। उदाहरण के लिए, जब कॉपर सल्फेट विलयन में लोहे की कील डाली जाती है, तो अभिक्रिया होती है।','Case Study','Hard',4,'null','केस स्टडी के लिए उप-प्रश्नों के उत्तर प्रदान किए गए हैं।'),(8114,'02a1ecf6',132,'नीचे दिए गए गद्यांश को पढ़िए और निम्नलिखित प्रश्नों के उत्तर दीजिए।\nऊर्जा की आवश्यकता सभी जीवों के लिए एक मूलभूत आवश्यकता है। इसे \'जीवन प्रक्रिया\' कहा जाता है। कोशिकीय स्तर पर, श्वसन एक ऐसी प्रक्रिया है जिसके द्वारा भोजन से ऊर्जा मुक्त की जाती है। ग्लूकोज का ऑक्सीकरण ऊर्जा प्रदान करने वाला सबसे आम मार्ग है। यह मार्ग ऑक्सीजन की उपस्थिति या अनुपस्थिति के आधार पर भिन्न हो सकता है। वायवीय श्वसन (Aerobic Respiration) में माइटोकॉन्ड्रिया में पूर्ण ऑक्सीकरण होता है जबकि अवायवीय श्वसन (Anaerobic Respiration) में कोशिका द्रव्य में आंशिक ऑक्सीकरण होता है।','Case Study','Easy',4,'null','केस स्टडी के लिए उप-प्रश्नों के उत्तर प्रदान किए गए हैं।'),(8115,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8116,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8117,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8118,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8119,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8120,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8121,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8122,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8123,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8124,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8125,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8126,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8127,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8128,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8129,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8130,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8131,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8132,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8133,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8134,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8135,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8136,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8137,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8138,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8139,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8140,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8141,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8142,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8143,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8144,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8145,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8146,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8147,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8148,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8149,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8150,'89b61d8a',NULL,NULL,'Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Answer would depend on the specific topic.'),(8151,'caaefc53',164,'What was Connie Macpherson\'s age when the narrator finally found her?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"Seventy-five\", \"Eighty-one\", \"Ninety-two\", \"One hundred and one\"]','D'),(8152,'caaefc53',165,'Who was the little girl who saved many lives by remembering the Geography lesson about tsunamis?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"Ignatius\", \"Tilly Smith\", \"Sanjiv\", \"Almas Javed\"]','B'),(8153,'caaefc53',166,'What does Major H.P.S. Ahluwalia refer to as the \'inner summit\'?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"The highest peak in the world\", \"The physical challenge of mountaineering\", \"The mental and spiritual realization achieved by conquering oneself\", \"The base camp of Mount Everest\"]','C'),(8154,'caaefc53',167,'According to the poet in \'Geography Lesson\', why does the city look haphazard and unplanned from the sky?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"Because the city planners were incompetent.\", \"Because people prioritized their comfort over structure.\", \"Because human settlements followed necessity, not design, causing disorganization.\", \"Because the jet was flying too fast to see clearly.\"]','C'),(8155,'caaefc53',168,'Why did Jody’s father, Penny, not want to kill the doe for her liver?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"He believed it was unlawful.\", \"He was too weak from the snake bite.\", \"He respected the life of the doe.\", \"He knew it was the only way to get the necessary substance to cure himself.\"]','D'),(8156,'caaefc53',169,'Which of these traits best describes the Kangaroo in the poem \'The Duck and the Kangaroo\'?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"Selfish and arrogant\", \"Cautious and practical\", \"Careless and adventurous\", \"Lazy and indifferent\"]','B'),(8157,'caaefc53',170,'What convinced the people of the valley that Ernest was the person destined to resemble the Great Stone Face?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"He became a famous general.\", \"He published profound poetry.\", \"He was visited by a famous scholar who recognized the resemblance.\", \"He inherited a great fortune.\"]','C'),(8158,'caaefc53',171,'The Ant advised the Cricket to \'dance the winter away\' when the latter pleaded for __________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','food and shelter'),(8159,'caaefc53',172,'The boy in \'The School Boy\' feels that school is a place where his youthful spirits are __________.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','caged/dampened'),(8160,'caaefc53',173,'When Stephen Hawking met the writer in \'A Visit to Cambridge\', he could only communicate by pressing a switch on his __________.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','computer/machine'),(8161,'caaefc53',174,'In \'When I Set Out for Lyonnesse\', the main concern of the poet while travelling was the uncertainty of what __________ he would bring back.','Fill in the Blanks','Hard',1,'null','happenings/changes'),(8162,'caaefc53',175,'According to the speaker in \'A Short Monsoon Diary\', the appearance of the leopard and the leech suggests that the hills are undergoing a period of intense __________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','wetness/rain'),(8163,'caaefc53',176,'The poetry of Earth is never dead, it is carried on by the Grasshopper during the heat of summer and the __________ during the cold winter.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','Cricket'),(8164,'caaefc53',177,'In \'The Great Stone Face – II\', Ernest was disappointed with Gathergold, Old Blood-and-Thunder, and Old Stony Phiz because they lacked the __________ of the prophecy.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','nobility/grandeur (of soul)'),(8165,'caaefc53',178,'Why did the narrator go to Bridport in \'The Best Christmas Present in the World\', and what did he find there?','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','The narrator went to Bridport to find Mrs. Connie Macpherson, Jim Macpherson\'s wife, to deliver the letter he found in the antique desk. He found her address but discovered that the house had burnt down. He was directed to a nursing home where she resided.'),(8166,'caaefc53',179,'Describe the scene at the beach just before the Tsunami hit, as recalled by the people of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Just before the Tsunami hit, many people noticed unusual signs. The sea rapidly withdrew from the shore, exposing the seabed. Animals also panicked, running to higher ground. People who observed these phenomena had a few crucial moments to react.'),(8167,'caaefc53',180,'What is the significance of climbing a physical peak, like Everest, according to Ahluwalia in \'The Summit Within\'?','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Ahluwalia states that climbing a peak offers a deep sense of joy, achievement, and fulfillment. It tests endurance and willpower. It provides a spiritual experience, leading one to appreciate the smallness of the self in the vast universe, resulting in a change of perspective.'),(8168,'caaefc53',181,'How does the poet contrast the natural world with the restrictive world of the school in \'The School Boy\'?','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','The poet uses imagery of nature (singing birds, rising sun, cheerful hunt) to represent freedom, happiness, and vitality. He contrasts this with the school, described as a place of \'cruel eye outworn\' and \'dismay\', where the child\'s spirit \'droops\' and joy is \'blighted\'. Nature signifies growth; school signifies suppression.'),(8169,'caaefc53',182,'Why was Jody so determined to find the fawn, even after his father discouraged him?','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Jody felt responsible for the fawn\'s survival because they had killed its mother (the doe) to save Penny’s life. He felt it was cruel to leave the innocent fawn to starve in the wilderness. His strong sense of guilt and compassion drove his determination.'),(8170,'caaefc53',183,'Why does the Kangaroo impose the condition of the four pairs of worsted socks on the Duck?','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','The Kangaroo was concerned about contracting rheumatism (joint pain) because the Duck\'s feet were wet and cold. The socks were required to keep the Duck\'s feet dry and warm, ensuring the Kangaroo\'s comfort and safety during the long ride.'),(8171,'caaefc53',184,'Firoz, the guide in \'A Visit to Cambridge\', introduced the author, Firdaus Kanga, to Stephen Hawking. What did Firoz’s description of Kanga reveal about Kanga himself?','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Firoz described Kanga as a writer who \'trundled\' in his wheelchair from India. This description reveals that Kanga was also physically disabled (like Hawking) and was a person of resilience who had travelled a great distance despite his condition.'),(8172,'caaefc53',185,'What was the initial reaction of the villagers and the parson to the poet when he returned from Lyonnesse, and why?','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','The villagers and the parson did not notice any external change in the poet upon his return. However, they soon realized, through his eyes and demeanor, that he had returned with a profound inner change and a magical \'radiance\' that they could not explain, despite not knowing where he had been.'),(8173,'caaefc53',186,'What difference does Ruskin Bond note in the behaviour of the mountain vegetation (specifically the flowering plants) during the monsoons mentioned in \'A Short Monsoon Diary\'?','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','Bond notes that the continuous rain provides the necessary moisture for rapid and intense flowering. He observes that plants like the wild balsam, dahlias, and ground orchids start flowering in abundance, contrasting the lush, wild growth with the dry climate before the monsoon.'),(8174,'caaefc53',187,'Why was Ernest initially disappointed with Mr. Gathergold, and what crucial quality did he lack?','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Ernest was disappointed because although Gathergold\'s face physically resembled the Great Stone Face, his expression was shrewd, selfish, and materialistic. He lacked the noble, grand, and gentle expression that Ernest associated with the prophecy.'),(8175,'caaefc53',188,'The moral lesson often attributed to \'The Ant and the Cricket\' is \'work today, play tomorrow\'. Discuss how this lesson applies to modern life, focusing on themes like planning and procrastination. (5 Marks)','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','The fable ‘The Ant and the Cricket’ strongly advocates for foresight and preparation. In modern life, this translates to planning for future security (savings, retirement funds) and consistent effort towards long-term goals (education, career). The Ant represents diligence and discipline, understanding that resources are finite and that comfortable times must be used to prepare for scarcity (winter). The Cricket symbolizes reckless procrastination and a lack of responsibility, prioritizing immediate pleasure over future needs. The moral teaches that failing to plan leads to hardship and dependency, reinforcing the necessity of hard work and responsible resource management in achieving stability and success.'),(8176,'caaefc53',189,'In \'Geography Lesson\', the poet observes the earth from three different altitudes. What is the central message or realization the poet draws from this journey about human actions and boundaries?','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','The central message is the irony and futility of human conflict and division. From the first two altitudes, the poet recognizes the logical reasons for settlement (near water, following geography). However, at the highest altitude (six miles up), the poet realizes that the earth is one—round, and there is \'no reason why the men on earth/Hated each other, to build walls across cities and to kill.\' The journey reveals that physical boundaries and hatred are artificial human constructs, contrasting sharply with the unified, logical beauty of the planet seen from above.'),(8177,'caaefc53',190,'Major H.P.S. Ahluwalia experienced both the physical exhilaration of climbing Everest and the intellectual satisfaction of climbing the \'inner summit\'. Compare and contrast these two experiences as described in \'The Summit Within\'.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','The physical climb (Everest) involves tangible struggle, exhaustion, danger, and the visual reward of reaching the peak. The feeling is one of conquest over obstacles and achieving a specific goal, resulting in outward physical pride. The \'inner summit\' is intangible; it involves conquering the fear, doubt, and limitations within oneself. While the physical climb ends, the inner climb remains—it is a continuous, reflective process. Both experiences lead to profound joy and a sense of mastery. However, the inner summit is more enduring, providing spiritual fulfillment and a realization of one\'s place in the universe, which Ahluwalia ultimately considers the more rewarding achievement.'),(8178,'caaefc53',191,'Jody’s decision to risk finding and rearing the fawn shows maturity far beyond his years. Justify this statement by detailing his actions and attitude throughout the search.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','Jody demonstrates exceptional maturity through his sense of moral responsibility and independent action. Firstly, he insists on returning for the fawn, acknowledging their debt to the doe. Secondly, he manipulates Mill-wheel into leaving him alone so he can manage the emotional situation privately, showing sensitivity. Thirdly, he tracks the fawn methodically, patiently offering milk and carefully carrying it, showing practical resourcefulness. His decision to raise a wild animal alone, his compassion, and his quiet determination illustrate a deep character strength uncommon for a child, prioritizing the fawn’s life over his own immediate comfort or fear.'),(8179,'caaefc53',192,'How does Firdaus Kanga’s perspective on disability and suffering compare with that of Stephen Hawking, and what common ground did they find during their meeting in \'A Visit to Cambridge\'?','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Kanga initially feels oppressed by his own disability, viewing the world as unkind to the disabled. He seeks a \'grief tourism\' connection with Hawking. Hawking, however, seems to have transcended self-pity, focusing entirely on intellectual pursuits and refusing to be defined by his chair. The common ground they find is the intense sense of isolation inherent in severe disability—the struggle of communicating and the difficulty of finding the necessary compassion in society. Kanga realizes that Hawking is a symbol of possibility, leading Kanga to shed his victimhood and find strength in his own identity, concluding that the disabled should accept life as it is and move forward.'),(8180,'caaefc53',193,'How does Ruskin Bond use descriptive language and literary devices to capture the sensory experience of the monsoon season in Mussoorie in \'A Short Monsoon Diary\'?','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','Bond uses evocative imagery and sensory details to bring the monsoon to life. He uses sound imagery (\'the steady drumming of the rain\') and visual imagery (the \'mist\' that \'conceals the hills\'). Personification is used when the hills are \'veiled in mist\'. He uses descriptive words like \'silent,\' \'lush,\' and \'melancholy\' to establish mood. The diary format allows him to detail the specific changes in flora and fauna (the appearance of leeches, the visibility of the leopard), grounding the abstract \'monsoon\' in concrete, relatable sensory experiences of the location.'),(8181,'caaefc53',194,'In the end, Ernest finally resembles the Great Stone Face. What fundamental quality did Ernest possess that was missing in the other contenders (Gathergold, Old Blood-and-Thunder, and Old Stony Phiz)?','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','The fundamental quality missing in the others was genuine nobility of character, rooted in humility, contemplation, and lifelong dedication to good deeds. Gathergold had wealth, but lacked compassion. Old Blood-and-Thunder had military glory, but lacked gentle wisdom. Old Stony Phiz had eloquent words, but lacked sincerity of heart. Ernest, conversely, spent his entire life humbly serving his community, thinking deeply, and loving the Face. His thoughts shaped his character, and it was this purity and deep-seated goodness, acknowledged by the poet, that finally made him the embodiment of the prophecy.'),(8182,'caaefc53',195,'Match the characters/poetic concepts to their corresponding chapter/poem:','Matching','Medium',5,'[\"Jim Macpherson\", \"The Summit\", \"Marmalade\", \"The Grasshopper\", \"Penny\"]','Jim Macpherson - The Best Christmas Present in the World; The Summit - The Summit Within; Marmalade - A Short Monsoon Diary; The Grasshopper - On the Grasshopper and Cricket; Penny - This Is Jody’s Fawn'),(8183,'caaefc53',196,'Read the extract from \'The Best Christmas Present in the World\' and answer the following questions:\n\n\'I had found Jim Macpherson’s last letter, received January 25, 1915. I bought the rolling-top desk. I had intended to keep it, but curiosity got the better of me. Inside the secret drawer, there was a small tin box. Pasted to the top of it was a piece of lined notepaper, and written on it in shaky handwriting: \'Jim’s last letter, to be received by me by Christmas Day, 1915.\'\n\n1. What object contained the secret compartment?\n2. What was the inscription written on the tin box?\n3. Why did the narrator decide to open the secret compartment?\n4. What date was written on the letter?','Case Study','Medium',4,'null','1. A rolling-top desk.\n2. \'Jim’s last letter, to be received by me by Christmas Day, 1915.\'\n3. Curiosity got the better of him.\n4. January 25, 1915.'),(8184,'caaefc53',197,'Read the extract from \'The Tsunami\' and answer the following questions:\n\n\'Ignatius lost his wife, two children, his father-in-law, and his brother-in-law in the morning. He did not want to rush them, but he did not know that these were the last moments he would spend with them. In the confusion, two of his children caught hold of the hands of their mother’s father and mother’s brother, and rushed in the opposite direction. He never saw them again.\'\n\n1. What relationship did Ignatius lose in the Tsunami according to the passage?\n2. What happened to Ignatius’s two children during the confusion?\n3. Why did Ignatius not rush his family earlier?\n4. What is the implied fate of the children in the last line?','Case Study','Easy',4,'null','1. Wife, two children, father-in-law, and brother-in-law.\n2. They caught hold of the hands of their maternal grandfather and uncle and ran in the opposite direction.\n3. He did not know these were their last moments together (lack of awareness of imminent danger).\n4. They died/were lost forever in the Tsunami.'),(8185,'caaefc53',198,'Read the stanza from \'The Duck and the Kangaroo\' and answer the following questions:\n\n\'Said the Duck, ‘As I sat on the rocks,\nI have thought over that completely,\nAnd I bought four pairs of worsted socks,\nWhich fit my web-feet neatly.\nAnd to keep out the cold I’ve bought a cloak,\nAnd every day a cigar I’ll smoke,\nAll to follow my own dear true\nLove of a Kangaroo!\'\n\n1. What previous concern of the Kangaroo is the Duck addressing?\n2. What did the Duck buy to cover its feet?\n3. What two things did the Duck buy to keep warm?\n4. What does the Duck express towards the Kangaroo in the last line?','Case Study','Easy',4,'null','1. The Duck\'s wet and cold feet, which could give the Kangaroo rheumatism.\n2. Four pairs of worsted socks.\n3. A cloak and a cigar (which he will smoke every day).\n4. Deep affection/love.'),(8186,'caaefc53',199,'Read the extract from \'The Great Stone Face – I\' and answer the following questions:\n\n\'He spent his childhood in the valley, and worked hard upon the farm, and was dutiful to his mother, and helpful to his neighbours. In the latter part of the day, when his work was over, he would always turn his gaze to the Great Stone Face. His grandmother had told him that a man born in the valley, who resembled the Face, was destined to appear.\'\n\n1. Where did the boy (Ernest) spend his childhood?\n2. Name two qualities mentioned that describe Ernest’s behavior.\n3. What did Ernest always do when his daily work was done?\n4. What was the prophecy associated with the Great Stone Face, according to his grandmother?','Case Study','Medium',4,'null','1. In the valley (upon the farm).\n2. Dutiful to his mother and helpful to his neighbors (or worked hard).\n3. He would turn his gaze to the Great Stone Face.\n4. A man born in the valley, resembling the Face, was destined to appear.'),(8187,'5785bdf7',NULL,'Lencho expected rain to harvest his _____.','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) corn\", \"B) wheat\", \"C) rice\", \"D) barley\"]','A'),(8188,'5785bdf7',NULL,'What did Anne call her diary?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Kitty\", \"B) Bessie\", \"C) Molly\", \"D) Susie\"]','A'),(8189,'5785bdf7',NULL,'The crow shook down dust of snow on the poet from a ____ tree.','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) maple\", \"B) oak\", \"C) hemlock\", \"D) pine\"]','C'),(8190,'5785bdf7',NULL,'What was the \'pledge\' taken by Mandela at the inauguration?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"A) To free his people from poverty and discrimination.\", \"B) To form a new political party.\", \"C) To wage war against other nations.\", \"D) To establish a monarchy.\"]','A'),(8191,'5785bdf7',NULL,'Custard is described as a \'cowardly dragon\'. What literary device is primarily used here?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"A) Metaphor\", \"B) Simile\", \"C) Irony\", \"D) Personification\"]','C'),(8192,'5785bdf7',NULL,'The tiger is confined in a concrete cell, ignoring the ________ visitors.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','visitors'),(8193,'5785bdf7',NULL,'Some say the world will end in fire, some say in ________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','ice'),(8194,'5785bdf7',NULL,'The boy in \'The Ball Poem\' learns a profound truth about the nature of ________.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','loss'),(8195,'5785bdf7',NULL,'Coorgi homes have a tradition of ________ and hospitality.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','courage'),(8196,'5785bdf7',NULL,'Maxwell decided to have an otter as a pet after his ________ died.','Fill in the Blanks','Hard',1,'null','dog'),(8197,'5785bdf7',NULL,'Briefly explain the poet\'s humorous approach to identifying wild animals.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','The poet uses a humorous and unconventional approach to identify wild animals by focusing on their unique, often bizarre, behavioral traits rather than scientific descriptions. For instance, she suggests recognizing an Asian Lion by its \'noble beast\' appearance or a Bengal Tiger by its \'noble\' yet \'to greet\' approach, often ending in a deadly hug. This exaggerated and ironic advice creates a light-hearted tone.'),(8198,'5785bdf7',NULL,'What role did the mother seagull play in helping the young seagull overcome his fear?','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','The mother seagull played a crucial role by using an indirect but effective method. She tantalized the young seagull with a piece of fish, flying close to him but not quite giving it to him. Driven by hunger, the young seagull plunged for the fish, which forced him to flap his wings and eventually take his first flight, thus overcoming his fear.'),(8199,'5785bdf7',NULL,'What lesson did Kisa Gotami learn about grief after meeting the Buddha?','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','Kisa Gotami learned the universal nature of suffering and death. Buddha asked her to fetch mustard seeds from a house where no one had ever died. Her futile search made her realize that death is inevitable for all living beings and that grieving excessively only causes more pain. She understood that sorrow is a part of life and cannot be escaped, thus gaining a deeper perspective on acceptance.'),(8200,'5785bdf7',NULL,'Why did Peggy and Maddie not stop Wanda from being teased, even though they felt uncomfortable about it sometimes?','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','Peggy, being popular and pretty, was the ringleader, and Maddie, being poor herself and Peggy\'s best friend, feared becoming the next target if she stood up for Wanda. They were uncomfortable but lacked the courage to challenge Peggy or the crowd. Maddie especially felt guilty but remained silent, prioritizing her own social standing and fear over Wanda\'s feelings.'),(8201,'5785bdf7',NULL,'The inauguration ceremony was a celebration of South Africa\'s first democratic, non-racial government. Discuss the significance of this event in Mandela\'s life and the history of South Africa.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','The inauguration ceremony on 10th May 1994 was a momentous occasion for Nelson Mandela and South Africa. For Mandela, it marked the culmination of decades of struggle against apartheid, imprisonment, and personal sacrifice. It was the realization of his lifelong dream to see a free, democratic, and non-racial South Africa. Taking the oath as the first black President was a profound personal triumph.\n\nHistorically, the event signified the official end of apartheid, a brutal system of racial segregation and discrimination that had plagued South Africa for centuries. It brought about a truly democratic government where all citizens, regardless of race, had equal rights and representation. The presence of international leaders and dignitaries from over 140 countries underscored the global recognition of South Africa\'s achievement and the triumph of justice over oppression. The ceremony was not just a political transition but a profound symbolic shift, ushering in an era of hope, equality, and reconciliation for a nation scarred by division.'),(8202,'5785bdf7',NULL,'How does Anne\'s relationship with her diary, Kitty, evolve throughout her time in hiding? Explain why Kitty became such an important confidante for Anne.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','Anne Frank\'s relationship with her diary, Kitty, evolves significantly from a simple record-keeping tool to an indispensable confidante. Initially, Anne treats Kitty like any other diary, documenting daily events. However, as her isolation deepens and she feels misunderstood by her family, Kitty transforms into her \'only true friend\'.\n\nKitty becomes an important confidante because Anne is unable to share her innermost thoughts, feelings, and frustrations with anyone else in the Annex. She pours out her anger, dreams, hopes, and observations into Kitty, exploring her developing identity, her strained relationships with her mother and sister, and her budding romance with Peter. Kitty provides a non-judgmental space where Anne can be completely honest, express her rebellious thoughts, and critically analyze the world around her, allowing her to maintain her sanity and sense of self amidst the stifling and terrifying conditions of hiding.'),(8203,'5785bdf7',NULL,'Lencho\'s unwavering faith in God is the central theme of the story. Discuss how his faith is depicted and its consequences.','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','Lencho\'s unwavering faith in God is the cornerstone of the story \'A Letter to God\'. His faith is depicted as absolute and childlike, bordering on naive. When his entire corn crop is destroyed by the hailstones, his immediate and only recourse is to God. He does not lament or seek help from neighbors; instead, he writes a letter to God, asking for a hundred pesos, confident that God will not let him starve.\n\nThis faith has significant consequences. Firstly, it leads him to write the letter, which then falls into the hands of the postmaster. Secondly, his conviction moves the postmaster and his employees to contribute money, demonstrating an act of human kindness inspired by divine faith. Thirdly, when Lencho receives only seventy pesos, his faith in God remains unshaken, but he suspects the post office employees, calling them \'a bunch of crooks\'. This highlights the purity of his faith in God, contrasted with his distrust of humanity. Ultimately, his deep belief, though misdirected in its suspicion of others, reinforces the central idea that faith can move mountains (or at least prompt postal workers to act).'),(8204,'5785bdf7',NULL,'Both \'Amanda!\' and \'The Trees\' deal with the theme of freedom and confinement. Compare and contrast how these themes are explored in the two poems, highlighting the desires of the protagonists in each.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Both \'Amanda!\' and \'The Trees\' poignantly explore the themes of freedom and confinement, though through very different lenses and with distinct \'protagonists\'.\n\nIn \'Amanda!\', the confinement is psychological and social. Amanda, a young girl, is constantly nagged and controlled by her parents, who dictate every aspect of her behavior, from her posture to her eating habits. Her desires for freedom manifest in escapist fantasies: she wishes to be a \'mermaid\' drifting aimlessly, an \'orphan\' roaming freely, or \'Rapunzel\' living alone in a tower. Her confinement is the suffocating expectation of societal norms and parental control, hindering her individuality and self-expression. She longs for an untroubled, independent existence where she can define herself.\n\nConversely, in \'The Trees\', the confinement is physical and symbolic. The trees are physically trapped within the confines of a human house, their roots \'toil all night\' to break free. They symbolize oppressed nature or perhaps even oppressed women. Their desire is a primal urge for physical liberation – to \'move out into the forest\', reclaim their natural habitat, and fulfill their ecological role. The poem contrasts the \'empty forest\' with the \'forest that was empty all these days\', emphasizing the unnaturalness of their captivity. The trees are actively struggling, embodying a powerful push for liberation from artificial boundaries.\n\nWhile Amanda\'s struggle is internal and imaginative against a stifling environment, the trees\' struggle is a tangible, physical fight against unnatural domestication. Both, however, highlight a universal yearning for autonomy and the right to exist freely according to one\'s own nature or desires.'),(8205,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Which of the following is an example of a Rabi crop?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. Paddy\", \"B. Maize\", \"C. Wheat\", \"D. Cotton\"]','C'),(8206,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'The process of converting sugar into alcohol by yeast is called:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. Fermentation\", \"B. Pasteurisation\", \"C. Nitrogen fixation\", \"D. Photosynthesis\"]','A'),(8207,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Which synthetic fibre is known as artificial silk?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. Nylon\", \"B. Rayon\", \"C. Polyester\", \"D. Acrylic\"]','B'),(8208,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'The fusion of male and female gametes is called:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. Budding\", \"B. Fertilisation\", \"C. Regeneration\", \"D. Metamorphosis\"]','B'),(8209,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Which of the following non-metals is essential for our life and all living beings inhale it during breathing?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"A. Nitrogen\", \"B. Carbon\", \"C. Sulphur\", \"D. Oxygen\"]','D'),(8210,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'The least polluting fuel for vehicles is:','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"A. Petrol\", \"B. Diesel\", \"C. Compressed Natural Gas (CNG)\", \"D. Kerosene\"]','C'),(8211,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'A substance that does not catch fire easily is called:','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"A. Combustible\", \"B. Inflammable\", \"C. Non-combustible\", \"D. Spontaneous\"]','C'),(8212,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'The force per unit area is called:','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"A. Thrust\", \"B. Pressure\", \"C. Friction\", \"D. Work\"]','B'),(8213,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'The splitting of white light into its constituent colours is known as:','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"A. Reflection\", \"B. Refraction\", \"C. Dispersion\", \"D. Diffraction\"]','C'),(8214,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Which of the following is responsible for depletion of the ozone layer?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"A. Carbon dioxide\", \"B. Sulphur dioxide\", \"C. Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)\", \"D. Methane\"]','C'),(8215,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'The place where animals are protected in their natural habitat is called a wildlife __________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','sanctuary'),(8216,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'The onset of puberty is controlled by __________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','hormones'),(8217,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Substances which reduce friction are called __________.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','lubricants'),(8218,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'The process of depositing a layer of any desired metal on another material by means of electricity is called __________.','Fill in the Blanks','Hard',1,'null','electroplating'),(8219,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'The __________ of sound is determined by its amplitude.','Fill in the Blanks','Hard',1,'null','loudness'),(8220,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'What are weeds? Give an example.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Weeds are unwanted plants that grow along with cultivated crops and compete for water, nutrients, space, and light. An example of a weed is Parthenium (carrot grass) or Amaranthus (pigweed).'),(8221,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Name two microorganisms that are useful to us and state one use for each.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','1. Lactobacillus: Used in the making of curd from milk. \n2. Yeast: Used in the baking industry for making bread and in the production of alcohol.'),(8222,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'List two advantages of using synthetic fibres over natural fibres.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','1. Synthetic fibres are generally more durable and stronger than natural fibres. \n2. They are usually wrinkle-resistant, quick-drying, and less expensive.'),(8223,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Differentiate between metals and non-metals based on their malleability and ductility.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Metals are generally malleable, meaning they can be beaten into thin sheets, and ductile, meaning they can be drawn into wires. For example, gold and copper are highly malleable and ductile.\nNon-metals, on the other hand, are neither malleable nor ductile. They are brittle and tend to break when hammered or stretched. For example, sulphur and carbon (in the form of coal) will break into pieces.'),(8224,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'What are the main uses of CNG and LPG?','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','CNG (Compressed Natural Gas) is primarily used as a fuel in vehicles because it is a cleaner fuel. LPG (Liquefied Petroleum Gas) is used as a fuel for cooking in homes (domestic fuel) and in some industries.'),(8225,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Differentiate between internal and external fertilisation with an example of each.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','Internal fertilisation is the fusion of male and female gametes that occurs inside the female body. Example: Humans, hens. \nExternal fertilisation is the fusion of male and female gametes that occurs outside the female body, usually in water. Example: Frogs, fish.'),(8226,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Explain the greenhouse effect and name two greenhouse gases.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','The greenhouse effect is a natural process that warms the Earth\'s surface. When the Sun\'s energy reaches Earth\'s atmosphere, some of it is reflected back to space and the rest is absorbed and re-radiated by greenhouse gases. These gases trap heat, preventing it from escaping into space, thus keeping the Earth warm enough to sustain life. Two greenhouse gases are Carbon dioxide (CO2) and Methane (CH4).'),(8227,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'What is the difference between regular and diffused reflection?','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','Regular reflection occurs when light rays strike a smooth, polished surface (like a mirror) and are reflected parallel to each other in a single direction, forming a clear image.\nDiffused (or irregular) reflection occurs when light rays strike a rough or uneven surface (like a wall or piece of paper) and are reflected in different directions, scattering the light and not forming a clear image.'),(8228,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'What are the characteristics of an ideal fuel? Explain any three.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','An ideal fuel is one that is readily available, cheap, burns easily at a moderate rate, produces a large amount of heat, and does not leave behind any undesirable substances.\n\nThree characteristics are:\n1.  **High Calorific Value:** It should produce a large amount of heat per unit mass when burned. This makes it efficient for various applications.\n2.  **Moderate Ignition Temperature:** It should have an appropriate ignition temperature – not too low (to prevent accidental fires) and not too high (so it can be easily ignited).\n3.  **Low Pollution/Clean Burning:** It should not produce harmful gases (like carbon monoxide, sulphur dioxide) or smoke and ash upon combustion, thus being environmentally friendly.'),(8229,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Explain any five consequences of deforestation.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','Deforestation, the clearing of forests for other land uses, has several severe consequences:\n1.  **Global Warming:** Trees absorb carbon dioxide. Deforestation leads to an increase in atmospheric CO2, contributing to the greenhouse effect and global warming.\n2.  **Soil Erosion:** Tree roots hold soil particles together. Removing trees makes the soil loose and more susceptible to erosion by wind and water, leading to loss of fertile topsoil.\n3.  **Loss of Biodiversity:** Forests are home to a vast variety of plants and animals. Deforestation destroys their natural habitat, leading to the endangerment and extinction of many species.\n4.  **Desertification:** With the loss of trees and soil erosion, the land can become barren and desert-like, a process known as desertification.\n5.  **Water Cycle Disturbance:** Trees play a crucial role in the water cycle through transpiration. Deforestation can disrupt rainfall patterns, leading to reduced rainfall and increased drought in some areas, and flooding in others.'),(8230,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Define pressure. Explain why school bags have broad straps. Give one more example from daily life where pressure application is useful.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Pressure is defined as the force acting perpendicularly on a unit area of a surface (Pressure = Force/Area).\n\nSchool bags have broad straps because broad straps increase the area of contact with the shoulders. Since pressure is inversely proportional to the area, a larger area reduces the pressure exerted by the bag\'s weight on the shoulders. This makes it more comfortable to carry the bag.\n\nAnother example where pressure application is useful in daily life is in cutting tools like knives. A sharp knife has a very small cutting edge (small area). When a force is applied, it exerts a large pressure on the object, allowing it to cut through easily.'),(8231,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Describe the different phases of the Moon. Why do we see different phases of the Moon?','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','The different phases of the Moon are the varying appearances of the Moon as seen from Earth, which include: New Moon, Crescent Moon, Quarter Moon, Gibbous Moon, and Full Moon. These phases occur in a regular cycle of approximately 29.5 days.\n\nWe see different phases of the Moon because the Moon does not produce its own light; it reflects sunlight. As the Moon revolves around the Earth, the amount of the illuminated side of the Moon visible to us changes. During the New Moon, the side facing Earth is not illuminated by the Sun. As it orbits, more and more of the sunlit portion becomes visible (waxing), until the Full Moon when the entire side facing Earth is illuminated. After the Full Moon, the visible illuminated portion decreases (waning) until the next New Moon.'),(8232,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'What are earthquakes? List three safety measures one should take during an earthquake.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Earthquakes are sudden tremors or shaking of the Earth\'s crust caused by the movement of tectonic plates deep inside the Earth. These movements generate waves that travel through the Earth and cause the ground to shake.\n\nThree safety measures one should take during an earthquake:\n1.  **Indoors:** Take cover under a sturdy table or desk and hold on to it. Stay away from windows, mirrors, and tall furniture that might fall.\n2.  **Outdoors:** Move to an open space, away from buildings, trees, streetlights, and utility wires. Lie down or crouch until the shaking stops.\n3.  **In a vehicle:** Stop your vehicle immediately and stay inside. Do not stop near or under buildings, trees, overpasses, or utility wires.'),(8233,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Match the items in Column A with Column B:\n\n**Column A**\n1. Yeast\n2. Nylon\n3. Tadpole\n4. Larynx\n5. Ozone layer\n\n**Column B**\n(a) Fermentation\n(b) Synthetic fibre\n(c) Metamorphosis\n(d) Voice box\n(e) Protects from UV radiation','Matching','Medium',5,'null','1 - a, 2 - b, 3 - c, 4 - d, 5 - e'),(8234,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Rina, a girl of 13, noticed that her height had increased suddenly, and her voice had started to become a little husky. Her mother explained to her that these changes are normal and are part of a developmental stage called adolescence. This period brings about many physical, mental, and emotional changes, driven by hormones.\n\n1. At what age does adolescence generally begin in girls?\n2. Name two hormones primarily responsible for changes in boys during adolescence.\n3. Besides physical changes, what are two other types of changes experienced during adolescence?','Case Study','Medium',3,'null','1. Adolescence generally begins around 10 to 12 years of age in girls.\n2. Testosterone and other androgens are primarily responsible for changes in boys during adolescence.\n3. Besides physical changes, individuals experience mental and emotional (or psychological) changes during adolescence.'),(8235,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'Ravi was performing an experiment to test the conductivity of different liquids. He observed that distilled water did not conduct electricity, but tap water did. Later, during a thunderstorm, he saw lightning flash across the sky and heard thunder. He learned that lightning is also an electric discharge.\n\n1. Why does tap water conduct electricity while distilled water does not?\n2. What process is used to make a liquid conductor into a non-conductor of electricity (or poor conductor)?\n3. Explain briefly how lightning occurs.','Case Study','Hard',3,'null','1. Tap water conducts electricity because it contains dissolved salts (minerals) and impurities, which provide ions to carry the electric current. Distilled water, being pure, lacks these dissolved salts and therefore does not conduct electricity.\n2. The process used to make a liquid conductor into a non-conductor (or poor conductor) of electricity is distillation, which removes dissolved impurities.\n3. Lightning occurs due to the accumulation of electric charges in the clouds. As air currents move within the clouds, they cause ice crystals and water droplets to collide, leading to the separation of charges. Positive charges accumulate near the top of the clouds and negative charges near the bottom. When the accumulated charge becomes very large, a sudden discharge of electricity occurs, either between two clouds or between a cloud and the Earth, which we see as lightning.'),(8236,'9ef75ab6',NULL,'In a small industrial town, people started complaining about respiratory problems and skin irritations. Investigations revealed that factories were releasing untreated effluents into the nearby river, and smoke containing harmful gases was emitted into the air. This led to a significant increase in both air and water pollution, affecting the local ecosystem and human health.\n\n1. Name two harmful gases typically released by factories that contribute to air pollution.\n2. How can untreated industrial effluents affect aquatic life in the river?\n3. Suggest one measure that factories can take to reduce air pollution.','Case Study','Hard',3,'null','1. Two harmful gases typically released by factories are Sulphur dioxide (SO2) and Nitrogen dioxide (NO2), which contribute to acid rain and respiratory problems.\n2. Untreated industrial effluents contain toxic chemicals, heavy metals, and high organic loads. These pollutants can deplete oxygen in the water, poison aquatic organisms, disrupt their reproductive cycles, and destroy their habitats, leading to a decline in aquatic biodiversity.\n3. One measure factories can take to reduce air pollution is to install scrubbers or electrostatic precipitators in their chimneys to filter out harmful gases and particulate matter before they are released into the atmosphere.'),(8237,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Which of the following is NOT a rabi crop?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"Wheat\", \"Gram\", \"Pea\", \"Paddy\"]','D'),(8238,'8af3aedc',NULL,'The process of converting sugar into alcohol by microorganisms is known as:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"Pasteurisation\", \"Fermentation\", \"Nitrification\", \"Sterilization\"]','B'),(8239,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Which of the following synthetic fibres is also known as artificial silk?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"Nylon\", \"Rayon\", \"Polyester\", \"Acrylic\"]','B'),(8240,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Which of the following metals is stored in kerosene?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"Iron\", \"Copper\", \"Sodium\", \"Aluminium\"]','C'),(8241,'8af3aedc',NULL,'The process of heating coal in the absence of air to obtain useful products like coke, coal tar, and coal gas is called:','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"Fractional distillation\", \"Destructive distillation\", \"Cracking\", \"Hydrogenation\"]','B'),(8242,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Project Tiger was launched to protect tigers in India. In which year was it launched?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"1970\", \"1973\", \"1980\", \"1985\"]','B'),(8243,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Which part of the female reproductive system connects the ovary to the uterus?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"Cervix\", \"Vagina\", \"Fallopian tube\", \"Urethra\"]','C'),(8244,'8af3aedc',NULL,'The pressure exerted by air around us is known as:','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"Gravitational pressure\", \"Atmospheric pressure\", \"Muscular pressure\", \"Frictional pressure\"]','B'),(8245,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Which of the following statements is true regarding concave mirrors?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"They always form real images.\", \"They always form virtual images.\", \"They can form both real and virtual images.\", \"They are used as rearview mirrors in vehicles.\"]','C'),(8246,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Which gas is responsible for depletion of the ozone layer?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"Carbon dioxide\", \"Sulphur dioxide\", \"Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)\", \"Methane\"]','C'),(8247,'8af3aedc',NULL,'A chemical process in which a substance reacts with oxygen to give off heat is called ______.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','Combustion'),(8248,'8af3aedc',NULL,'The onset of puberty in girls is marked by the start of ______.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','Menstruation'),(8249,'8af3aedc',NULL,'The force that opposes the motion of an object is called ______.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','Friction'),(8250,'8af3aedc',NULL,'The time taken by an object to complete one oscillation is called its ______.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','Time period'),(8251,'8af3aedc',NULL,'When electric current is passed through a conducting liquid, it causes ______ effects.','Fill in the Blanks','Hard',1,'null','Chemical'),(8252,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Name and define the two main types of crops based on seasons in India. Give one example for each.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','The two main types of crops are: \n1. Kharif crops: These are sown in the rainy season (June-September). E.g., Paddy.\n2. Rabi crops: These are sown in the winter season (October-March). E.g., Wheat.'),(8253,'8af3aedc',NULL,'List three harmful effects of microorganisms on humans.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Three harmful effects of microorganisms are:\n1.  They cause diseases in humans (e.g., bacteria causing tuberculosis, viruses causing common cold).\n2.  They spoil food by causing decay and producing toxins.\n3.  They cause diseases in plants (e.g., citrus canker, rust of wheat) and animals (e.g., anthrax).'),(8254,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Differentiate between thermoplastics and thermosetting plastics with two points of difference.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','1. Thermoplastics can be softened repeatedly by heating and bent easily, while thermosetting plastics, once molded, cannot be softened by heating.\n2. Thermoplastics are recyclable, whereas thermosetting plastics are not recyclable.'),(8255,'8af3aedc',NULL,'What is meant by the terms \'malleability\' and \'ductility\'? Give one example of a metal exhibiting these properties.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Malleability is the property of metals by which they can be beaten into thin sheets. Ductility is the property of metals by which they can be drawn into thin wires. Gold and silver are excellent examples of metals exhibiting both malleability and ductility.'),(8256,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Why are fossil fuels considered exhaustible natural resources? Give two reasons.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Fossil fuels are considered exhaustible natural resources for two main reasons:\n1.  They are formed from the dead remains of organisms over millions of years, a process that is extremely slow.\n2.  Their known reserves are limited and are being consumed at a much faster rate than their formation.'),(8257,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Explain deforestation and list any two of its consequences.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','Deforestation is the clearing of forests and converting the land to another use. Two consequences are:\n1.  Increase in atmospheric carbon dioxide, leading to global warming.\n2.  Loss of biodiversity, as many species lose their natural habitat.'),(8258,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Differentiate between internal fertilization and external fertilization. Give one example for each.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','Internal fertilization occurs inside the female body (e.g., humans, hens), while external fertilization occurs outside the female body, usually in water (e.g., frogs, fish).'),(8259,'8af3aedc',NULL,'State the laws of reflection. Draw a neat diagram to illustrate these laws.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','Laws of Reflection:\n1. The angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection.\n2. The incident ray, the reflected ray, and the normal to the surface at the point of incidence all lie in the same plane.\n(Diagram should show an incident ray, reflected ray, normal, and equal angles of incidence and reflection on a plane surface)'),(8260,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Explain atmospheric pressure. Describe with an activity how you can demonstrate the existence of atmospheric pressure.','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','Atmospheric pressure is the force per unit area exerted on a surface by the weight of the air above that surface. It is caused by the column of air extending from the surface to the outer limits of the atmosphere. \n\nActivity to demonstrate atmospheric pressure:\nMaterials: A glass tumbler, a piece of cardboard (slightly larger than the mouth of the tumbler), and water.\nProcedure:\n1. Fill the glass tumbler completely with water.\n2. Place the piece of cardboard over the mouth of the tumbler, ensuring no air bubbles are trapped inside.\n3. Hold the cardboard firmly with one hand and quickly invert the tumbler.\n4. Carefully remove your hand from the cardboard.\nObservation: The cardboard does not fall, and the water does not pour out.\nExplanation: The atmospheric pressure acting upwards on the cardboard is greater than the downward pressure exerted by the water column inside the tumbler. This upward atmospheric pressure supports the cardboard and the water.'),(8261,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Describe the formation of stars. Explain what a constellation is and give two examples.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','Stars are formed from giant clouds of gas and dust called nebulae. Gravity causes these clouds to collapse, forming a dense, hot core. As the core contracts, its temperature and pressure increase until nuclear fusion begins, converting hydrogen into helium and releasing immense energy, making the star shine.\n\nA constellation is a group of stars that form a recognizable pattern or outline in the night sky. These patterns are purely apparent from Earth and are often associated with mythological figures, animals, or objects.\nExamples: Ursa Major (Great Bear or Saptarishi), Orion (The Hunter).'),(8262,'8af3aedc',NULL,'What is acid rain? Discuss its harmful effects on the environment and historical monuments.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Acid rain refers to rain or any other form of precipitation that is unusually acidic, meaning that it possesses elevated levels of hydrogen ions (low pH). It is primarily caused by emissions of sulfur dioxide (SO₂) and nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) into the atmosphere, which react with water, oxygen, and other chemicals to form sulfuric and nitric acids.\n\nHarmful effects:\n1.  **On the environment:** Acid rain significantly damages forests, lakes, and soil. It can dissolve nutrients in the soil, making them unavailable to plants, and releases toxic substances like aluminium into water bodies, harming aquatic life. It also corrodes buildings, bridges, and monuments.\n2.  **On historical monuments:** Acid rain reacts with materials like marble (calcium carbonate) used in historical buildings and statues, causing them to corrode and deteriorate. This process is known as \'marble cancer\', where the surface of the marble becomes pitted and discolored, leading to the gradual erosion of intricate carvings and structures. A prime example is the damage to the Taj Mahal due to acid rain.'),(8263,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Describe the process of electroplating. Give two practical applications of electroplating in our daily lives.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Electroplating is the process of coating one metal with a thin layer of another metal using an electric current. In this process:\n1.  The object to be electroplated (e.g., an iron spoon) is placed at the cathode (negative terminal) of the electric circuit.\n2.  A block of the metal to be deposited (e.g., copper or chromium) is used as the anode (positive terminal).\n3.  An electrolyte solution containing ions of the coating metal (e.g., copper sulphate solution for copper plating) is used.\n4.  When electric current is passed, the coating metal at the anode dissolves into the electrolyte, and its ions migrate towards the cathode, where they gain electrons and deposit as a thin, uniform layer on the object.\n\nTwo practical applications:\n1.  **Corrosion prevention:** Many metal objects, like iron, are electroplated with metals like chromium, nickel, or zinc (galvanization) to protect them from rusting and corrosion. For example, bicycle parts are often chromium-plated.\n2.  **Decorative purposes:** Jewellery is often electroplated with gold or silver to give it an attractive finish, making it appealing and more affordable than solid precious metal items.'),(8264,'8af3aedc',NULL,'Explain the major physical and emotional changes that occur in boys and girls during adolescence.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Adolescence is a period of transition from childhood to adulthood, marked by significant physical and emotional changes. \n\n**Physical Changes:**\nIn **Boys**:\n1.  Increase in height and weight.\n2.  Broadening of shoulders and chest.\n3.  Growth of facial hair (beard and moustache).\n4.  Deepening of voice (voice crack).\n5.  Development of reproductive organs.\n6.  Growth of hair in armpits, chest, and pubic region.\n\nIn **Girls**:\n1.  Increase in height and weight.\n2.  Broadening of hips and pelvic region.\n3.  Development of breasts.\n4.  Onset of menstruation (menarche).\n5.  Growth of hair in armpits and pubic region.\n6.  Development of reproductive organs.\n\n**Emotional Changes (for both boys and girls):**\n1.  **Increased self-awareness and self-consciousness:** Adolescents often become more concerned about their appearance and how others perceive them.\n2.  **Mood swings:** Hormonal changes can lead to rapid shifts in mood, from excitement to sadness or anger.\n3.  **Search for identity:** They start questioning their roles and values, trying to establish their unique identity.\n4.  **Increased independence:** A desire for greater autonomy from parents and guardians.\n5.  **Peer group influence:** Friends become increasingly important, and peer pressure can be significant.\n6.  **Emotional sensitivity:** They may become more sensitive to criticism and emotional ups and downs.'),(8265,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'The practice of leaving the land uncultivated for a season to allow the soil to regain its fertility naturally is called:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. Crop rotation\", \"B. Fallowing\", \"C. Weeding\", \"D. Harvesting\"]','B'),(8266,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Which of the following is used in the production of alcohol and bread?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. Bacteria\", \"B. Viruses\", \"C. Protozoa\", \"D. Yeast\"]','D'),(8267,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Which of the following is a thermosetting plastic?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"A. Polyethylene\", \"B. PVC\", \"C. Bakelite\", \"D. Nylon\"]','C'),(8268,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Which metal is stored in kerosene to prevent its reaction with air and moisture?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. Iron\", \"B. Copper\", \"C. Sodium\", \"D. Aluminium\"]','C'),(8269,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'A push or a pull on an object is called:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. Pressure\", \"B. Force\", \"C. Friction\", \"D. Gravity\"]','B'),(8270,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'The angle of incidence is always equal to the angle of reflection:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. Only for smooth surfaces\", \"B. Only for rough surfaces\", \"C. Always\", \"D. Never\"]','C'),(8271,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'The unit of frequency is:','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"A. Metre\", \"B. Second\", \"C. Hertz\", \"D. Decibel\"]','C'),(8272,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'In which type of fertilization does the fusion of gametes occur inside the female body?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"A. External fertilization\", \"B. Internal fertilization\", \"C. Asexual reproduction\", \"D. Budding\"]','B'),(8273,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Electroplating is based on the:','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"A. Heating effect of electric current\", \"B. Magnetic effect of electric current\", \"C. Chemical effect of electric current\", \"D. Mechanical effect of electric current\"]','C'),(8274,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'The gas primarily responsible for the depletion of the ozone layer is:','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"A. Carbon dioxide\", \"B. Methane\", \"C. Sulphur dioxide\", \"D. Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)\"]','D'),(8275,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'The basic structural and functional unit of all living organisms is the ________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','cell'),(8276,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Friction always ________ the motion between two surfaces in contact.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','opposes'),(8277,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'The planet known as the \'Red Planet\' is ________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','Mars'),(8278,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'A place where animals are protected in their natural habitat is called a ________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','wildlife sanctuary'),(8279,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'The most destructive part of a cyclone is the ________.','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','eye wall'),(8280,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Differentiate between Kharif and Rabi crops, giving one example of each.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Kharif crops are sown in the rainy season (June to September) and harvested in autumn. Examples: Paddy, maize, soybean. Rabi crops are sown in the winter season (October to March) and harvested in spring. Examples: Wheat, gram, mustard.'),(8281,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Name two diseases caused by viruses and two caused by protozoa.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Two diseases caused by viruses: Common cold, Influenza (Flu), Polio, Measles. Two diseases caused by protozoa: Malaria, Dysentery, Kala-azar.'),(8282,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Why is plastic considered non-biodegradable? Give one disadvantage of using plastics.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Plastics are considered non-biodegradable because microorganisms cannot decompose them naturally. They remain in the environment for hundreds to thousands of years. One disadvantage of using plastics is that they cause environmental pollution when discarded, harming wildlife and ecosystems, and clogging drainage systems.'),(8283,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Give three uses of non-metals.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','1. Oxygen is a non-metal essential for respiration and burning. \n2. Nitrogen is used in fertilizers and for preserving food. \n3. Chlorine is used for water purification. \n4. Carbon (in the form of graphite) is used in pencil leads and electrodes. (Any three valid uses)'),(8284,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'What is deforestation? List two consequences of deforestation.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Deforestation is the permanent removal of forests for other land uses, such as agriculture, timber, or urbanization. Two consequences of deforestation are: 1. Increased carbon dioxide in the atmosphere, contributing to global warming. 2. Soil erosion and desertification due to the loss of tree roots holding the soil. 3. Loss of biodiversity, leading to the extinction of various plant and animal species.'),(8285,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Draw a labelled diagram of an animal cell.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','A well-drawn diagram of an animal cell showing organelles like cell membrane, nucleus, cytoplasm, mitochondria, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus, and ribosomes. Labels should be accurate.'),(8286,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Explain what causes pressure and give its formula.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','Pressure is caused by a force acting perpendicularly on a surface. It describes how concentrated a force is over a given area. The formula for pressure is: Pressure (P) = Force (F) / Area (A). Its SI unit is Pascal (Pa) or N/m².'),(8287,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'What is acid rain? Name two pollutants responsible for acid rain.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','Acid rain refers to any form of precipitation (rain, snow, fog, hail) that is unusually acidic, meaning it possesses elevated levels of hydrogen ions (low pH). It is caused by the emission of sulfur dioxide (SO₂) and nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) into the atmosphere. These gases react with water, oxygen, and other chemicals to form sulfuric and nitric acids, which then fall to the Earth as acid rain.'),(8288,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Describe the process of metamorphosis in frogs with a suitable diagram.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','Metamorphosis in frogs is a biological process by which a frog develops from a fertilised egg to an adult frog. The process begins with the female frog laying eggs in water. These eggs hatch into larvae called tadpoles. Tadpoles are aquatic and feed on algae. They have gills for breathing, a tail for swimming, and no limbs. As they grow, hind legs appear, followed by front legs. The gills disappear, and lungs develop for breathing air. The tail gradually shortens and eventually disappears. The tadpole transforms into a young frog, which can live on both land and water, and finally matures into an adult frog capable of reproduction. (Diagram should show egg, early tadpole, late tadpole with limbs, young frog, and adult frog).'),(8289,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Explain the role of hormones in the reproductive health of adolescents.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','Hormones play a crucial role in initiating and regulating reproductive health during adolescence. The pituitary gland, an endocrine gland, secretes hormones that stimulate the testes in males and the ovaries in females to release their respective hormones. In males, testosterone is released by the testes, which causes the development of secondary sexual characteristics like facial hair, deepening of voice, and sperm production. In females, estrogen is released by the ovaries, leading to the development of breasts, broadening of hips, and onset of menstruation. Progesterone, also from the ovaries, helps regulate the menstrual cycle. These hormones control the reproductive cycle, the development of reproductive organs, and the changes associated with puberty, preparing the individual for reproduction. Imbalance or issues with these hormones can affect reproductive health.'),(8290,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'How is sound produced? Explain with an example. Describe the conditions necessary for sound propagation.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','Sound is produced by vibrations. When an object vibrates, it sets the particles of the surrounding medium (like air) into vibration, creating compressions (regions of high pressure) and rarefactions (regions of low pressure) that travel outwards as a wave. For example, when you strike a drum, the drumhead vibrates, causing the air molecules around it to vibrate, which then propagate as sound waves to our ears. Conditions necessary for sound propagation: 1. A vibrating source: An object must vibrate to produce sound. 2. A medium: Sound requires a material medium (solid, liquid, or gas) to travel. It cannot travel through a vacuum because there are no particles to vibrate and transmit the sound energy. The speed of sound varies with the medium.'),(8291,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Draw a ray diagram to show the formation of an image by a plane mirror. List five characteristics of the image formed by a plane mirror.','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','A ray diagram showing an object placed in front of a plane mirror, with at least two incident rays from a point on the object hitting the mirror and reflecting. The reflected rays should be traced back behind the mirror to show where they appear to originate from, forming a virtual image. The image should be shown at an equal distance behind the mirror as the object is in front. \n\nFive characteristics of the image formed by a plane mirror:\n1.  **Virtual:** The image cannot be obtained on a screen.\n2.  **Erect:** The image is upright, not inverted.\n3.  **Laterally inverted:** The left side of the object appears on the right side of the image, and vice-versa.\n4.  **Same size as the object:** The height of the image is equal to the height of the object.\n5.  **Formed as far behind the mirror as the object is in front:** The distance of the image from the mirror is equal to the distance of the object from the mirror.'),(8292,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Describe the process of electroplating. Give two applications of electroplating in daily life.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','Electroplating is the process of depositing a thin layer of one metal onto the surface of another metal by means of an electric current. The object to be electroplated is made the cathode (negative electrode), and the metal to be deposited is made the anode (positive electrode). Both are immersed in an electrolyte solution containing ions of the metal to be deposited. When electric current is passed, the metal ions from the electrolyte migrate to the cathode (the object) and get deposited on its surface. Simultaneously, the anode metal dissolves into the solution to replenish the lost ions.\n\nTwo applications of electroplating in daily life:\n1.  **Jewellery:** Gold and silver plating is used on less expensive metals to make artificial jewellery appear more attractive and valuable.\n2.  **Corrosion Protection:** Iron objects (like car parts, bicycle handlebars) are often electroplated with chromium or nickel to prevent rust and provide a shiny, durable surface.\n3.  **Enhanced Appearance:** Items like tap fittings, car bumpers, and bicycle parts are electroplated with chromium to give them a bright, shiny finish.'),(8293,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Match the following:','Matching','Medium',4,'null','1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b'),(8294,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Read the following passage and answer the questions that follow:\n\nForests are vital for human life and play a crucial role in maintaining ecological balance. However, human activities such as industrialization, urbanization, and agricultural expansion have led to widespread deforestation and loss of natural habitats. This has serious consequences, including the threat to various plant and animal species, some of which are on the brink of extinction. To counter this, protected areas like national parks, wildlife sanctuaries, and biosphere reserves have been established.\n\nQ1. Why is conservation of biodiversity important? (2 marks)\nQ2. Name two examples of protected areas in India. (2 marks)\nQ3. What are endangered species? Give an example. (2 marks)\nQ4. What is the purpose of a Red Data Book? (2 marks)','Case Study','Easy',8,'null','A1. Conservation of biodiversity is important because it ensures the stability of ecosystems, provides essential resources for humans (food, medicine, clean air, water), and maintains the genetic diversity necessary for adaptation and evolution of life forms. \nA2. Two examples of protected areas in India are Jim Corbett National Park, Ranthambore National Park, Sunderbans National Park, Bharatpur Bird Sanctuary, Pachmarhi Biosphere Reserve (Any two valid examples). \nA3. Endangered species are species of plants or animals that are at a very high risk of extinction in the wild. Example: Tiger, Asiatic Lion, One-horned Rhinoceros (Any valid example). \nA4. The Red Data Book is a sourcebook that keeps a record of all the endangered animals and plants. It helps in identification and protection efforts for these threatened species.'),(8295,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Read the following passage and answer the questions that follow:\n\nAgriculture involves several steps, from preparing the soil to storing the harvested produce. Farmers undertake these practices diligently to ensure good yield and quality. Proper preparation of the soil is crucial for successful cultivation. Once seeds are sown, regular watering is required, and farmers must also deal with unwanted plants that grow alongside the crops. After the crops mature, they are harvested and then prepared for storage to prevent spoilage.\n\nQ1. What is \'tilling\' or \'ploughing\'? (2 marks)\nQ2. Why is it important to select healthy seeds for sowing? (2 marks)\nQ3. What are weeds? How are they removed? (2 marks)\nQ4. Define \'manure\' and \'fertilizer\'. (2 marks)','Case Study','Easy',8,'null','A1. Tilling or ploughing is the process of loosening and turning the soil. It helps in aeration of the soil, allows roots to penetrate deeper, and brings nutrient-rich soil to the surface.\nA2. It is important to select healthy seeds for sowing because healthy seeds are viable, germinate better, and grow into healthy plants, resulting in a good yield.\nA3. Weeds are unwanted plants that grow along with the crop and compete with it for water, nutrients, space, and light. They can be removed by manual methods (like hand-weeding, using a khurpi) or by using weedicides (chemicals).\nA4. Manure is an organic substance obtained from the decomposition of plant and animal waste, providing nutrients to the soil. Fertilizers are chemical substances, rich in specific nutrients (like NPK), produced in factories to enhance soil fertility.'),(8296,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Read the following passage and answer the questions that follow:\n\nMicroorganisms are omnipresent and play diverse roles in our environment. Some are beneficial, helping in food production, waste decomposition, and even medicine. Others are harmful, causing various diseases in plants, animals, and humans. Understanding their life cycles and interactions with their environment is crucial for both exploiting their benefits and controlling their detrimental effects.\n\nQ1. Name two beneficial microorganisms and their uses. (2 marks)\nQ2. How do antibiotics work? (2 marks)\nQ3. What is fermentation? Give an example of a food product made through fermentation. (2 marks)\nQ4. What is pasteurization? (2 marks)','Case Study','Medium',8,'null','A1. Two beneficial microorganisms and their uses are: Yeast (used in bread making and alcohol production), Lactobacillus (used in making curd from milk), Rhizobium (helps in nitrogen fixation in leguminous plants), Fungi (decompose dead organic matter). (Any two with uses)\nA2. Antibiotics are medicines that kill or stop the growth of disease-causing microorganisms, primarily bacteria. They work by interfering with bacterial processes like cell wall formation, protein synthesis, or DNA replication, without harming human cells.\nA3. Fermentation is the process by which microorganisms (like yeast or bacteria) convert sugars into alcohol or acids in the absence of oxygen. An example of a food product made through fermentation is bread, curd, idli, dosa, or alcohol.\nA4. Pasteurization is a process of heating a liquid (like milk) to a specific temperature for a set period, then immediately cooling it down, to kill harmful bacteria without significantly altering its taste or nutritional value. This increases its shelf life.'),(8297,'61a4bfc9',NULL,'Read the following passage and answer the questions that follow:\n\nLight is a form of energy that enables us to see. It travels in straight lines and interacts with surfaces in various ways, leading to phenomena like reflection, refraction, and dispersion. When light falls on objects, it reflects, and our eyes perceive these reflected rays. Beyond our Earth, the universe is vast, filled with celestial bodies like stars, planets, and moons. These bodies follow specific paths and exhibit unique characteristics, governed by fundamental physical laws.\n\nQ1. State the laws of reflection. (2 marks)\nQ2. What is dispersion of light? Give an example. (2 marks)\nQ3. Explain why astronauts cannot hear each other on the moon. (2 marks)\nQ4. Differentiate between a planet and a star. (2 marks)','Case Study','Hard',8,'null','A1. The laws of reflection state: (i) The angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection. (ii) The incident ray, the normal to the surface at the point of incidence, and the reflected ray all lie in the same plane.\nA2. Dispersion of light is the phenomenon where white light splits into its constituent colours (VIBGYOR) when it passes through a transparent medium like a prism. Example: Formation of a rainbow after rainfall, or light passing through a prism.\nA3. Astronauts cannot hear each other on the moon because there is no atmosphere on the moon. Sound requires a material medium (like air, water, or solid) to propagate. In the vacuum of space, there are no particles to vibrate and transmit sound waves, so sound cannot travel.\nA4. A planet is a celestial body that orbits a star, is massive enough to be rounded by its own gravity, and has cleared its orbital neighborhood of other debris. It does not produce its own light or heat but reflects light from its star. A star is a massive, luminous ball of plasma, held together by its own gravity, that generates its own light and heat through nuclear fusion in its core.'),(8298,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Which of the following is a Kharif crop?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Wheat\", \"B) Gram\", \"C) Paddy\", \"D) Pea\"]','C'),(8299,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Which of these microorganisms is used in the production of alcohol?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Bacteria\", \"B) Yeast\", \"C) Protozoa\", \"D) Algae\"]','B'),(8300,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Which synthetic fibre resembles wool and is used for making shawls and blankets?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Nylon\", \"B) Rayon\", \"C) Acrylic\", \"D) Polyester\"]','C'),(8301,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Which of the following is a good conductor of electricity?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Sulphur\", \"B) Coal\", \"C) Copper\", \"D) Oxygen\"]','C'),(8302,'04c5cd89',NULL,'The control center of the cell is the:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Cytoplasm\", \"B) Nucleus\", \"C) Cell wall\", \"D) Mitochondria\"]','B'),(8303,'04c5cd89',NULL,'The fusion of male and female gametes is called:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Budding\", \"B) Fragmentation\", \"C) Fertilization\", \"D) Binary fission\"]','C'),(8304,'04c5cd89',NULL,'The period of life, when the body undergoes changes leading to reproductive maturity, is called:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Childhood\", \"B) Adolescence\", \"C) Adulthood\", \"D) Infancy\"]','B'),(8305,'04c5cd89',NULL,'The SI unit of force is:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Pascal\", \"B) Joule\", \"C) Newton\", \"D) Watt\"]','C'),(8306,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Which of the following reduces friction?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Rough surface\", \"B) Lubricant\", \"C) Increasing weight\", \"D) Pressing surfaces\"]','B'),(8307,'04c5cd89',NULL,'The phenomenon of splitting of white light into its constituent colours is called:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A) Reflection\", \"B) Refraction\", \"C) Dispersion\", \"D) Scattering\"]','C'),(8308,'04c5cd89',NULL,'The gas mainly responsible for global warming is __________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','Carbon dioxide'),(8309,'04c5cd89',NULL,'__________ is a place where animals are protected in their natural habitat.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','Wildlife Sanctuary (or National Park/Biosphere Reserve)'),(8310,'04c5cd89',NULL,'The strength of a sound wave is expressed in terms of its __________.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','amplitude'),(8311,'04c5cd89',NULL,'The chemical effect of electric current causes __________ changes.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','chemical'),(8312,'04c5cd89',NULL,'The Earth is the __________ planet from the Sun.','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','third'),(8313,'04c5cd89',NULL,'What is irrigation? Name two traditional methods of irrigation.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Irrigation is the process of supplying water to crops at regular intervals. Two traditional methods of irrigation are: 1. Moat (Pulley-system) 2. Chain pump.'),(8314,'04c5cd89',NULL,'State three uses of bacteria in our daily lives.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','1. Production of curd from milk. 2. Production of cheese. 3. Decomposing organic waste (e.g., in sewage treatment). 4. In the production of antibiotics and vaccines. (Any three)'),(8315,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Why are plastics considered non-biodegradable? Give two examples of non-biodegradable waste.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Plastics are considered non-biodegradable because they cannot be decomposed by natural processes (like action of bacteria or other microorganisms) over a long period. Two examples of non-biodegradable waste are plastic bags and aluminum cans.'),(8316,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Give three points of difference between metals and non-metals based on their reactivity with oxygen.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','1. Metals generally react with oxygen to form basic oxides (e.g., Magnesium oxide is basic). Non-metals react with oxygen to form acidic or neutral oxides (e.g., Sulphur dioxide is acidic, Carbon monoxide is neutral).\n2. Metallic oxides turn red litmus blue. Non-metallic oxides turn blue litmus red or have no effect on litmus.\n3. Some metals react readily with oxygen at room temperature (e.g., Sodium, Potassium), while others react on heating (e.g., Magnesium, Copper). Non-metals often react with oxygen at higher temperatures.'),(8317,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Define migration. Why do some animals migrate?','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','Migration is the seasonal movement of animals from one region to another. Some animals migrate to escape harsh, cold climatic conditions and to find suitable breeding grounds and food.'),(8318,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Draw a neat labelled diagram of a plant cell.','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null',' (A clear, well-labelled diagram of a plant cell showing cell wall, cell membrane, nucleus, cytoplasm, vacuole, chloroplasts, mitochondria, etc. should be provided by the student.)'),(8319,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Distinguish between asexual and sexual reproduction.','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','1. **Number of Parents:** Asexual reproduction involves a single parent, while sexual reproduction involves two parents (male and female).\n2. **Gametes:** Asexual reproduction does not involve the fusion of gametes, whereas sexual reproduction involves the fusion of male and female gametes.\n3. **Offspring:** Offspring produced by asexual reproduction are genetically identical to the parent. Offspring produced by sexual reproduction are genetically similar but not identical to the parents.'),(8320,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Why is it difficult to walk on a slippery floor? Explain in terms of friction.','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','It is difficult to walk on a slippery floor because there is very little friction between our feet and the floor. Friction is the force that opposes relative motion between two surfaces in contact. When we walk, we push the ground backward, and the friction between our feet and the ground pushes us forward. On a slippery surface, the friction is greatly reduced, so the ground cannot exert enough forward push, making it hard to move without slipping.'),(8321,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Explain the greenhouse effect. List two greenhouse gases and their sources.','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','The greenhouse effect is a natural process that warms the Earth\'s surface. When the Sun\'s energy reaches Earth\'s atmosphere, some of it is reflected back to space and the rest is absorbed and re-radiated by greenhouse gases. These gases trap heat in the atmosphere, preventing it from escaping back into space, thus keeping the Earth warm enough to support life.\nTwo greenhouse gases and their sources:\n1.  **Carbon dioxide (CO2):** Sources include burning of fossil fuels (coal, petroleum, natural gas) in industries and vehicles, and deforestation.\n2.  **Methane (CH4):** Sources include paddy fields, marshes, decomposition of organic matter, and cattle.'),(8322,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Describe the nitrogen cycle with the help of a diagram. Explain the role of nitrogen-fixing bacteria.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','(A well-labelled diagram of the nitrogen cycle showing atmospheric nitrogen, nitrogen fixation, nitrification, assimilation, ammonification, and denitrification should be included by the student.)\n\n**Role of Nitrogen-fixing Bacteria:**\nNitrogen-fixing bacteria, such as Rhizobium (found in the root nodules of leguminous plants) and certain free-living bacteria in the soil (e.g., Azotobacter), play a crucial role in the nitrogen cycle. They convert atmospheric nitrogen (N2), which plants cannot directly use, into usable forms like nitrates and nitrites. This process is called nitrogen fixation, making nitrogen available to plants for synthesizing proteins and other compounds.'),(8323,'04c5cd89',NULL,'What is adolescence? Describe any five changes that occur in boys and girls during this period.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','Adolescence is the period of life when the body undergoes changes, leading to reproductive maturity. This period generally begins around 10-11 years of age and lasts up to 18 or 19 years of age.\n\nFive changes during adolescence:\n1.  **Increase in height:** Both boys and girls experience a sudden spurt in growth, increasing their height rapidly.\n2.  **Change in body shape:** Boys develop broader shoulders and narrower waists, while girls develop broader hips and rounded body parts.\n3.  **Voice change:** In boys, the voice box (larynx) grows larger, leading to a deeper voice. Girls usually have a high-pitched voice.\n4.  **Development of sex organs:** Reproductive organs mature, producing gametes. Testes develop in boys, and ovaries enlarge in girls.\n5.  **Appearance of secondary sexual characteristics:** In boys, facial hair (moustache, beard) and hair on the chest develop. In girls, breasts develop, and menstruation begins.'),(8324,'04c5cd89',NULL,'State the laws of reflection. With a neat labelled diagram, explain the formation of an image by a plane mirror.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','**Laws of Reflection:**\n1.  The angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection (∠i = ∠r).\n2.  The incident ray, the reflected ray, and the normal to the surface at the point of incidence all lie in the same plane.\n\n**(A neat, labelled diagram showing an object, a plane mirror, incident rays, reflected rays, extended rays forming a virtual image behind the mirror, and the normal at the point of incidence should be provided by the student.)**\n\n**Image Formation by a Plane Mirror:**\nWhen an object is placed in front of a plane mirror, light rays from the object strike the mirror and get reflected. The reflected rays appear to originate from a point behind the mirror. This point is where the image is formed. Since the rays only appear to meet and do not actually meet, the image formed is virtual. The image formed by a plane mirror is always virtual, erect, laterally inverted, of the same size as the object, and formed at the same distance behind the mirror as the object is in front.'),(8325,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Describe how sound is produced and propagated. Give two methods to reduce friction in machines.','Long Answer','Medium',5,'null','**Sound Production and Propagation:**\nSound is produced by vibrations. When an object vibrates, it causes the particles of the medium (like air, water, or solids) around it to vibrate. These vibrations are transmitted from one particle to another, creating a disturbance that travels through the medium. This traveling disturbance is called a sound wave. Sound needs a medium to travel; it cannot travel in a vacuum.\n\n**Methods to Reduce Friction in Machines:**\n1.  **Using Lubricants:** Applying lubricants like oil or grease between the moving parts of machines creates a thin layer that reduces direct contact and thus friction. This allows parts to slide smoothly over each other.\n2.  **Using Ball Bearings:** Ball bearings are used in rotating parts of machines (e.g., bicycle hubs). They convert sliding friction into rolling friction, which is much smaller, thereby reducing energy loss due to friction and wear and tear.\n3.  **Polishing Surfaces:** Making surfaces smoother by polishing them can reduce friction, although too smooth surfaces can sometimes increase adhesive friction. (Any two)'),(8326,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Match Column A with Column B:','Matching','Medium',4,'null','1-(c), 2-(a), 3-(b), 4-(d)'),(8327,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Farmers prepare their fields before sowing seeds. This involves various agricultural practices like ploughing, levelling, and manuring. One of the crucial steps is the selection of seeds and their sowing. Proper sowing methods ensure healthy crop growth and better yield.\n\n1. What is ploughing? Name one tool used for ploughing. (2 marks)\n2. Why is it important to level the soil after ploughing? (2 marks)\n3. What is broadcasting? Name an advantage of sowing seeds using a seed drill. (2 marks)\n4. Briefly explain the importance of adding manure and fertilisers to the soil. (2 marks)','Case Study','Easy',8,'null','1. Ploughing is the process of loosening and turning the soil. One tool used for ploughing is a plough (or a cultivator/tractor).\n2. It is important to level the soil after ploughing to prevent soil erosion by wind and water, and for uniform irrigation.\n3. Broadcasting is the method of scattering seeds manually over the field. An advantage of using a seed drill is that it sows seeds uniformly at a proper distance and depth, ensuring better yield and preventing overcrowding.\n4. Manure and fertilisers are added to the soil to replenish soil nutrients and maintain its fertility, which is essential for healthy plant growth and good crop yield.'),(8328,'04c5cd89',NULL,'Ritu was performing an experiment in the lab. She took a sample of substance \'X\' and tried to hammer it, but it broke into pieces. She then tried to conduct electricity through it and found it to be a poor conductor. Another substance \'Y\' she took could be hammered into thin sheets and was a good conductor of heat and electricity.\n\n1. Identify substance \'X\' and \'Y\'. Give a reason for your identification. (2 marks)\n2. What property of substance \'Y\' allows it to be hammered into thin sheets? Give another example of a substance showing this property. (2 marks)\n3. Would substance \'X\' react with dilute acids to produce hydrogen gas? Justify your answer. (2 marks)\n4. Name one application where substance \'Y\' is used due to its property of being a good conductor of heat. (2 marks)','Case Study','Hard',8,'null','1. Substance \'X\' is a non-metal because it is brittle (breaks on hammering) and a poor conductor of electricity. Substance \'Y\' is a metal because it is malleable (can be hammered into sheets) and a good conductor of heat and electricity.\n2. The property of substance \'Y\' that allows it to be hammered into thin sheets is malleability. Another example of a substance showing this property is gold (or aluminum, silver).\n3. Non-metals (substance \'X\') generally do not react with dilute acids to produce hydrogen gas. Only reactive metals react with dilute acids to displace hydrogen.\n4. Substance \'Y\' (a metal) is a good conductor of heat. It is used in cooking utensils, heating elements, or electrical wires (though question asks for heat application specifically). For example, copper or aluminum for making cooking pots.'),(8329,'04c5cd89',NULL,'A group of students visited a nearby forest. They observed many trees being cut down. The teacher explained that this activity, called deforestation, has severe consequences like soil erosion and desertification. She also discussed how some species are becoming extinct due to habitat loss and highlighted the importance of conservation efforts.\n\n1. Define deforestation. List two major causes of deforestation. (2 marks)\n2. What is soil erosion? How does deforestation contribute to it? (2 marks)\n3. What are endemic species? Give an example. (2 marks)\n4. Suggest two measures to conserve forests and wildlife. (2 marks)','Case Study','Medium',8,'null','1. Deforestation is the clearing of forests and conversion of the cleared land to other uses. Two major causes are: procuring land for cultivation and building houses/factories.\n2. Soil erosion is the removal of topsoil by wind or water. Deforestation contributes to soil erosion because tree roots hold the soil particles together. When trees are cut, the topsoil becomes loose and is easily carried away by wind and water.\n3. Endemic species are those species of plants and animals which are found exclusively in a particular area. An example is the Sal and Wild Mango in Pachmarhi Biosphere Reserve.\n4. Two measures to conserve forests and wildlife are: establishment of National Parks and Wildlife Sanctuaries, and promoting afforestation (planting more trees).'),(8330,'04c5cd89',NULL,'During a school science exhibition, a student demonstrated an experiment where he used lemon juice to complete an electric circuit to light a small LED bulb. Another student presented a model explaining how lightning occurs and the safety measures during a thunderstorm.\n\n1. Explain why lemon juice can conduct electricity. (2 marks)\n2. What is electroplating? Give one application of electroplating. (2 marks)\n3. Briefly explain how lightning occurs. (2 marks)\n4. List two ways to protect buildings from the damage caused by lightning. (2 marks)','Case Study','Hard',8,'null','1. Lemon juice contains citric acid, which dissociates into ions when dissolved in water. These free ions in the solution allow electric current to pass through it, making lemon juice a conductor of electricity.\n2. Electroplating is the process of depositing a layer of any desired metal on another material by means of electricity. One application is to coat metal objects with chromium for shine and protection against corrosion (e.g., car parts, bath taps).\n3. Lightning occurs due to the accumulation of electric charges (positive and negative) in the clouds. When the magnitude of these accumulated charges becomes very large, the air, which is normally a poor conductor of electricity, fails to resist their flow. This leads to a sudden, massive discharge of electricity between two oppositely charged clouds or between clouds and the Earth, producing a bright flash of light and sound.\n4. Two ways to protect buildings from lightning damage are: installing a lightning conductor on the building and ensuring proper earthing of electrical systems.'),(8331,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'कोशिका का \'ऊर्जा घर\' किसे कहा जाता है?','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. लाइसोसोम\", \"B. राइबोसोम\", \"C. माइटोकॉन्ड्रिया\", \"D. रिक्तिका\"]','C'),(8332,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'डाल्टन के परमाणु सिद्धांत के अनुसार, परमाणु:','MCQ','Easy',1,'[\"A. विभाज्य है\", \"B. अविभाज्य है\", \"C. सिर्फ एक प्रकार के कणों से बना है\", \"D. आवेशित होता है\"]','B'),(8333,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'वेग का SI मात्रक क्या है?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"A. m/s²\", \"B. m/s\", \"C. km/h\", \"D. N\"]','B'),(8334,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'जीवों के वर्गीकरण की मूल इकाई क्या है?','MCQ','Medium',1,'[\"A. वर्ग (Class)\", \"B. गण (Order)\", \"C. जाति (Species)\", \"D. वंश (Genus)\"]','C'),(8335,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'एंटीबायोटिक दवाएँ किसके विरुद्ध प्रभावी होती हैं?','MCQ','Hard',1,'[\"A. केवल वायरस\", \"B. केवल जीवाणु\", \"C. वायरस और जीवाणु दोनों\", \"D. फंगस और शैवाल\"]','B'),(8336,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'पौधों में जल का संवहन ___________ ऊतक द्वारा होता है।','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','जाइलम'),(8337,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'प्रोटॉन की खोज ___________ ने की थी।','Fill in the Blanks','Easy',1,'null','रदरफोर्ड (या गोल्डस्टीन)'),(8338,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'पृथ्वी पर गुरुत्वीय त्वरण \'g\' का मान लगभग ___________ m/s² होता है।','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','9.8'),(8339,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'वायुमंडल में ऑक्सीजन का प्रतिशत लगभग ___________ है।','Fill in the Blanks','Medium',1,'null','21%'),(8340,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'कार्य करने की क्षमता को ___________ कहते हैं।','Fill in the Blanks','Hard',1,'null','ऊर्जा'),(8341,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'खरीफ फसलें आमतौर पर ___________ ऋतु में बोई जाती हैं।','Fill in the Blanks','Hard',1,'null','वर्षा'),(8342,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'जल (H₂O) का मोलर द्रव्यमान ज्ञात कीजिए। (परमाणु द्रव्यमान: H = 1 u, O = 16 u)','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','जल (H₂O) का मोलर द्रव्यमान:\nहाइड्रोजन (H) का परमाणु द्रव्यमान = 1 u\nऑक्सीजन (O) का परमाणु द्रव्यमान = 16 u\nजल के एक अणु में 2 हाइड्रोजन परमाणु और 1 ऑक्सीजन परमाणु होता है।\nअतः, जल का मोलर द्रव्यमान = (2 × H का परमाणु द्रव्यमान) + (1 × O का परमाणु द्रव्यमान)\n= (2 × 1 u) + (1 × 16 u)\n= 2 u + 16 u\n= 18 u\nजल का मोलर द्रव्यमान 18 ग्राम/मोल (18 g/mol) होता है।'),(8343,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'रदरफोर्ड के परमाणु मॉडल की कोई दो कमियाँ लिखिए।','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','रदरफोर्ड के परमाणु मॉडल की दो मुख्य कमियाँ निम्नलिखित हैं:\n1.  **परमाणु के स्थायित्व की व्याख्या न कर पाना:** रदरफोर्ड के मॉडल के अनुसार, इलेक्ट्रॉन नाभिक के चारों ओर वृत्ताकार कक्षाओं में घूमते हैं। मैक्सवेल के विद्युत-चुंबकीय सिद्धांत के अनुसार, त्वरित आवेशित कण ऊर्जा का विकिरण करते हैं और अंततः नाभिक में गिर जाएँगे। यदि ऐसा होता, तो परमाणु अस्थिर होता, लेकिन परमाणु स्थायी होते हैं। रदरफोर्ड का मॉडल इस स्थायित्व की व्याख्या नहीं कर पाया।\n2.  **इलेक्ट्रॉनिक संरचना की व्याख्या न कर पाना:** यह मॉडल इलेक्ट्रॉन के वितरण और उसकी ऊर्जा के बारे में कोई जानकारी नहीं देता है। यह स्पेक्ट्रम की रेखीय प्रकृति की भी व्याख्या नहीं कर सका।'),(8344,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'प्लाज्मा झिल्ली को वर्णात्मक पारगम्य झिल्ली क्यों कहते हैं?','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','प्लाज्मा झिल्ली को वर्णात्मक पारगम्य झिल्ली (Selectively Permeable Membrane) इसलिए कहा जाता है क्योंकि यह कुछ चुनिंदा पदार्थों को कोशिका के अंदर या बाहर आने-जाने देती है, जबकि अन्य पदार्थों के लिए अवरोधक का काम करती है। यह कोशिका के आंतरिक वातावरण को नियंत्रित करने में महत्वपूर्ण भूमिका निभाती है, जिससे कोशिका के लिए आवश्यक पदार्थों को अंदर जाने और अनावश्यक या हानिकारक पदार्थों को बाहर निकालने में मदद मिलती है।'),(8345,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'पैरेन्काइमा और स्क्लेरेन्काइमा ऊतक में कोई दो अंतर लिखिए।','Short Answer','Easy',3,'null','पैरेन्काइमा और स्क्लेरेन्काइमा ऊतक में दो अंतर निम्नलिखित हैं:\n\n1.  **कोशिका भित्ति:** पैरेन्काइमा की कोशिका भित्ति पतली होती है, जबकि स्क्लेरेन्काइमा की कोशिका भित्ति लिग्निन के जमाव के कारण मोटी और कठोर होती है।\n2.  **जीवित/मृत अवस्था:** पैरेन्काइमा ऊतक की कोशिकाएँ जीवित होती हैं, जबकि स्क्लेरेन्काइमा ऊतक की कोशिकाएँ परिपक्वता पर मृत हो जाती हैं।\n3.  **कार्य:** पैरेन्काइमा ऊतक भोजन का भंडारण, प्रकाश संश्लेषण और गैसों के आदान-प्रदान में मदद करता है। स्क्लेरेन्काइमा ऊतक पौधों को यांत्रिक सहारा और कठोरता प्रदान करता है।'),(8346,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'संक्रामक रोगों के फैलने के कोई दो तरीके बताइए।','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','संक्रामक रोगों के फैलने के दो मुख्य तरीके निम्नलिखित हैं:\n\n1.  **वायु द्वारा:** खाँसने, छींकने या बोलने से निकलने वाली छोटी-छोटी बूँदें (ड्रॉपलेट्स) हवा में फैल जाती हैं। जब कोई स्वस्थ व्यक्ति इन दूषित बूँदों को साँस के साथ अंदर लेता है, तो वह संक्रमित हो सकता है (जैसे सर्दी-खाँसी, निमोनिया, टीबी)।\n2.  **जल द्वारा:** दूषित जल पीने या ऐसे जल से भोजन तैयार करने से जल-जनित रोग फैल सकते हैं। दूषित जल में रोगाणु होते हैं जो पाचन तंत्र को प्रभावित करते हैं (जैसे हैजा, टाइफाइड, हेपेटाइटिस ए)।\n3.  **लैंगिक संपर्क द्वारा:** कुछ रोग संक्रमित व्यक्ति के साथ सीधे लैंगिक संपर्क से फैलते हैं (जैसे सिफलिस, गोनोरिया, HIV-AIDS)।'),(8347,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'न्यूटन का गति का पहला नियम लिखिए और जड़त्व को परिभाषित कीजिए।','Short Answer','Medium',3,'null','**न्यूटन का गति का पहला नियम (जड़त्व का नियम):**\nप्रत्येक वस्तु अपनी विराम अवस्था में अथवा एक सीधी रेखा में एकसमान गति की अवस्था में बनी रहती है, जब तक कि उस पर कोई बाहरी असंतुलित बल कार्य न करे।\n\n**जड़त्व (Inertia):**\nकिसी वस्तु का वह गुण जिसके कारण वह अपनी विराम अवस्था या एकसमान गति की अवस्था में परिवर्तन का विरोध करती है, जड़त्व कहलाता है। जड़त्व वस्तु के द्रव्यमान पर निर्भर करता है; जिस वस्तु का द्रव्यमान अधिक होता है, उसका जड़त्व भी अधिक होता है।'),(8348,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'ध्वनि के परावर्तन के कोई दो व्यावहारिक अनुप्रयोग बताइए।','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','ध्वनि के परावर्तन के दो व्यावहारिक अनुप्रयोग निम्नलिखित हैं:\n\n1.  **मेगाफोन या हॉर्न:** इनमें शंकु के आकार के मुख होते हैं जो ध्वनि को एक विशेष दिशा में केंद्रित करके परावर्तित करते हैं, जिससे ध्वनि दूर तक सुनाई देती है।\n2.  **स्टेथोस्कोप:** डॉक्टर इसका उपयोग शरीर के भीतर की ध्वनियों (जैसे हृदय की धड़कन, फेफड़ों की आवाज़) को सुनने के लिए करते हैं। स्टेथोस्कोप में बार-बार ध्वनि का परावर्तन होता है, जिससे ध्वनि चिकित्सक के कानों तक पहुँचती है।\n3.  **कंसर्ट हॉल या सिनेमा हॉल की छतें:** इन हॉल की छतें घुमावदार बनाई जाती हैं ताकि ध्वनि परावर्तन के बाद हॉल के सभी कोनों तक पहुँच सके और समान रूप से वितरित हो।'),(8349,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'ग्रीनहाउस प्रभाव क्या है? इसके दो दुष्परिणाम लिखिए।','Short Answer','Hard',3,'null','**ग्रीनहाउस प्रभाव:**\nग्रीनहाउस प्रभाव एक प्राकृतिक प्रक्रिया है जिसके द्वारा पृथ्वी का वायुमंडल गर्म होता है। वायुमंडल में उपस्थित कुछ गैसें (जैसे कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड, मीथेन, नाइट्रस ऑक्साइड, जलवाष्प), जिन्हें ग्रीनहाउस गैसें कहा जाता है, सूर्य से आने वाली लघु तरंग विकिरण को पृथ्वी की सतह तक पहुँचने देती हैं। लेकिन पृथ्वी से परावर्तित होने वाली दीर्घ तरंग विकिरण (अवरक्त विकिरण) को अवशोषित कर लेती हैं और उन्हें अंतरिक्ष में वापस जाने से रोकती हैं, जिससे पृथ्वी का तापमान बना रहता है।\n\n**दुष्परिणाम:**\n1.  **वैश्विक तापन (Global Warming):** मानव गतिविधियों (जैसे जीवाश्म ईंधन जलाना, वनों की कटाई) के कारण ग्रीनहाउस गैसों की सांद्रता में वृद्धि हुई है, जिससे ग्रीनहाउस प्रभाव बढ़ गया है और पृथ्वी का औसत तापमान बढ़ रहा है।\n2.  **जलवायु परिवर्तन:** वैश्विक तापन के कारण चरम मौसमी घटनाएँ (जैसे अत्यधिक गर्मी, बाढ़, सूखा, तूफान) बढ़ गई हैं, समुद्र का स्तर बढ़ रहा है (ध्रुवीय बर्फ पिघलने के कारण), और जैव विविधता पर नकारात्मक प्रभाव पड़ रहा है।'),(8350,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'पादप कोशिका का नामांकित चित्र बनाइए और उसके किन्हीं तीन प्रमुख अंगों के कार्य लिखिए।','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','**(चित्र यहाँ बनाना संभव नहीं है, लेकिन एक पादप कोशिका के नामांकित चित्र की कल्पना करें जिसमें कोशिका भित्ति, कोशिका झिल्ली, केंद्रक, क्लोरोप्लास्ट, रिक्तिका, माइटोकॉन्ड्रिया, साइटोप्लाज्म आदि स्पष्ट रूप से नामांकित हों।)**\n\n**पादप कोशिका के तीन प्रमुख अंगों के कार्य:**\n1.  **कोशिका भित्ति (Cell Wall):** यह पादप कोशिका की सबसे बाहरी परत होती है, जो मुख्य रूप से सेल्यूलोज की बनी होती है।\n    *   **कार्य:** यह कोशिका को निश्चित आकार प्रदान करती है, उसे यांत्रिक सहारा देती है, और कोशिका को बाहरी आघातों तथा अत्यधिक जल हानि से बचाती है। यह कोशिका को स्फीति (turgidity) बनाए रखने में भी मदद करती है।\n2.  **क्लोरोप्लास्ट (Chloroplast):** ये विशेष प्रकार के प्लास्टिड होते हैं जिनमें हरा वर्णक क्लोरोफिल पाया जाता है।\n    *   **कार्य:** क्लोरोप्लास्ट प्रकाश संश्लेषण की प्रक्रिया द्वारा सूर्य के प्रकाश की ऊर्जा का उपयोग करके भोजन (ग्लूकोज) का निर्माण करते हैं। इस प्रकार, ये पादप कोशिका के लिए ऊर्जा उत्पादन का मुख्य स्थल हैं।\n3.  **रिक्तिका (Vacuole):** पादप कोशिकाओं में एक बड़ी केंद्रीय रिक्तिका होती है जो कोशिका के आयतन का 50-90% तक घेर सकती है।\n    *   **कार्य:** यह कोशिका में जल, पोषक तत्वों, अपशिष्ट उत्पादों और वर्णकों का भंडारण करती है। यह कोशिका को स्फीति प्रदान करके उसे कठोर बनाए रखने में मदद करती है और कोशिका के अपशिष्ट उत्पादों का निपटान भी करती है।'),(8351,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'गुरुत्वाकर्षण का सार्वत्रिक नियम लिखिए और सार्वत्रिक गुरुत्वाकर्षण स्थिरांक (G) का SI मात्रक और महत्व बताइए।','Long Answer','Easy',5,'null','**गुरुत्वाकर्षण का सार्वत्रिक नियम:**\nइस नियम के अनुसार, ब्रह्मांड में प्रत्येक पिंड प्रत्येक दूसरे पिंड को आकर्षित करता है। यह आकर्षण बल उन दो पिंडों के द्रव्यमानों के गुणनफल के समानुपाती होता है तथा उनके बीच की दूरी के वर्ग के व्युत्क्रमानुपाती होता है। यह बल दोनों पिंडों को मिलाने वाली रेखा की दिशा में लगता है।\n\nयदि दो पिंडों का द्रव्यमान क्रमशः m₁ और m₂ हो, और उनके केंद्रों के बीच की दूरी r हो, तो उनके बीच लगने वाला गुरुत्वाकर्षण बल (F) इस प्रकार दिया जाता है:\nF ∝ (m₁ × m₂)\nF ∝ 1/r²\nदोनों को मिलाने पर:\nF = G (m₁ × m₂) / r²\nजहाँ G एक आनुपातिकता स्थिरांक है, जिसे सार्वत्रिक गुरुत्वाकर्षण स्थिरांक कहा जाता है।\n\n**सार्वत्रिक गुरुत्वाकर्षण स्थिरांक (G):**\n1.  **SI मात्रक:** न्यूटन मीटर² प्रति किलोग्राम² (N m²/kg²)\n2.  **महत्व:** सार्वत्रिक गुरुत्वाकर्षण स्थिरांक (G) का महत्व निम्नलिखित है:\n    *   यह दर्शाता है कि गुरुत्वाकर्षण बल एक सार्वभौमिक बल है जो ब्रह्मांड में हर जगह और हर दो द्रव्यमानों के बीच कार्य करता है।\n    *   यह पृथ्वी को सूर्य के चारों ओर और चंद्रमा को पृथ्वी के चारों ओर गति में बनाए रखने वाले बल को निर्धारित करने में मदद करता है।\n    *   यह ज्वार-भाटा, ग्रहों की गति और खगोलीय पिंडों की संरचना जैसी कई खगोलीय घटनाओं की व्याख्या करता है।\n    *   G का मान बहुत कम होता है (लगभग 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg²), जिसका अर्थ है कि सामान्य वस्तुओं के बीच गुरुत्वाकर्षण बल बहुत कमजोर होता है, लेकिन बड़े खगोलीय पिंडों के लिए यह बहुत महत्वपूर्ण होता है।'),(8352,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'गतिज ऊर्जा को परिभाषित कीजिए और इसके सूत्र को व्युत्पन्न कीजिए।','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','**गतिज ऊर्जा (Kinetic Energy):**\nकिसी वस्तु में उसकी गति के कारण निहित ऊर्जा को गतिज ऊर्जा कहते हैं। यानी, जब कोई वस्तु गतिमान होती है, तो उसमें कार्य करने की क्षमता होती है, और यह क्षमता ही गतिज ऊर्जा कहलाती है।\n\n**गतिज ऊर्जा के सूत्र का व्युत्पन्न:**\nमान लीजिए कि m द्रव्यमान की एक वस्तु u प्रारंभिक वेग से गति कर रही है। उस पर F बल लगाने पर वह a त्वरण के साथ s दूरी तय करती है, और उसका अंतिम वेग v हो जाता है।\n\nगति के तीसरे समीकरण से:\nv² - u² = 2as\nअतः, s = (v² - u²) / 2a  ---(1)\n\nन्यूटन के गति के दूसरे नियम से:\nF = ma  ---(2)\n\nबल द्वारा किया गया कार्य (W) = बल (F) × विस्थापन (s)\nW = Fs\nसमीकरण (1) और (2) से F और s के मान रखने पर:\nW = (ma) × [(v² - u²) / 2a]\nW = m (v² - u²) / 2\nW = ½ m (v² - u²)\n\nयदि वस्तु विराम अवस्था (u = 0) से चलना प्रारंभ करती है, तो किया गया कार्य उसकी गतिज ऊर्जा के रूप में संचित होता है।\nतो, u = 0 रखने पर:\nW = ½ m (v² - 0²)\nW = ½ mv²\n\nचूंकि किया गया कार्य (W) ही गतिज ऊर्जा (KE) है, अतः गतिज ऊर्जा का सूत्र है:\n**KE = ½ mv²**\nजहाँ, m = वस्तु का द्रव्यमान, v = वस्तु का वेग।'),(8353,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'पाँच-जगत वर्गीकरण की अवधारणा का वर्णन कीजिए। प्रत्येक जगत के दो-दो उदाहरण दीजिए।','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','**पाँच-जगत वर्गीकरण (Five-Kingdom Classification):**\nR.H. Whittaker द्वारा 1969 में प्रस्तावित पाँच-जगत वर्गीकरण जीवों को वर्गीकृत करने का एक व्यापक रूप से स्वीकृत तरीका है। यह वर्गीकरण कोशिका संरचना (प्रोकैरियोटिक या यूकैरियोटिक), शारीरिक संगठन (एककोशिकीय या बहुकोशिकीय), पोषण का तरीका (स्वपोषी या विषमपोषी) और प्रजनन जैसे मानदंडों पर आधारित है। ये पाँच जगत हैं:\n\n1.  **मोनेरा (Monera):**\n    *   **विशेषताएँ:** ये सभी प्रोकैरियोटिक जीव होते हैं (सुविकसित केंद्रक नहीं होता)। ये एककोशिकीय होते हैं। इनमें कोशिका भित्ति हो सकती है या नहीं भी। पोषण स्वपोषी या विषमपोषी हो सकता है।\n    *   **उदाहरण:** जीवाणु (Bacteria), नील-हरित शैवाल (Cyanobacteria)।\n2.  **प्रोटिस्टा (Protista):**\n    *   **विशेषताएँ:** ये सभी यूकैरियोटिक (सुविकसित केंद्रक वाले) और एककोशिकीय जीव होते हैं। इनमें कुछ में प्रचलन के लिए सीलिया, फ्लैजेला या स्यूडोपोडिया होते हैं। पोषण स्वपोषी या विषमपोषी हो सकता है।\n    *   **उदाहरण:** अमीबा (Amoeba), पैरामीशियम (Paramecium), यूग्लीना (Euglena)।\n3.  **फंजाई (Fungi):**\n    *   **विशेषताएँ:** ये विषमपोषी, यूकैरियोटिक जीव हैं। ये मृतोपजीवी होते हैं, यानी सड़े-गले पदार्थों से पोषण प्राप्त करते हैं। इनकी कोशिका भित्ति काइटिन की बनी होती है।\n    *   **उदाहरण:** खमीर (Yeast), मशरूम (Mushroom)।\n4.  **प्लांटी (Plantae):**\n    *   **विशेषताएँ:** ये सभी बहुकोशिकीय, यूकैरियोटिक और स्वपोषी (प्रकाश संश्लेषण द्वारा भोजन बनाने वाले) जीव हैं। इनकी कोशिकाओं में क्लोरोफिल और कोशिका भित्ति (मुख्यतः सेल्यूलोज की) होती है।\n    *   **उदाहरण:** आम (Mango), गुलाब (Rose), शैवाल (Algae)।\n5.  **एनीमेलिया (Animalia):**\n    *   **विशेषताएँ:** ये सभी बहुकोशिकीय, यूकैरियोटिक और विषमपोषी जीव हैं। इनमें कोशिका भित्ति नहीं होती। ये भोजन के लिए प्रत्यक्ष या अप्रत्यक्ष रूप से पौधों पर निर्भर करते हैं और भोजन को आंतरिक रूप से पचाते हैं।\n    *   **उदाहरण:** मानव (Humans), शेर (Lion), मछली (Fish)।'),(8354,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'मृदा प्रदूषण के प्रमुख कारण और उसके प्रभावों का वर्णन कीजिए। मृदा प्रदूषण को नियंत्रित करने के तीन उपाय भी सुझाइए।','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','**मृदा प्रदूषण के प्रमुख कारण:**\n1.  **रासायनिक उर्वरकों और कीटनाशकों का अत्यधिक उपयोग:** कृषि में अंधाधुंध रासायनिक उर्वरकों और कीटनाशकों के उपयोग से मृदा की उर्वरता कम होती है और हानिकारक रसायन मिट्टी में जमा हो जाते हैं।\n2.  **औद्योगिक अपशिष्ट:** उद्योगों से निकलने वाले अनुपचारित अपशिष्टों में भारी धातुएँ और अन्य विषैले रसायन होते हैं जो मृदा को प्रदूषित करते हैं।\n3.  **घरेलू कचरा:** प्लास्टिक, कांच, इलेक्ट्रॉनिक कचरा, और जैविक अपशिष्टों का अनुचित निपटान मृदा को प्रदूषित करता है और उसकी गुणवत्ता को कम करता है।\n4.  **अम्लीय वर्षा:** वायु प्रदूषण के कारण होने वाली अम्लीय वर्षा मृदा की pH को बदल देती है, जिससे उसकी संरचना और सूक्ष्मजीवों पर नकारात्मक प्रभाव पड़ता है।\n5.  **वनों की कटाई और मृदा अपरदन:** वनों की कटाई से मृदा का ऊपरी उपजाऊ भाग कटकर बह जाता है या उड़ जाता है, जिससे मिट्टी की गुणवत्ता खराब होती है।\n\n**मृदा प्रदूषण के प्रभाव:**\n1.  **कृषि उत्पादकता में कमी:** प्रदूषित मिट्टी में पौधों की वृद्धि बाधित होती है, जिससे फसल उत्पादन में कमी आती है।\n2.  **जल प्रदूषण:** मृदा में मौजूद प्रदूषक वर्षा जल के साथ रिसकर भूजल या सतही जल स्रोतों को भी प्रदूषित करते हैं।\n3.  **मानव स्वास्थ्य पर प्रभाव:** प्रदूषित मिट्टी में उगाई गई फसलों को खाने से हानिकारक रसायन मानव शरीर में प्रवेश कर सकते हैं, जिससे विभिन्न स्वास्थ्य समस्याएँ उत्पन्न हो सकती हैं।\n4.  **जैव विविधता का नुकसान:** मिट्टी में रहने वाले सूक्ष्मजीवों और अन्य जीवों की संख्या कम हो जाती है, जिससे मिट्टी का पारिस्थितिकी तंत्र असंतुलित हो जाता है।\n\n**मृदा प्रदूषण को नियंत्रित करने के उपाय:**\n1.  **जैविक खेती को बढ़ावा:** रासायनिक उर्वरकों और कीटनाशकों के स्थान पर जैविक खाद, कंपोस्ट और जैव-कीटनाशकों का उपयोग करना चाहिए।\n2.  **औद्योगिक अपशिष्टों का उपचार:** उद्योगों से निकलने वाले अपशिष्टों को मिट्टी में छोड़ने से पहले उनका उचित उपचार किया जाना चाहिए ताकि हानिकारक तत्व हटाए जा सकें।\n3.  **ठोस कचरा प्रबंधन:** प्लास्टिक और अन्य गैर-बायोडिग्रेडेबल कचरे का पुनर्चक्रण (recycling) और उचित निपटान सुनिश्चित करना चाहिए, तथा जैविक कचरे को कंपोस्ट में परिवर्तित करना चाहिए।'),(8355,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'फसल उत्पादन बढ़ाने के लिए विभिन्न तरीकों का वर्णन कीजिए, जैसे कि फसल चक्रण, अंतराफसलीकरण और मिश्रित खेती।','Long Answer','Hard',5,'null','फसल उत्पादन को बढ़ाने के लिए कई वैज्ञानिक विधियाँ अपनाई जाती हैं, जो मृदा की उर्वरता को बनाए रखने और कीटों तथा बीमारियों को नियंत्रित करने में मदद करती हैं:\n\n1.  **फसल चक्रण (Crop Rotation):**\n    *   **वर्णन:** यह एक ही खेत में विभिन्न फसलों को एक निश्चित क्रम में उगाने की प्रथा है। आमतौर पर, फलीदार फसलों (जैसे दालें) को अनाज वाली फसलों (जैसे गेहूँ, मक्का) के साथ चक्र में उगाया जाता है।\n    *   **लाभ:** फलीदार फसलें मिट्टी में नाइट्रोजन का स्थिरीकरण करती हैं, जिससे मिट्टी की उर्वरता बढ़ती है और रासायनिक उर्वरकों की आवश्यकता कम होती है। यह कीटों, बीमारियों और खरपतवारों के चक्र को तोड़ने में भी मदद करता है।\n\n2.  **अंतराफसलीकरण (Intercropping):**\n    *   **वर्णन:** इस विधि में, एक ही खेत में एक साथ दो या दो से अधिक फसलों को एक निश्चित पैटर्न में उगाया जाता है (जैसे एक पंक्ति में मक्का और अगली पंक्ति में लोबिया)। फसलों का चुनाव इस तरह किया जाता है कि उनकी पोषक तत्वों की आवश्यकताएँ भिन्न हों और वे एक-दूसरे को नुकसान न पहुँचाएँ।\n    *   **लाभ:** यह प्रति इकाई क्षेत्र में उत्पादन बढ़ाता है। यह कीटों और बीमारियों को फैलने से रोकता है क्योंकि एक फसल पर लगने वाले कीट दूसरी फसल को प्रभावित नहीं कर पाते। यह मिट्टी के कटाव को भी कम करता है।\n\n3.  **मिश्रित खेती (Mixed Cropping):**\n    *   **वर्णन:** इस विधि में एक ही खेत में दो या दो से अधिक फसलों को एक साथ बिना किसी निश्चित पैटर्न के बोया जाता है (जैसे गेहूँ और चना, या गेहूँ और सरसों)।\n    *   **लाभ:** यह कृषि जोखिम को कम करता है। यदि एक फसल विफल हो जाती है, तो दूसरी फसल से कुछ उपज प्राप्त हो जाती है। यह मिट्टी की उर्वरता बनाए रखने में भी मदद कर सकता है यदि फलीदार फसलों को अनाज के साथ बोया जाता है।'),(8356,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'एक वस्तु सीधी रेखा में 5 m/s के प्रारंभिक वेग से चलना प्रारंभ करती है। 10 सेकंड बाद उसका वेग 15 m/s हो जाता है।\n\n1.  वस्तु का त्वरण ज्ञात कीजिए। (2 अंक)\n2.  इन 10 सेकंड में वस्तु द्वारा तय की गई दूरी ज्ञात कीजिए। (3 अंक)','Case Study','Easy',5,'null','1.  **त्वरण ज्ञात करना:**\n    प्रारंभिक वेग (u) = 5 m/s\n    अंतिम वेग (v) = 15 m/s\n    समय (t) = 10 s\n    गति के पहले समीकरण से, v = u + at\n    15 = 5 + a × 10\n    15 - 5 = 10a\n    10 = 10a\n    a = 10 / 10\n    **a = 1 m/s²**\n\n2.  **दूरी ज्ञात करना:**\n    हम गति के दूसरे समीकरण का उपयोग कर सकते हैं: s = ut + ½ at²\n    s = (5 × 10) + ½ × 1 × (10)²\n    s = 50 + ½ × 1 × 100\n    s = 50 + 50\n    **s = 100 m**\n    वैकल्पिक रूप से, गति के तीसरे समीकरण से भी कर सकते हैं: v² = u² + 2as\n    (15)² = (5)² + 2 × 1 × s\n    225 = 25 + 2s\n    225 - 25 = 2s\n    200 = 2s\n    **s = 100 m**'),(8357,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'कार्बन का परमाणु क्रमांक 6 है। इसके दो समस्थानिक C-12 और C-14 हैं।\n\n1.  कार्बन-12 में प्रोटॉन और न्यूट्रॉन की संख्या बताइए। (2 अंक)\n2.  कार्बन-14 का उपयोग कहाँ किया जाता है? (1 अंक)\n3.  समस्थानिक किसे कहते हैं? (2 अंक)','Case Study','Easy',5,'null','1.  **कार्बन-12 में प्रोटॉन और न्यूट्रॉन की संख्या:**\n    परमाणु क्रमांक (Z) = 6 (जो प्रोटॉनों की संख्या के बराबर होता है)\n    द्रव्यमान संख्या (A) = 12\n    प्रोटॉन की संख्या = परमाणु क्रमांक = **6**\n    न्यूट्रॉन की संख्या = द्रव्यमान संख्या - प्रोटॉन की संख्या = 12 - 6 = **6**\n\n2.  **कार्बन-14 का उपयोग:**\n    कार्बन-14 का उपयोग **कार्बन डेटिंग** (Carbon Dating) में किया जाता है, जिससे जीवाश्मों, चट्टानों और अन्य पुरातात्विक नमूनों की आयु निर्धारित की जाती है।\n\n3.  **समस्थानिक:**\n    एक ही तत्व के वे परमाणु जिनकी परमाणु संख्या (प्रोटॉन की संख्या) समान होती है, लेकिन द्रव्यमान संख्या (प्रोटॉन और न्यूट्रॉन की कुल संख्या) भिन्न होती है, समस्थानिक कहलाते हैं।'),(8358,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'एक छात्र प्याज के छिलके का सूक्ष्मदर्शी से अवलोकन करता है और कुछ विशेष प्रकार की कोशिकाओं को देखता है।\n\n1.  वह कौन सा ऊतक देख रहा है? (1 अंक)\n2.  इस ऊतक की कोशिकाओं की दो विशेषताएँ लिखिए। (2 अंक)\n3.  यह ऊतक पौधों में क्या कार्य करता है? (2 अंक)','Case Study','Medium',5,'null','1.  छात्र प्याज के छिलके का अवलोकन कर रहा है, तो वह **पैरेन्काइमा (Parenchyma) ऊतक** देख रहा है, जो पौधों में मृदुता और भंडारण का कार्य करता है। यह एक प्रकार का साधारण स्थायी ऊतक है।\n\n2.  **पैरेन्काइमा कोशिकाओं की दो विशेषताएँ:**\n    *   ये कोशिकाएँ जीवित होती हैं और इनकी कोशिका भित्ति पतली होती है।\n    *   इनमें एक बड़ा केंद्रीय रिक्तिका (vacuole) होती है जो कोशिका के अधिकांश आयतन को घेरती है।\n    *   इनकी कोशिकाएँ आमतौर पर ढीली-ढाली (loosely packed) होती हैं, जिनके बीच बड़े अंतराकोशिकीय स्थान (intercellular spaces) होते हैं।\n\n3.  **यह ऊतक पौधों में निम्नलिखित कार्य करता है:**\n    *   **भोजन भंडारण:** यह स्टार्च, प्रोटीन और तेल जैसे पोषक तत्वों का भंडारण करता है।\n    *   **प्रकाश संश्लेषण:** यदि इसमें क्लोरोफिल होता है (जिसे क्लोरेन्काइमा कहते हैं), तो यह प्रकाश संश्लेषण का कार्य भी करता है।\n    *   **सहायता:** यह पौधों को संरचनात्मक सहायता प्रदान करता है, विशेषकर जब कोशिकाएँ स्फीत (turgid) होती हैं।\n    *   **गैसों का आदान-प्रदान:** अंतराकोशिकीय स्थानों के माध्यम से गैसों के आदान-प्रदान में मदद करता है।'),(8359,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'रवि को तेज बुखार, खाँसी और बदन दर्द है। डॉक्टर ने उसे एंटीबायोटिक दवा दी।\n\n1.  संभवतः रवि को कौन सा रोग हुआ है? (1 अंक)\n2.  एंटीबायोटिक दवाएँ किस प्रकार के सूक्ष्मजीवों के विरुद्ध प्रभावी होती हैं? (2 अंक)\n3.  वायरल संक्रमण के इलाज में एंटीबायोटिक क्यों प्रभावी नहीं होतीं? (2 अंक)','Case Study','Medium',5,'null','1.  रवि के लक्षणों (तेज बुखार, खाँसी, बदन दर्द) और एंटीबायोटिक दिए जाने से यह संकेत मिलता है कि उसे संभवतः कोई **जीवाणु संक्रमण (Bacterial infection)** हुआ है, जैसे कि जीवाणु जनित ब्रोंकाइटिस, निमोनिया का प्रारंभिक चरण, या कोई अन्य जीवाणु जनित श्वसन संक्रमण।\n\n2.  एंटीबायोटिक दवाएँ मुख्य रूप से **जीवाणुओं (Bacteria)** के विरुद्ध प्रभावी होती हैं। वे जीवाणुओं की वृद्धि को रोककर या उन्हें मारकर काम करती हैं।\n\n3.  वायरल संक्रमण के इलाज में एंटीबायोटिक प्रभावी नहीं होतीं क्योंकि:\n    *   वायरस में कोशिका भित्ति या अन्य जैव रासायनिक पथ नहीं होते हैं जिन पर एंटीबायोटिक काम कर सकें। एंटीबायोटिक आमतौर पर जीवाणु कोशिकाओं की विशिष्ट संरचनाओं या प्रक्रियाओं को लक्षित करती हैं।\n    *   वायरस मेजबान कोशिका के अंदर अपनी प्रतिकृति करते हैं, और एंटीबायोटिक मेजबान कोशिकाओं को नुकसान पहुँचाए बिना वायरस को सीधे मार नहीं सकतीं।'),(8360,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'उद्योगों और वाहनों से निकलने वाला धुआँ वायुमंडल को प्रदूषित करता है, जिससे कई पर्यावरणीय समस्याएँ उत्पन्न होती हैं।\n\n1.  वायु प्रदूषण के किन्हीं दो मुख्य कारणों का उल्लेख कीजिए। (2 अंक)\n2.  वायु प्रदूषण से होने वाले किन्हीं दो स्वास्थ्य संबंधी समस्याओं का वर्णन कीजिए। (2 अंक)\n3.  ताजमहल पर वायु प्रदूषण का क्या प्रभाव पड़ा है? (1 अंक)','Case Study','Hard',5,'null','1.  **वायु प्रदूषण के दो मुख्य कारण:**\n    *   **जीवाश्म ईंधन का जलना:** कोयला, पेट्रोलियम और प्राकृतिक गैस जैसे जीवाश्म ईंधनों को उद्योगों, बिजली संयंत्रों और वाहनों में जलाने से कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड, सल्फर डाइऑक्साइड, नाइट्रोजन ऑक्साइड और पार्टिकुलेट मैटर जैसे प्रदूषक निकलते हैं।\n    *   **औद्योगिक उत्सर्जन:** विभिन्न उद्योगों से निकलने वाले धुएँ और रसायनों में हानिकारक गैसें और कण होते हैं, जो सीधे वायुमंडल में मिल जाते हैं।\n    *   **वनों की कटाई:** पेड़ कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड को अवशोषित करते हैं। वनों की कटाई से वायुमंडल में कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड का स्तर बढ़ जाता है, जिससे ग्रीनहाउस प्रभाव बढ़ता है।\n\n2.  **वायु प्रदूषण से होने वाली दो स्वास्थ्य संबंधी समस्याएँ:**\n    *   **श्वसन संबंधी रोग:** वायु प्रदूषक (जैसे PM2.5 कण) फेफड़ों में प्रवेश कर सकते हैं, जिससे अस्थमा, ब्रोंकाइटिस, वातस्फीति (emphysema) और फेफड़ों के कैंसर जैसी बीमारियाँ हो सकती हैं।\n    *   **हृदय रोग:** वायु प्रदूषण के संपर्क में आने से हृदय रोग, उच्च रक्तचाप और स्ट्रोक का खतरा बढ़ जाता है।\n    *   **तंत्रिका संबंधी समस्याएँ:** कुछ भारी धातुएँ और विषैले प्रदूषक मस्तिष्क और तंत्रिका तंत्र को भी प्रभावित कर सकते हैं।\n\n3.  **ताजमहल पर वायु प्रदूषण का प्रभाव:**\n    वायु प्रदूषण, विशेष रूप से अम्लीय वर्षा (सल्फर डाइऑक्साइड और नाइट्रोजन ऑक्साइड के कारण) के कारण ताजमहल का सफेद संगमरमर **पीला** पड़ रहा है। इस घटना को \'मार्बल कैंसर\' भी कहते हैं।'),(8361,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'किसान अपने खेतों में जैविक खाद का उपयोग करते हैं और रासायनिक उर्वरकों तथा कीटनाशकों से बचते हैं।\n\n1.  जैविक खाद के कोई दो लाभ बताइए। (2 अंक)\n2.  जैविक कृषि (Organic Farming) किसे कहते हैं? (2 अंक)\n3.  केंचुए किस प्रकार किसानों के मित्र कहलाते हैं? (1 अंक)','Case Study','Hard',5,'null','1.  **जैविक खाद के दो लाभ:**\n    *   **मृदा की उर्वरता बढ़ाना:** जैविक खाद मिट्टी की संरचना में सुधार करती है, जल धारण क्षमता बढ़ाती है और आवश्यक सूक्ष्मजीवों की गतिविधियों को बढ़ावा देती है, जिससे मिट्टी स्वाभाविक रूप से उपजाऊ बनती है।\n    *   **पर्यावरण के अनुकूल:** ये रासायनिक उर्वरकों और कीटनाशकों के विपरीत पर्यावरण को प्रदूषित नहीं करते, जिससे मिट्टी, जल और वायु स्वच्छ रहते हैं।\n\n2.  **जैविक कृषि (Organic Farming):**\n    जैविक कृषि खेती की वह प्रणाली है जिसमें रासायनिक उर्वरकों, कीटनाशकों, खरपतवारनाशकों और आनुवंशिक रूप से संशोधित जीवों (GMOs) का उपयोग किए बिना फसलें उगाई जाती हैं। इसके बजाय, यह फसल चक्रण, हरी खाद, कंपोस्ट, जैविक कीट नियंत्रण और पशु अपशिष्ट जैसे प्राकृतिक तरीकों पर निर्भर करती है ताकि मिट्टी की उर्वरता और पारिस्थितिकी संतुलन को बनाए रखा जा सके।\n\n3.  **केंचुए किसानों के मित्र क्यों कहलाते हैं:**\n    केंचुए मिट्टी में सुरंगें बनाकर उसे **छिद्रपूर्ण (porous)** बनाते हैं, जिससे मिट्टी में वायु और जल का संचार बेहतर होता है। वे जैविक पदार्थों को खाकर और पचाकर मिट्टी को उपजाऊ बनाते हैं, जिसे **वर्मीकम्पोस्ट (vermicompost)** कहते हैं। इस प्रकार, वे मिट्टी की गुणवत्ता और उर्वरता बढ़ाने में मदद करते हैं, इसलिए उन्हें किसानों का मित्र कहा जाता है।'),(8362,'74d5f0b0',NULL,'कॉलम \'A\' में दिए गए मदों को कॉलम \'B\' में दिए गए उनके सही विवरण से सुमेलित कीजिए।\n\n**कॉलम \'A\'**\n1. माइटोकॉन्ड्रिया\n2. संवेग\n3. न्यूक्लियॉन\n4. जाइलम\n\n**कॉलम \'B\'**\na. प्रोटॉन और न्यूट्रॉन\nb. कोशिका का ऊर्जा घर\nc. पौधों में जल परिवहन\nd. द्रव्यमान × वेग','Matching','Medium',4,'null','1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c');
