INSERT INTO `questions` VALUES (1,'MCQ','Easy','The process in which a gas changes directly into liquid state without passing through the solid state is called:','[\"Melting\", \"Sublimation\", \"Evaporation\", \"Condensation\"]',1,'D','AI','Condensation is the process where water vapour or gas turns back into liquid, usually due to cooling.','english',279),(2,'MCQ','Easy','Which cell organelle is known as the \'Powerhouse of the cell\' because it produces ATP?','[\"Nucleus\", \"Mitochondria\", \"Chloroplast\", \"Endoplasmic Reticulum\"]',1,'B','AI','Mitochondria are the sites of cellular respiration, which generates ATP (energy currency).','english',279),(3,'MCQ','Easy','If an object is taken from the Earth to the Moon, which of the following physical quantities will change?','[\"Mass\", \"Density\", \"Volume\", \"Weight\"]',1,'D','AI','Weight (W = mg) changes because the acceleration due to gravity (g) is different on the Moon, while mass remains constant.','english',279),(4,'MCQ','Easy','An atom of an element contains 6 protons and 6 neutrons. What is the mass number of this atom?','[\"6\", \"0\", \"12\", \"18\"]',1,'C','AI','Mass number is the sum of the number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus (6 + 6 = 12).','english',279),(5,'MCQ','Medium','The primary function of Xylem tissue in plants is the transport of:','[\"Sugars and manufactured food\", \"Water and dissolved minerals from the roots\", \"Hormones synthesized in the shoot tip\", \"Amino acids only\"]',1,'B','AI','Xylem tissue is specialized for the upward conduction of water and dissolved mineral salts from the roots to other parts of the plant.','english',279),(6,'MCQ','Medium','A body continues to be in its state of rest or uniform motion unless acted upon by an external force. This principle defines:','[\"Law of conservation of energy\", \"Newton\'s First Law of Motion (Law of Inertia)\", \"Newton\'s Second Law of Motion\", \"Newton\'s Third Law of Motion\"]',1,'B','AI','Newton\'s First Law defines inertia, stating that an object maintains its velocity unless an external force acts on it.','english',279),(7,'Short Answer','Easy','Define acceleration and state its SI unit. If a bus starting from rest attains a speed of 30 m/s in 10 seconds, calculate its acceleration.','null',3,'Acceleration is defined as the rate of change of velocity. Its SI unit is meter per second squared (m/s²).\nCalculation: Initial velocity (u) = 0 m/s. Final velocity (v) = 30 m/s. Time (t) = 10 s.\nAcceleration (a) = (v - u) / t = (30 - 0) / 10 = 3 m/s².','AI','Acceleration is a fundamental concept in motion. The calculation is a direct application of the definition formula a=(v-u)/t.','english',279),(8,'Short Answer','Easy','Briefly state the primary functions of two important cell organelles: (i) Ribosomes and (ii) Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum (SER).','null',3,'(i) Ribosomes: They are the sites of protein synthesis (translation) in the cell.\n(ii) Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum (SER): It is crucial for the synthesis of lipids and steroids, and also plays a role in the detoxification of poisons and drugs in liver cells.','AI','This tests basic knowledge regarding the structure and function of key cellular components.','english',279),(9,'Short Answer','Medium','Differentiate between a True Solution and a Suspension based on (i) particle size and (ii) stability.','null',3,'True Solution vs. Suspension:\n(i) Particle Size: True solutions have particle sizes less than 1 nm, whereas Suspensions have particle sizes greater than 100 nm.\n(ii) Stability: True solutions are highly stable; the particles do not settle down. Suspensions are unstable; the particles settle down under gravity upon standing.','AI','Differentiating characteristics of different types of mixtures, based on physical properties.','english',279),(10,'Short Answer','Medium','State Newton\'s Second Law of Motion. Give the mathematical relation derived from this law, and define the resulting unit of force.','null',3,'Newton\'s Second Law states that the rate of change of momentum of an object is proportional to the applied unbalanced force in the direction of the force.\nMathematical relation: F = ma (Force = mass × acceleration).\nUnit of Force: The SI unit of force is the Newton (N). One Newton is defined as the force required to produce an acceleration of 1 m/s² in an object of mass 1 kg.','AI','Understanding the conceptual and mathematical aspects of Newton\'s second law.','english',279),(11,'Short Answer','Hard','Explain why organisms like Viruses are often considered controversial or are excluded from R.H. Whittaker\'s Five Kingdom Classification system. Give two reasons.','null',3,'Viruses are excluded because they do not display the characteristics of life until they enter a living cell, making them borderline between living and non-living.\n1. Non-cellular Structure: Viruses lack cellular machinery (like ribosomes or cytoplasm) and are essentially nucleic acid coated by protein, hence they do not fit into any kingdom based on cell structure.\n2. Obligate Parasitism: They can only reproduce by hijacking the metabolic machinery of a host cell. Outside a host, they are inert particles.','AI','This requires knowledge of viral structure and the criteria used for the Five Kingdom Classification.','english',279),(12,'Short Answer','Hard','Describe the two major limitations of Rutherford\'s model of the atom concerning atomic stability. How did Niels Bohr attempt to overcome these limitations?','null',3,'Limitations:\n1. Stability of the Atom: According to classical electromagnetic theory, an electron revolving in a circular orbit should accelerate and radiate energy. This would cause the electron to spiral into the nucleus, leading to the collapse of the atom, which contradicts observations.\n2. Spectral Explanation: Rutherford’s model could not explain the characteristic line spectra observed for elements.\nBohr\'s Resolution: Bohr proposed that electrons revolve only in specific, fixed orbits (discrete energy levels) and that they do not radiate energy while orbiting in these fixed shells, thus ensuring atomic stability.','AI','Requires critical understanding of the historical development of atomic models and their respective flaws.','english',279),(13,'Short Answer','Easy','List any three general principles that are essential for the prevention of infectious diseases in a community.','null',3,'Three general principles of prevention:\n1. Providing public hygiene and clean surroundings (e.g., safe drinking water, proper waste disposal).\n2. Preventing exposure (e.g., maintaining social distancing, vector control).\n3. Strengthening the immune system through specific measures like vaccination (immunization).','AI','This covers basic public health measures discussed in \'Why Do We Fall Ill\'.','english',279),(14,'Short Answer','Medium','Describe the critical role played by the bacterium Rhizobium in the Nitrogen Cycle and how this contributes to crop improvement.','null',3,'Role of Rhizobium:\nRhizobium bacteria live symbiotically in the root nodules of leguminous plants (like peas and beans).\nCritical Role: They perform Nitrogen Fixation, the process of converting atmospheric nitrogen gas (N₂) into usable forms like nitrates and nitrites. Atmospheric N₂ cannot be directly utilized by plants.\nContribution to Crop Improvement: By fixing nitrogen, Rhizobium makes the soil naturally rich in nitrogen compounds, reducing the need for artificial fertilizers and enhancing crop productivity in leguminous crops.','AI','Focuses on the symbiotic relationship and its significance in nutrient cycling and agriculture.','english',279),(15,'MCQ','Easy','Which of the following processes is responsible for the cooling effect when liquid evaporates?','[\"Condensation\", \"Sublimation\", \"Latent heat of fusion\", \"Latent heat of vaporisation\"]',1,'D','AI','Evaporation causes cooling because the liquid particles absorb energy (latent heat of vaporisation) from the surrounding area to overcome the forces of attraction and change state, thereby lowering the temperature of the surroundings.','english',279),(16,'MCQ','Easy','The powerhouse of the cell is:','[\"Ribosome\", \"Mitochondria\", \"Endoplasmic Reticulum\", \"Nucleus\"]',1,'B','AI','Mitochondria are responsible for cellular respiration and generating ATP, which supplies energy to the cell, hence they are called the powerhouse of the cell.','english',279),(17,'MCQ','Medium','A body starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 2 m/s². What will be its velocity after 5 seconds?','[\"5 m/s\", \"10 m/s\", \"2 m/s\", \"20 m/s\"]',1,'B','AI','Using the first equation of motion, v = u + at. Since the body starts from rest, u=0. v = 0 + (2 m/s² * 5 s) = 10 m/s.','english',279),(18,'MCQ','Medium','Calculate the molar mass of water (H₂O). (Given: Atomic masses H=1 u, O=16 u)','[\"17 g/mol\", \"18 g/mol\", \"16 g/mol\", \"20 g/mol\"]',1,'B','AI','Molar mass of H₂O = (2 * Mass of H) + (1 * Mass of O) = (2 * 1) + (1 * 16) = 18 g/mol.','english',279),(19,'MCQ','Easy','Which kingdom includes organisms that are eukaryotic, multicellular, and possess cell walls but cannot perform photosynthesis?','[\"Monera\", \"Protista\", \"Fungi\", \"Plantae\"]',1,'C','AI','Fungi are multicellular eukaryotes with cell walls (made of chitin) but are heterotrophic (cannot perform photosynthesis).','english',279),(20,'MCQ','Medium','The maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in the M shell (n=3) of an atom is:','[\"2\", \"8\", \"18\", \"32\"]',1,'C','AI','The maximum number of electrons in any shell is given by the formula 2n². For M shell, n=3. Max electrons = 2 * (3)² = 18.','english',279),(21,'Fill in the Blanks','Easy','The value of acceleration due to gravity (g) is maximum at the _________ of the Earth.','null',1,'Poles','AI','Due to the Earth\'s flattening at the poles, the radius is smallest there, leading to the maximum value of g.','english',279),(22,'Fill in the Blanks','Easy','The process of getting rid of unwanted plants (weeds) in the field is called _________.','null',1,'Weeding','AI','Weeding is the manual or chemical removal of unwanted plants (weeds) that compete with crops for nutrients and light.','english',279),(23,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','Tissues that are responsible for the movement in our body are the _________ tissues.','null',1,'Muscular','AI','Muscular tissues are composed of specialized cells (muscle fibres) that contract and relax to cause movement.','english',279),(24,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','A substance is said to be a compound if it is composed of two or more elements chemically combined in a fixed _________.','null',1,'Ratio','AI','Compounds must adhere to the Law of Constant Proportions, meaning the elements are combined in a fixed ratio by mass.','english',279),(25,'Short Answer','Easy','Why does a person tending to jump out of a moving bus fall forward?','null',3,'This is due to the Law of Inertia (Newton\'s First Law of Motion). When the person jumps, their entire body is initially moving forward with the velocity of the bus (Inertia of Motion). As their feet hit the ground, the feet stop, but the upper body continues to move forward due to inertia, causing the person to fall forward.','AI','Applies Newton\'s First Law: the tendency of the body to maintain its state of motion.','english',279),(26,'Short Answer','Easy','Define work done. Under what condition is the work done on an object zero?','null',3,'Work done is defined as the product of the force applied on an object and the displacement of the object in the direction of the force (W = F × d).\nWork done is zero under two main conditions:\n1. If the displacement (d) is zero (e.g., pushing a solid wall).\n2. If the force (F) is applied perpendicular (90°) to the direction of displacement (e.g., carrying a load horizontally).','AI','Work requires both force and displacement, and they must be in the same direction or have a component in the same direction.','english',279),(27,'Short Answer','Medium','Differentiate between tendon and ligament based on their structure and function.','null',3,'Tendon:\n1. Connects muscle to bone.\n2. Made of white fibrous tissue; great strength but limited flexibility.\nLigament:\n1. Connects bone to bone.\n2. Made of yellow fibrous tissue; very elastic and flexible.','AI','Tendon and ligament are types of specialized connective tissue crucial for skeletal movement and stability. Their primary difference lies in what they connect and their relative elasticity.','english',279),(28,'Short Answer','Medium','State the Law of Conservation of Mass. If 10g of Calcium Carbonate decomposes completely to give 4.4g of Carbon Dioxide, what mass of Calcium Oxide will be formed?','null',3,'Law of Conservation of Mass states that mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction. The total mass of reactants must equal the total mass of products.\nCalculation:\nReactants: Mass of CaCO₃ = 10 g\nProducts: Mass of CO₂ + Mass of CaO\nAccording to the law: 10 g = 4.4 g + Mass of CaO\nMass of CaO = 10 g - 4.4 g = 5.6 g.','AI','The law provides the basis for balancing chemical equations and calculating unknown masses in a reaction.','english',279),(29,'Short Answer','Hard','Explain Rutherford’s major flaw in his nuclear model of the atom and how Niels Bohr addressed this flaw.','null',3,'Rutherford\'s flaw: His model predicted that electrons, revolving in circular orbits, would accelerate and, according to classical electromagnetic theory, should continuously emit energy. This energy loss would cause the electron to spiral into the nucleus, making the atom highly unstable. However, atoms are observed to be stable.\nBohr’s correction: Bohr proposed that electrons occupy specific, discrete orbits (stationary states) and, as long as they remain in these orbits, they do not radiate energy. They only absorb or emit energy when they jump between these fixed energy levels.','AI','Rutherford\'s model failed to explain atomic stability. Bohr introduced the concept of quantized energy levels to solve this fundamental problem.','english',279),(30,'Long Answer','Hard','a) State the Universal Law of Gravitation. b) Derive the mathematical expression for this law. c) How is the value of \'G\' determined, and why is it known as the Universal Constant?','null',5,'a) The Universal Law of Gravitation states that every object in the universe attracts every other object with a force which is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.\nb) Derivation: Let M and m be the masses of two objects and r be the distance between their centres. According to the law:\n1. F ∝ M × m\n2. F ∝ 1 / r²\nCombining (1) and (2): F ∝ (M × m) / r²\nIntroducing the constant of proportionality G (Universal Gravitational Constant): F = G (M × m) / r².\nc) The value of G was experimentally determined by Henry Cavendish using a sensitive torsion balance apparatus. G is known as the Universal Constant because its value remains constant throughout the universe, regardless of the nature or size of the interacting bodies or the surrounding environment.','AI','This question tests the understanding of the theoretical, mathematical, and conceptual aspects of gravitation.','english',279),(31,'Long Answer','Hard','Draw a well-labelled diagram of an animal cell. Explain the structure and primary function of the Plasma Membrane and the Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER).','null',5,'Diagram: Must show nucleus, plasma membrane, mitochondria, ER (rough and smooth), Golgi apparatus, cytoplasm, etc., clearly labelled.\nPlasma Membrane (Cell Membrane):\nStructure: Composed of a lipid bilayer in which proteins are embedded (fluid mosaic model). It is flexible and selectively permeable.\nFunction: It regulates the movement of substances into and out of the cell (selective permeability). It maintains the cell’s shape and provides protection.\nEndoplasmic Reticulum (ER):\nStructure: A large network of membrane-bound tubules and sheets. It exists as Rough ER (RER, having ribosomes) and Smooth ER (SER, lacking ribosomes).\nFunction: RER is the site for protein synthesis (due to ribosomes) and transport. SER is responsible for the synthesis of lipids, steroids, and detoxification of drugs/poisons.','AI','Requires visual recall and detailed functional knowledge of key eukaryotic organelles.','english',279),(32,'Long Answer','Medium','a) Define the mole concept. b) Calculate the number of moles in 52 g of Helium gas (He). c) Calculate the mass of 0.5 mole of Nitrogen gas (N₂). (Atomic mass He = 4 u, N = 14 u; Avogadro Constant = 6.022 x 10²³ particles/mol).','null',5,'a) The mole is defined as the amount of substance that contains exactly 6.022 × 10²³ elementary entities (atoms, molecules, or ions). This number is the Avogadro constant.\nb) Calculation for Moles in 52 g He:\nMolar mass of He = 4 g/mol.\nNumber of moles = Given Mass / Molar Mass = 52 g / 4 g/mol = 13 moles.\nc) Calculation for Mass of 0.5 mole N₂:\nMolar mass of N₂ = 2 × 14 u = 28 g/mol.\nMass = Number of moles × Molar Mass = 0.5 mol × 28 g/mol = 14 g.','AI','Tests the foundational chemical concept of the mole and the ability to perform mass-to-mole conversions.','english',279),(33,'Long Answer','Hard','Describe the main steps involved in the Nitrogen Cycle. Explain the crucial role played by bacteria in this cycle.','null',5,'The Nitrogen Cycle involves four main steps:\n1. Nitrogen Fixation: Conversion of atmospheric nitrogen (N₂) into usable compounds like ammonia (NH₃) or nitrates. This is done primarily by certain bacteria (e.g., Rhizobium in root nodules or free-living blue-green algae).\n2. Ammonification: Conversion of complex organic nitrogen compounds (from dead plants/animals and waste) back into ammonia by decaying bacteria and fungi.\n3. Nitrification: Conversion of ammonia into nitrites by Nitrosomonas bacteria, and then nitrites are converted into nitrates by Nitrobacter bacteria. Plants absorb nitrogen mainly in the form of nitrates.\n4. Denitrification: Conversion of nitrates back into atmospheric nitrogen (N₂) by denitrifying bacteria (e.g., Pseudomonas), completing the cycle.\nCrucial Role of Bacteria: Bacteria are indispensable as they are the only organisms capable of performing nitrogen fixation (breaking the strong N₂ triple bond) and denitrification, thus controlling the flow of nitrogen between the atmosphere and the soil/living matter.','AI','Requires detailed knowledge of this specific biogeochemical cycle and the role of biological agents.','english',279),(34,'Matching','Medium','Match the items in Column A with the appropriate description/function in Column B.','null',3,'Plastids - Storage or photosynthesis in plant cells; Causative agent of AIDS - Virus (HIV); Sclerenchyma - Protective tissue in plants (provides structural support and rigidity).','AI','Plastids (like chloroplasts) handle photosynthesis/storage. AIDS is caused by HIV (a virus). Sclerenchyma provides mechanical strength.','english',279),(35,'Case Study','Medium','A sound wave emitted by a boat is detected by a microphone at the bottom of a lake. The time interval between the emission of the signal and its reception is 0.4 seconds. If the speed of sound in water is 1500 m/s, answer the following questions:\na) Name the phenomenon responsible for the signal returning to the detector. \nb) Calculate the depth of the lake at that point. \nc) What characteristic of the wave determines its loudness? \nd) If the frequency of the sound wave increases, how does it affect the pitch?','null',4,'a) The phenomenon is Echo or Reflection of Sound.\nb) Calculation: Since the time given (0.4 s) is the round trip time (down and back up).\nDistance = Speed × Time = 1500 m/s × 0.4 s = 600 m.\nDepth of the lake (one way distance) = Total Distance / 2 = 600 m / 2 = 300 m.\nc) The loudness (or intensity) of a sound wave is determined by its Amplitude.\nd) If the frequency of the sound wave increases, the pitch increases (becomes sharper or shriller).','AI','Applies concepts of sound reflection, calculation of distance using speed and time, and characteristics of sound waves (pitch and loudness).','english',279),(36,'Case Study','Medium','A farmer decides to diversify his income by starting practices that rely on both land and water resources. He plans to start integrated farming involving rearing fish in ponds and keeping beehives near his crops.\na) What is the scientific term for rearing fish on a large scale? \nb) State two differences between Composite Fish Culture and Capture Fishing. \nc) What advantage does the presence of beehives provide to the farmer’s crop production? \nd) Why is proper resource management crucial in integrated farming practices?','null',4,'a) Pisciculture (or Aquaculture).\nb) Composite Fish Culture (CFC) involves culturing 5 or 6 species in a single pond, utilizing all food zones, and aims for high yield in closed systems. Capture fishing involves catching fish from natural water bodies (seas, rivers) where yields are unpredictable.\nc) Bees act as excellent pollinators. Their presence increases the efficiency of pollination of the crops, leading to better seed setting and higher overall yields.\nd) Proper resource management (e.g., managing nutrient runoff from fish waste to fertilize crops, or using agricultural waste as fish food) minimizes waste, optimizes resource utilization, and reduces environmental impact, making the system economically viable and sustainable.','AI','This case study focuses on Improvement in Food Resources, testing knowledge of animal husbandry techniques and resource integration.','english',279),(37,'MCQ','Easy','When solid calcium oxide reacts vigorously with water, it produces calcium hydroxide and releases a large amount of heat. What type of chemical reaction is this?','[\"Decomposition reaction\", \"Combination reaction\", \"Displacement reaction\", \"Double displacement reaction\"]',1,'B','AI','Two reactants (CaO and H2O) combine to form a single product (Ca(OH)2). It is also an exothermic reaction.','english',352),(38,'MCQ','Easy','Which of the following is the energy currency for most cellular processes?','[\"DNA\", \"RNA\", \"ATP\", \"Glucose\"]',1,'C','AI','ATP (Adenosine Triphosphate) is the molecule used to store and transfer energy in cells.','english',352),(39,'MCQ','Medium','If two resistors, R1 and R2, are connected in parallel, how does their equivalent resistance (Req) compare to the individual resistances?','[\"Req is always greater than both R1 and R2.\", \"Req is equal to R1 + R2.\", \"Req is always smaller than the smallest individual resistance.\", \"Req is equal to the average of R1 and R2.\"]',1,'C','AI','In a parallel combination, the equivalent resistance is given by 1/Req = 1/R1 + 1/R2. This calculation always results in an equivalent resistance that is less than the value of the smallest resistor in the combination.','english',352),(40,'MCQ','Medium','A solution turns red litmus paper blue. Its pH value is likely to be:','[\"2\", \"5\", \"7\", \"10\"]',1,'D','AI','The change of red litmus to blue indicates that the solution is basic. Basic solutions have a pH greater than 7.','english',352),(41,'MCQ','Hard','A student uses a concave mirror to project the image of a candle flame on a screen. If the screen is moved closer to the mirror, the image size decreases. Where must the candle (object) currently be placed?','[\"At the focus (F)\", \"Between the pole (P) and focus (F)\", \"Between the center of curvature (C) and focus (F)\", \"Beyond the center of curvature (C)\"]',1,'C','AI','For a real image in a concave mirror, moving the screen closer means the image distance is decreasing. This happens when the object moves away from the mirror (towards C or beyond). Since the image is currently real and decreasing in size as the image distance decreases, the object must be between F and C (Image forms beyond C, magnified). Moving the screen closer corresponds to moving the object away from the mirror, resulting in a smaller image.','english',352),(42,'MCQ','Easy','Which mode of reproduction is generally favored by organisms living in a stable environment?','[\"Sexual reproduction\", \"Fragmentation\", \"Budding\", \"Fission\"]',1,'D','AI','Fission, budding, and fragmentation are forms of asexual reproduction. Asexual reproduction is faster and ideal for stable environments as it preserves the successful genotype without requiring searching for a mate or high variation.','english',352),(43,'Short Answer','Easy','What are amphoteric oxides? Give one example of a metal oxide that exhibits this property.','null',2,'Amphoteric oxides are metal oxides that react with both acids and bases to produce salts and water. Example: Aluminium oxide (Al2O3) or Zinc oxide (ZnO).','AI','This tests the basic definition and recall of the characteristic property of certain metal oxides (Al and Zn) that fall between basic and acidic behavior.','english',352),(44,'Short Answer','Medium','State the role of the receptor and effector organs in the process of a reflex action.','null',2,'Receptors are structures (like specialized cells or sense organs) that detect the stimulus (change in environment) and convert it into a nerve impulse. Effectors are muscles or glands that receive the instruction from the central nervous system (via motor nerves) and carry out the appropriate response.','AI','This requires understanding the two fundamental components needed for immediate involuntary response (reflex action).','english',352),(45,'Short Answer','Medium','A current-carrying straight conductor experiences a force when placed in a uniform magnetic field. State the rule used to determine the direction of this force and list the three quantities related by this rule.','null',2,'The rule used to determine the direction of the force is Fleming\'s Left-Hand Rule. The three quantities related by this rule are: 1. Force (Thumb), 2. Magnetic Field Direction (Forefinger), 3. Direction of Current (Middle finger).','AI','This assesses knowledge of the fundamental physics rule (Fleming\'s LHR) and the quantities represented by the fingers.','english',352),(46,'Short Answer','Hard','Explain why only about 10% of the energy is transferred to the next trophic level in a food chain.','null',2,'The energy transfer is limited to 10% because the remaining 90% of the energy is lost to the environment. This loss occurs primarily through metabolic processes of the organism at that level, such as respiration, digestion, movement, and heat loss to maintain body temperature. This makes food chains typically short (3-4 steps).','AI','This requires applying the concept of the 10% law and providing the reasons for the energy dissipation, which links biology and environmental science concepts.','english',352),(47,'Short Answer','Medium','Draw the electron dot structure for Methane (CH4). Explain the type of bonding present in this molecule.','null',3,'Electron Dot Structure: (The structure must show Carbon sharing 4 electrons with 4 Hydrogen atoms, resulting in 4 covalent bonds.) \nType of Bonding: Methane exhibits Covalent Bonding. This type of bonding is formed by the mutual sharing of valence electrons between the atoms (Carbon and Hydrogen) so that both achieve the stable noble gas configuration.','AI','This combines a drawing skill (electron dot structure for a simple hydrocarbon) with the theoretical explanation of covalent bonding, a key concept in Carbon compounds.','english',352),(48,'Short Answer','Medium','What is Myopia? State the two possible causes of this defect and suggest the type of lens required for its correction.','null',3,'Myopia (Nearsightedness) is the eye defect where a person can see nearby objects clearly but cannot see distant objects distinctly. \nPossible Causes: 1. Excessive curvature of the eye lens. 2. Elongation of the eyeball. \nCorrection: It is corrected by using a concave lens (or diverging lens) of appropriate power. The concave lens diverges the incoming light rays slightly before they reach the eye lens, ensuring the image is formed correctly on the retina.','AI','This comprehensive question requires defining the defect, listing its causes (3 marks requires more than just the definition), and specifying the corrective measure and lens type.','english',352),(49,'MCQ','Easy','Which process is responsible for the decrease in temperature during the cooling of water in an earthen pot (Matka)?','[\"Condensation\", \"Sublimation\", \"Evaporation\", \"Melting\"]',1,'C','AI','Evaporation causes cooling because the latent heat of vaporization is absorbed from the liquid inside the pot, lowering its temperature.','english',279),(50,'MCQ','Medium','If an object moves at a constant velocity, which of the following statements about its motion is true?','[\"Its acceleration is positive.\", \"A constant net force is acting on it.\", \"Its acceleration is zero.\", \"Its speed is increasing.\"]',1,'C','AI','Constant velocity means the rate of change of velocity (acceleration) is zero. According to Newton\'s First Law, zero net force results in zero acceleration.','english',279),(51,'MCQ','Easy','The powerhouse of the cell is known as:','[\"Ribosome\", \"Mitochondria\", \"Endoplasmic Reticulum\", \"Lysosome\"]',1,'B','AI','Mitochondria produce ATP, which is the energy currency of the cell, hence they are called the powerhouse.','english',279),(52,'MCQ','Medium','Which of the following elements has a valency of 4?','[\"Sodium (Na)\", \"Oxygen (O)\", \"Carbon (C)\", \"Magnesium (Mg)\"]',1,'C','AI','Carbon (Atomic Number 6) has an electronic configuration of 2, 4. It can share or gain/lose 4 electrons to achieve stability, giving it a valency of 4.','english',279),(53,'MCQ','Hard','Which type of disease spread relies primarily on vectors, and what is a common example?','[\"Air-borne; Tuberculosis\", \"Water-borne; Cholera\", \"Vector-borne; Malaria\", \"Sexual contact; AIDS\"]',1,'C','AI','Malaria is caused by the parasite Plasmodium and is transmitted by the female Anopheles mosquito, which acts as a vector. This connects concepts from \'Why Do We Fall Ill\' and \'Diversity in Living Organisms\' (insects as vectors).','english',279),(54,'Long Answer','Easy','A) Differentiate between meristematic tissues and permanent tissues in plants based on their dividing capacity and function. \nB) Explain the importance of cross-breeding and hybridisation in the context of Improvement in Food Resources.','null',7,'A) Meristematic tissues consist of actively dividing cells, responsible for growth, and are localized (apical, lateral, intercalary). Permanent tissues are derived from meristematic tissues, have lost the ability to divide, and perform specific functions (protection, conduction, support). B) Cross-breeding and hybridization are crucial for improving crop yields and quality by developing varieties with desirable characteristics like high yield, disease resistance, better quality produce, and adaptability to specific climatic conditions.','AI','This question requires recalling basic tissue classification (Biology) and defining fundamental agricultural improvement techniques (Food Resources). Straightforward recall and comparison make it Easy.','english',279),(55,'Long Answer','Medium','A) State the Law of Conservation of Energy. A body of mass 10 kg is lifted to a height of 5 m. Calculate the potential energy gained by the body (g = 9.8 m/s²). \nB) If this body is allowed to fall freely, what will its kinetic energy be just before it hits the ground? Justify your answer using the Law of Conservation of Energy.','null',7,'A) Law of Conservation of Energy states that energy can only be converted from one form to another; it cannot be created or destroyed. Total energy remains constant.\nPotential Energy (PE) = mgh = 10 kg * 9.8 m/s² * 5 m = 490 Joules.\nB) Just before hitting the ground, all the Potential Energy (490 J) will be converted entirely into Kinetic Energy (KE), assuming no energy loss due to air resistance. Therefore, KE = 490 J. This is justified by the Law of Conservation of Energy, where Total Energy (initial) = Total Energy (final), or PE (initial) = KE (final).','AI','Requires stating a physical law and applying the formula for potential energy, followed by an application of the conservation principle. This level of calculation and justification places it in the Medium difficulty category.','english',279),(56,'Long Answer','Easy','A) Define a homogeneous mixture and give two examples. \nB) Describe the method you would use to separate a mixture containing common salt (Sodium Chloride) and ammonium chloride. Explain the principle involved in the separation of these two components.','null',7,'A) A homogeneous mixture has a uniform composition throughout; its components are indistinguishable. Examples: Salt solution, sugar solution, air. \nB) Method: Sublimation. Ammonium chloride is a sublime substance (it changes directly from solid to gas upon heating) while common salt does not. The mixture is heated in a china dish covered with an inverted funnel plugged with cotton. Ammonium chloride vaporizes and solidifies upon hitting the cooler inner surface of the funnel, while salt remains behind in the dish. \nPrinciple: Differential tendency of components to sublime upon heating.','AI','Defining mixtures and outlining a standard separation technique (sublimation) based on chemical properties are core concepts of \'Is Matter Around Us Pure\'. This is primarily recall and procedural knowledge.','english',279),(57,'Long Answer','Medium','Explain the characteristics of the Carbon Cycle. How is atmospheric carbon dioxide primarily maintained in nature, and what human activities disrupt this balance? (Referencing Natural Resources)','null',7,'Characteristics of the Carbon Cycle: Carbon is cycled through the atmosphere, hydrosphere, lithosphere, and biosphere, mainly through photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition, and burning of fossil fuels.\nMaintenance of CO₂: The atmosphere\'s carbon dioxide concentration is primarily maintained by the balance between photosynthesis (which absorbs CO₂) and respiration/decomposition (which releases CO₂).\nDisrupting Activities: \n1. Combustion: Burning of fossil fuels (coal, petroleum) releases large quantities of stored carbon into the atmosphere as CO₂.\n2. Deforestation: Removal of forests reduces the amount of biomass available to absorb atmospheric CO₂ via photosynthesis, leading to an increased concentration of CO₂ in the atmosphere.','AI','This question requires explaining a complex biogeochemical cycle (the Carbon Cycle), detailing its maintenance mechanisms, and linking it explicitly to environmental issues caused by human activity (Natural Resources). This integration makes it Medium difficulty.','english',279),(58,'Long Answer','Hard','A) Describe Rutherford’s alpha-particle scattering experiment and state the two main conclusions he derived regarding the structure of the atom. \nB) Define the terms Atomic Number (Z) and Mass Number (A). An element has 18 electrons and a mass number of 35. Determine the number of protons, neutrons, and its atomic number. (Assume the atom is neutral).','null',7,'A) Experiment: Rutherford bombarded a very thin gold foil with fast-moving alpha (α) particles. He observed that most α-particles passed straight through, a few were deflected at small angles, and very few rebounded back. \nConclusions: 1) Most of the space inside the atom is empty. 2) The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom are concentrated in a very small central region called the nucleus. \nB) Atomic Number (Z): The total number of protons present in the nucleus of an atom. Mass Number (A): The sum of the number of protons and the number of neutrons in the nucleus.\nCalculation: For a neutral atom, Protons = Electrons. Thus, Protons = 18. Atomic Number (Z) = Number of Protons = 18. Mass Number (A) = 35. Number of Neutrons = A - Z = 35 - 18 = 17.','AI','This requires detailed descriptive knowledge (Rutherford\'s experiment and conclusions) and advanced application of atomic structure concepts (calculating protons, neutrons, and mass number from given electron count). The synthesis of descriptive and computational parts makes it Hard.','english',279),(59,'MCQ','Easy','Which of the following reaction types is represented by the reaction: $\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3 + 2\\text{Al} \\rightarrow 2\\text{Fe} + \\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$?','[\"Combination reaction\", \"Decomposition reaction\", \"Displacement reaction\", \"Double displacement reaction\"]',1,'C','AI','Aluminium (Al) is more reactive than Iron (Fe) and displaces Iron from its oxide, making it a displacement reaction.','english',352),(60,'MCQ','Easy','The site of photosynthesis in the cells of the leaf is:','[\"Mitochondria\", \"Cytoplasm\", \"Chloroplasts\", \"Vacuoles\"]',1,'C','AI','Photosynthesis occurs in the chloroplasts, which contain chlorophyll necessary for trapping sunlight.','english',352),(61,'MCQ','Medium','A student measures the pH of four different solutions (P, Q, R, S). P=12, Q=2, R=7, S=5. Which solution is strongly acidic?','[\"P\", \"Q\", \"R\", \"S\"]',1,'B','AI','A pH value close to 0 or 1 indicates a strong acid. pH 2 (Q) is strongly acidic, while pH 12 (P) is strongly basic, pH 7 (R) is neutral, and pH 5 (S) is weakly acidic.','english',352),(62,'MCQ','Medium','If a current-carrying straight conductor is held vertically, and the magnetic field lines around it are circles, then the direction of the magnetic field at a point directly east of the conductor, when the current flows upwards, will be:','[\"North\", \"South\", \"East\", \"West\"]',1,'A','AI','Using the Right-Hand Thumb Rule, if the thumb points up (current direction), the curled fingers indicate the magnetic field lines. At the east side, the magnetic field lines point towards the North.','english',352),(63,'MCQ','Hard','Which phenomenon is responsible for the splitting of white light into its seven constituent colours when passing through a prism?','[\"Reflection\", \"Scattering\", \"Total Internal Reflection\", \"Dispersion\"]',1,'D','AI','Dispersion is the phenomenon where white light splits into its constituent colours because the refractive index of the glass differs slightly for different wavelengths (colours).','english',352),(64,'Fill in the Blanks','Easy','Highly reactive metals like Sodium and Potassium are extracted by the process of ______________________ of their molten chlorides.','null',1,'Electrolysis','AI','Metals high in the reactivity series cannot be reduced by chemical means and require electrolytic reduction.','english',352),(65,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','The functional group present in ethanol and methanol is ______________________.','null',1,'Hydroxyl group (-OH)','AI','Alcohols contain the hydroxyl functional group, -OH.','english',352),(66,'Fill in the Blanks','Easy','In the process of fission in Amoeba, the division of the nucleus is called ______________________.','null',1,'Karyokinesis','AI','Karyokinesis refers specifically to the division of the nucleus, followed by cytokinesis (division of the cytoplasm).','english',352),(67,'Fill in the Blanks','Hard','The tendency of an organism to survive and reproduce in its environment is called ______________________.','null',1,'Natural Selection (or Survival of the Fittest)','AI','This term, central to the theory of evolution, defines the differential success in reproduction among individuals due to variations in their traits.','english',352),(68,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','The ability of carbon atoms to link with one another to form long chains, branched chains, and rings is called ______________________.','null',1,'Catenation','AI','Catenation is the self-linking property unique to carbon, allowing the formation of a large variety of organic compounds.','english',352),(69,'Short Answer','Easy','State the Law of Conservation of Mass. Balance the following chemical equation: $\\text{BaCl}_2 + \\text{Na}_2\\text{SO}_4 \\rightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4 + \\text{NaCl}$.','null',3,'The Law of Conservation of Mass states that mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction. The total mass of reactants must equal the total mass of products. Balanced equation: $\\text{BaCl}_2 + \\text{Na}_2\\text{SO}_4 \\rightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4 + 2\\text{NaCl}$.','AI','The law is foundational chemistry. Balancing requires ensuring the number of atoms of each element is equal on both sides; here, multiplying NaCl by 2 balances the Sodium and Chlorine atoms.','english',352),(70,'Short Answer','Medium','Differentiate between Aerobic and Anaerobic respiration based on the following criteria: (a) Requirement of Oxygen (b) End Products (c) Amount of Energy released.','null',3,'Aerobic respiration requires oxygen; its end products are $\\text{CO}_2$, $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$, and a large amount of energy ($\\approx 38$ ATP). Anaerobic respiration occurs without oxygen; its end products are ethanol (in yeast) or lactic acid (in muscle cells), $\\text{CO}_2$ (in yeast), and a small amount of energy ($\\approx 2$ ATP).','AI','Aerobic respiration is highly efficient, maximizing ATP production, while anaerobic respiration is faster but far less efficient.','english',352),(71,'Short Answer','Medium','Explain the characteristics of the image formed when an object is placed at the centre of curvature (C) of a concave mirror. Draw a neat ray diagram to support your answer.','null',3,'When an object is placed at C of a concave mirror, the image is formed (i) at C itself, (ii) Real and inverted, and (iii) of the same size as the object. The ray diagram should show two rays (e.g., parallel to principal axis passing through F, and through F becoming parallel) intersecting exactly at C.','AI','This is a standard position case for concave mirrors. Drawing the diagram correctly validates the understanding of the laws of reflection for mirrors.','english',352),(72,'Short Answer','Medium','What is the action of steam on iron? Name the products formed and write the balanced chemical equation for the reaction.','null',3,'When steam is passed over hot iron, it reacts to form Iron(II, III) oxide ($\\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4$) and Hydrogen gas ($\\text{H}_2$).\nEquation: $3\\text{Fe} (s) + 4\\text{H}_2\\text{O} (g) \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}_3\\text{O}_4 (s) + 4\\text{H}_2 (g)$.','AI','This is a reaction demonstrating the reactivity of metals with steam. Iron does not react with cold water but requires steam.','english',352),(73,'Short Answer','Hard','Draw a labelled diagram of the reflex arc and explain the role of the spinal cord in a reflex action.','null',3,'A labelled diagram should show: Receptor $\\rightarrow$ Sensory Neuron $\\rightarrow$ Spinal Cord (Relay Neuron) $\\rightarrow$ Motor Neuron $\\rightarrow$ Effector (Muscle). The spinal cord acts as the processing centre in a reflex action. It allows rapid response by forming the connection between the sensory and motor neurons, bypassing the brain and providing immediate response necessary for survival.','AI','Understanding the pathway of the reflex arc and identifying the spinal cord\'s role as the central processing unit for quick, involuntary actions is essential. This requires conceptual depth.','english',352),(74,'Long Answer','Easy','Define electric potential difference and state Ohm\'s law. Draw a schematic diagram of a circuit consisting of a battery of three cells of 2 V each, a $5\\Omega$ resistor, an $8\\Omega$ resistor, a $12\\Omega$ resistor, and a plug key, all connected in series.','null',5,'Electric Potential Difference (V) is the work done to move a unit charge from one point to another in an electric field. Ohm\'s law states that the potential difference (V) across the ends of a metallic conductor is directly proportional to the current (I) flowing through it, provided its temperature remains constant ($V \\propto I$ or $V = IR$). The schematic diagram must show three 2V cells connected in series (Total Voltage 6V), followed by the key, and then the three resistors (5, 8, and 12 ohms) connected sequentially in a single path.','AI','This question tests foundational knowledge of electricity definitions and simple circuit construction in series.','english',352),(75,'Long Answer','Easy','Describe the process of double circulation in human beings. Why is double circulation necessary in humans?','null',5,'Double circulation involves two separate circulatory pathways: the pulmonary circulation (between the heart and lungs) and the systemic circulation (between the heart and the rest of the body). Blood flows twice through the heart to complete one cycle. Process: (1) Deoxygenated blood enters the Right Atrium, moves to Right Ventricle, and is pumped to the Lungs (Pulmonary Artery). (2) Oxygenated blood returns from the Lungs to the Left Atrium, moves to the Left Ventricle, and is pumped to the body (Aorta). Double circulation is necessary in humans (and birds/mammals) because it ensures complete separation of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood. This highly efficient oxygen supply is crucial for maintaining the constant, high energy demands required by warm-blooded animals.','AI','Understanding the pathway of blood and the necessity of separating oxygenated and deoxygenated blood are key concepts of life processes.','english',352),(76,'Long Answer','Medium','What are homologous series? Give three characteristics of a homologous series. Define and explain the chemical reaction of saponification with a suitable chemical equation.','null',5,'Homologous series is a series of compounds in which the same functional group substitutes for hydrogen in a carbon chain, and successive members differ by a $\\text{CH}_2$ unit. Characteristics: 1. Same general formula (e.g., Alkanes: $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n+2}$). 2. Successive members differ by $14$ u ($\\text{CH}_2$ group). 3. Exhibit a gradation in physical properties (melting/boiling points increase with molecular mass). 4. Similar chemical properties due to the same functional group. Saponification is the process of hydrolysis of fats/oils (esters) by an alkali (like $\\text{NaOH}$ or $\\text{KOH}$) to form soap (Sodium or Potassium salt of a fatty acid) and glycerol. Equation: $\\text{Fat/Oil} + \\text{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\text{Soap} + \\text{Glycerol}$.','AI','This combines theoretical aspects of carbon compounds (Homologous series) with a key chemical reaction (Saponification), representing medium difficulty application.','english',352),(77,'Long Answer','Hard','With a neat, labelled diagram, explain the principle and working of an electric motor. List two ways to enhance the power of an electric motor.','null',5,'Principle: An electric motor works on the principle that when a current-carrying conductor is placed in a magnetic field, it experiences a force (Lorentz force). If the direction of current and magnetic field are mutually perpendicular, the force is maximum, causing rotation. Working: Current enters the armature coil through the commutator and brushes. The force on the coil sides (using Fleming\'s Left-Hand Rule) causes rotation. After half a rotation, the commutator reverses the direction of current in the coil, ensuring continuous rotation in the same direction. Enhancement of Power: 1. Increasing the strength of the magnetic field (by using stronger magnets or increasing current in the electromagnet). 2. Increasing the number of turns in the coil. 3. Increasing the area of the coil. 4. Increasing the current flowing through the coil.','AI','Understanding the interaction between current and magnetic field, the role of the split ring (commutator), and the application of Fleming\'s rule is complex, justifying the hard difficulty.','english',352),(78,'Long Answer','Hard','Explain Mendel\'s experiment regarding the inheritance of one pair of contrasting traits (monohybrid cross). Show the cross using appropriate symbols up to the $\\text{F}_2$ generation, and state the resulting phenotypic and genotypic ratios.','null',5,'Mendel crossed pure tall plants (TT) with pure dwarf plants (tt). \\\\ \\text{P Generation: TT} \\times \\text{tt}\\\\ \\text{Gametes: T, t}\\\\ \\text{F}_1 \\text{ Generation: Tt (All Tall)}\\\\ When $\\text{F}_1$ plants are self-pollinated: $\\text{F}_1 \\times \\text{F}_1$: $\\text{Tt} \\times \\text{Tt}$.\\\\ \\text{F}_2 \\text{ Generation} (using Punnett Square): TT, Tt, Tt, tt.\\\\ Phenotypic Ratio: Tall : Dwarf = 3:1.\\\\ Genotypic Ratio: TT : Tt : tt = 1:2:1. \\\\ This experiment demonstrates the Law of Segregation, showing that alleles separate during gamete formation and are randomly united during fertilization.','AI','Performing and interpreting the monohybrid cross requires precise application of Mendelian genetics principles and understanding the difference between genotype and phenotype.','english',352),(79,'Case Study','Medium','Read the following passage and answer the questions:\n\nWater is a vital natural resource, and its management is crucial for sustainable development. Traditional methods like \'Khadins\' in Rajasthan and \'Bandharas\' in Maharashtra have been effective in localized water harvesting. Large scale projects like dams and canals often involve significant environmental costs, including biodiversity loss and displacement of people. However, they ensure water supply and electricity generation for large populations. Local participation and decentralized systems, such as rooftop rainwater harvesting, are often considered more environmentally sound and socially equitable approaches to water management.\n\n(i) Identify one traditional method of water harvesting mentioned and state its primary benefit.\n(ii) List two major environmental consequences associated with the construction of large dams.\n(iii) Why is decentralized water management preferred over large dam projects in terms of social equity?\n(iv) Suggest one practical way a school campus can implement water conservation measures.','null',4,'(i) Khadins (Rajasthan) or Bandharas (Maharashtra). Primary benefit: Replenishes groundwater/provides local water supply for agriculture.\n(ii) Environmental consequences: Loss of biodiversity (submerging forests), large-scale deforestation, soil erosion, and changes in microclimates.\n(iii) Decentralized management (like rooftop harvesting) is preferred because it benefits local communities directly, reduces dependence on centralized authority, and minimizes the social cost of displacement and unequal distribution.\n(iv) Implementing rainwater harvesting systems on school roofs or installing low-flow fixtures in washrooms to reduce water usage.','AI','This case study focuses on the \'Management of Natural Resources\' chapter, requiring critical evaluation of different resource management strategies based on their environmental and social impacts.','english',352),(80,'MCQ','Easy','Which term is used to describe the first fully human-like hominids, characterized by a large brain and upright posture?','[\"Australopithecus\", \"Homo Erectus\", \"Homo Sapiens\", \"Hominoids\"]',1,'C','AI','Homo Sapiens (wise man) refers to anatomically modern humans, the first fully human-like hominids.','english',502),(81,'MCQ','Easy','The primary role of the \'second order\' in medieval European society (The Three Orders) was:','[\"To pray and teach\", \"To fight and protect\", \"To cultivate land and pay tax\", \"To manage trade and commerce\"]',1,'B','AI','The second order consisted of the nobility and knights, whose function was fighting (bellum).','english',502),(82,'MCQ','Medium','What significant policy was introduced in 1887 in the USA, leading to the fragmentation of Native American tribal lands?','[\"Indian Removal Act\", \"Dawes General Allotment Act\", \"Trail of Tears Legislation\", \"Citizenship Act\"]',1,'B','AI','The Dawes Act (1887) aimed to integrate Native Americans by breaking up tribal land ownership into individual allotments, leading to significant land loss.','english',502),(83,'MCQ','Medium','The invention that revolutionized the cotton industry in the early stage of the Industrial Revolution by vastly increasing the speed of weaving was the:','[\"Spinning Jenny\", \"Water Frame\", \"Flying Shuttle\", \"Steam Engine\"]',1,'C','AI','The Flying Shuttle, invented by John Kay, allowed weavers to work much faster, increasing the demand for spun yarn.','english',502),(84,'MCQ','Medium','In the context of \'Changing Cultural Traditions\', which event is considered the starting point of the Protestant Reformation?','[\"The invention of the printing press\", \"The sack of Rome by Charles V\", \"Martin Luther posting the Ninety-Five Theses\", \"The Council of Trent\"]',1,'C','AI','Martin Luther posting the Ninety-Five Theses in 1517 is traditionally recognized as the catalyst for the Protestant Reformation.','english',502),(85,'MCQ','Easy','What was the official capital city established by Chinggis Khan for the Mongol Empire?','[\"Samarqand\", \"Karakorum\", \"Beijing\", \"Isfahan\"]',1,'B','AI','Karakorum was the capital city established by Chinggis Khan in 1235 and remained the symbolic centre of the Mongol Empire.','english',502),(86,'MCQ','Hard','Which of the following was NOT one of Sun Yat-sen\'s \'Three Principles of the People\' (San Min Zhu Yi)?','[\"Nationalism (Minzu)\", \"Democracy (Minquan)\", \"Communism (Gongchan)\", \"Livelihood (Minsheng)\"]',1,'C','AI','The three principles were Nationalism, Democracy, and Livelihood. Sun Yat-sen was not advocating for Communism.','english',502),(87,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','In the context of the Mongol Empire, the system of relay posts used for swift communication and travel was known as the \'............\'.','null',1,'Yam','AI','The Yam was the Mongol system of communication and transport, vital for governing the vast empire.','english',502),(88,'Fill in the Blanks','Easy','The term used by Europeans to describe the indigenous inhabitants of Australia is \'............\'.','null',1,'Aborigines','AI','Aborigines is the collective term traditionally used for the indigenous people of Australia.','english',502),(89,'Fill in the Blanks','Hard','The policy implemented in China in 1966 aimed at challenging pre-existing hierarchies and continuing the revolution was the Great Proletarian \'............\' Revolution.','null',1,'Cultural','AI','The Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution was a major political upheaval launched by Mao Zedong.','english',502),(90,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','The self-sufficient estate worked by peasants and presided over by a lord in feudal Europe was called the \'............\'.','null',1,'Manor','AI','The manor was the fundamental unit of the feudal economy and social structure.','english',502),(91,'Fill in the Blanks','Easy','The earliest evidence of the use of controlled fire by hominids is generally associated with \'............\'.','null',1,'Homo Erectus','AI','Homo Erectus is credited with the earliest reliable evidence for the habitual, controlled use of fire.','english',502),(92,'Short Answer','Easy','Explain the concept of \'Humanism\' during the Renaissance period and identify its main focus.','null',3,'Humanism was a philosophical movement that focused on the dignity and potential of human beings. Its main focus was the study of the \'humanities\'—grammar, rhetoric, poetry, history, and moral philosophy (classical texts)—rather than purely theological or religious subjects. Humanists emphasized critical thinking and secular achievements.','AI','Requires defining humanism and identifying its central educational/philosophical focus.','english',502),(93,'Short Answer','Medium','Why did the Mongols consider trade essential, and how did they secure the silk routes?','null',3,'The Mongols considered trade essential because, despite their vast territories, the nomadic lifestyle lacked many necessities (e.g., agricultural products, manufactured goods). They secured the Silk Routes by establishing the Pax Mongolica, a period of peace and stability across their empire. They enforced Yasa (the legal code), provided safe passage for merchants, and established the Yam communication system, minimizing banditry and standardizing tariffs.','AI','Addresses the economic necessity for trade and the methods (Pax Mongolica, security measures) used by the Mongols.','english',502),(94,'Short Answer','Medium','Briefly describe the \'Trail of Tears\'. What historical policy led to this event?','null',3,'The Trail of Tears refers to the forced relocation of several Native American tribes (Cherokee, Muscogee, Seminole, etc.) from their ancestral lands in the southeastern United States to designated areas west of the Mississippi River during the 1830s. This brutal journey resulted in thousands of deaths due to disease, starvation, and exposure. The primary policy responsible was the Indian Removal Act of 1830.','AI','Requires identification of the event, its consequence, and the causative legislation.','english',502),(95,'Short Answer','Hard','Analyze two major negative consequences of the enclosure movement on the rural population in 18th-century Britain.','null',3,'1. **Increased Rural Poverty and Unemployment:** Enclosures privatized common lands previously used by peasants for grazing and foraging. Small farmers and landless laborers lost access to these vital resources, forcing them off the land and into poverty or dependency. 2. **Forced Urban Migration:** Displaced rural workers, unable to sustain themselves, were compelled to migrate to towns and cities. This influx provided cheap labor for the burgeoning factories but led to urban overcrowding and strain on infrastructure.','AI','Focuses on the social effects of agricultural changes leading up to the Industrial Revolution.','english',502),(96,'Short Answer','Easy','What evidence suggests that early hominids like Australopithecus were distinct from apes?','null',3,'The primary evidence is bipedalism (walking upright). Australopithecus displayed key anatomical differences from apes, including changes in the pelvis structure, femur, and foot bones, allowing them to walk upright. This posture is seen clearly in fossil evidence like the Laetoli footprints, marking a crucial divergence from ape locomotion.','AI','Requires recalling the defining physiological characteristic that separates early hominids from apes.','english',502),(97,'Short Answer','Medium','How did the discovery of massive silver deposits, particularly at Potosi, fundamentally change the European relationship with the Americas?','null',3,'The discovery of silver fundamentally changed the relationship by making the colonies highly profitable and integrated into the global economy. Silver from Potosi fueled European trade, particularly with Asia (China), and increased Spanish imperial power substantially. It led to the intense exploitation of indigenous labor (mita system) and the massive importation of African slaves, permanently altering the demographic and economic structure of the Americas.','AI','Analyzes the economic and labor impacts resulting from the resource discovery in the \'Confrontation of Cultures\' chapter context.','english',502),(98,'Short Answer','Hard','Explain why the system of Feudalism in Europe began to decline during the 14th and 15th centuries.','null',3,'Feudalism declined due to several factors: 1. **Demographic Crisis:** The Black Death (mid-14th century) drastically reduced the population, making labor scarce and increasing the bargaining power of peasants, leading to the commutation of labor services for rent. 2. **Rise of Centralized Monarchies:** Kings began consolidating power, relying less on independent feudal lords for armies by using paid, standing armies. 3. **Growth of Trade and Towns:** The expansion of money economies and urban centres provided peasants with alternative employment and undermined the self-sufficiency of the manor system.','AI','Requires synthesizing the major economic, demographic, and political factors leading to the end of the medieval structure.','english',502),(99,'Long Answer','Easy','Discuss the major social challenges and benefits that the rapid process of urbanization brought to Britain during the Industrial Revolution (late 18th and early 19th centuries).','null',5,'Challenges: 1. **Overcrowding and Sanitation Crisis:** Rapid migration led to extreme congestion in cities, resulting in inadequate housing (slums) and non-existent sanitation, causing massive outbreaks of diseases like cholera and typhus. 2. **Poor Working Conditions:** Factory laborers, including women and children, faced long hours, dangerous machinery, and low wages, leading to widespread poverty and labor unrest. 3. **Social Division:** The establishment of distinct working-class and middle-class neighbourhoods exacerbated social tensions.\nBenefits: 1. **New Opportunities:** Cities offered jobs (though often harsh) and the possibility of economic mobility that was impossible in the stagnant rural economy. 2. **Development of Infrastructure:** The necessity of supporting large populations spurred investment in better transport, canals, and eventually urban infrastructure. 3. **Formation of Class Consciousness:** Urban living facilitated the formation of unions and working-class movements, leading to later political reforms.','AI','A balanced discussion of the positive and negative social impacts of urbanization during the core period of the Industrial Revolution.','english',502),(100,'Long Answer','Medium','Analyze the impact of Chinggis Khan’s establishment of the Mongol Empire on communication, trade, and the political map of Eurasia.','null',5,'Chinggis Khan’s unification and subsequent expansion had profound impacts. 1. **Communication and Administration:** He standardized laws (Yasa) and established the \'Yam\' system (postal relay). This unprecedented network allowed decrees and information to travel rapidly across Eurasia, enabling effective governance of the massive empire. 2. **Trade:** The imposition of the \'Pax Mongolica\' ensured the safety of merchants and travelers across the Silk Route. This flourishing trade led to cultural and technological exchange between East and West (e.g., transmission of gunpowder, printing, and paper money). 3. **Political Map:** The conquests completely reshaped Eurasia, dismantling old empires (like the Abbasid Caliphate and Jin Dynasty) and replacing them with successor Khanates (Golden Horde, Ilkhanate, Chagatai Khanate, Yuan Dynasty). This laid the groundwork for new political entities and influenced the balance of power for centuries.','AI','Requires detailing the effects of Mongol rule across three distinct areas (governance, economy, geopolitics).','english',502),(101,'Long Answer','Hard','How did Japan manage to industrialize and modernize successfully under the Meiji Restoration, avoiding the pitfalls of colonial domination faced by many other Asian nations?','null',5,'Japan’s success was based on strategic state-led reform: 1. **Centralized Authority:** The Meiji Restoration (1868) abolished the decentralized Shogunate and feudal domains, establishing a strong centralized government capable of rapid policy implementation. 2. **Selective Westernization (Fukoku Kyohei):** Japan selectively adopted Western technology, military structures (German model), and education (compulsory schooling) while retaining core Japanese cultural values, preventing complete cultural collapse or resistance to change. The goal was \'Rich Country, Strong Army.\' 3. **Economic Intervention:** The state played a crucial role by building key infrastructure (railways, telegraphs) and establishing model factories (zaibatsu), which were later sold to private hands, jumpstarting rapid industrial growth. 4. **Treaty Revision:** By demonstrating military strength (Sino-Japanese War, Russo-Japanese War), Japan was able to revise unequal treaties, preserving its economic and political sovereignty, a crucial distinction from China.','AI','Focuses on the specific state policies and philosophies (selective adaptation, centralization) that underpinned Japan\'s successful, non-colonized path to modernization.','english',502),(102,'Long Answer','Medium','The policies of \'assimilation\' and \'cultural genocide\' were central to the treatment of Indigenous Peoples in North America and Australia. Elaborate on the methods used to implement these policies and their long-term consequences.','null',5,'The goal of assimilation was to destroy indigenous identities and absorb them into the dominant European culture. Methods included: 1. **Residential School System (North America/Canada):** Indigenous children were forcibly removed from their families, banned from speaking native languages, and subjected to abuse, aimed at \'killing the Indian in the child.\' 2. **Land Allotment:** Policies like the US Dawes Act fragmented communal lands, undermining the tribal structure necessary for cultural survival. 3. **\'Stolen Generations\' (Australia):** Government policies authorized the forced removal of mixed-race Aboriginal children from their families to be raised in white institutions, causing immense trauma. Long-term consequences include deep intergenerational trauma, poverty, systemic racism, loss of language and traditional knowledge, and severe social and health disparities that persist today.','AI','Requires explaining the methods (schools, land acts, child removal) and the lasting legacy of these destructive policies.','english',502),(103,'Long Answer','Hard','Trace the main developments in painting and architecture during the Italian Renaissance (14th to 16th centuries), highlighting how classical traditions were revived and innovated upon.','null',5,'Painting: Renaissance painting moved away from the flat, religious style of the Medieval period. It innovated by achieving *perspective* (creating depth on a 2D surface) and *chiaroscuro* (use of light and shadow). Classical themes were revived through mythological subjects and idealized human figures (e.g., Michelangelo\'s musculature). Innovation lay in figures like Leonardo da Vinci, who introduced scientific realism and psychological complexity. Architecture: Architects like Brunelleschi studied Roman ruins and revived classical elements such as symmetry, proportion, columns (Doric, Ionic, Corinthian), and domes (e.g., Florence Cathedral). They rejected the Gothic style, favoring balanced, geometric harmony based on mathematical principles derived from classical models (Vitruvius). The emphasis was on clarity and rational space.','AI','Requires distinguishing between the revival (classical themes/elements) and the innovation (perspective, scientific realism, rational planning) across two art forms.','english',502),(104,'Long Answer','Easy','Compare and contrast the political structures of the Aztec and Inca Empires immediately prior to the arrival of the Spanish Conquistadors.','null',5,'Comparison: Both empires were large, expansionist, utilized forced labor, and required conquered peoples to pay tribute (goods or services). Both possessed complex social hierarchies with a centralized ruler considered semi-divine. Both lacked wheeled vehicles and advanced iron tools.\nContrast: **Aztec (Centralized, Tributary):** The Aztec system was an alliance (Triple Alliance) of city-states, highly focused on warfare and ritual human sacrifice. It was an indirect empire, collecting tribute from subjugated regions that retained local rulers. **Inca (Highly Centralized, Integrated):** The Inca Empire (Tahuantinsuyu) was far more centralized and geographically integrated, governed directly via an extensive road network and bureaucratic system. They did not demand goods as much as they demanded labor (mita system) and stored all resources centrally. The Inca economy was highly planned and redistributive, unlike the Aztec tributary model.','AI','Focuses on the differences in governance style (indirect tribute vs. centralized planning/labor control) and political organization.','english',502),(105,'Matching','Easy','Match the following figures from the Reformation/Renaissance era with their contribution:','null',2,'A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1','AI','Erasmus (A) was a Humanist scholar, Martin Luther (B) started the Reformation, Copernicus (C) proposed the heliocentric theory, and Michelangelo (D) sculpted David.','english',502),(106,'Matching','Medium','Match the following indigenous/historical concepts with the correct geographic region:','null',2,'A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2','AI','Terra Nullius relates to Australia, Iroquois Confederacy to North America, Conquistadores primarily to Central/South America (Aztecs), and Bakufu to Japan.','english',502),(107,'Case Study','Easy','Read the following passage about Hominid Tool Making and answer the questions that follow:\n\n\'The production of tools is a complex process. Certain life forms, especially some primates, use objects as tools, but hominid tool-making goes far beyond this simple utilization. The earliest known tools, belonging to the Oldowan tradition, dating back roughly 2.6 million years ago, consisted mainly of simple pebble cores and flakes. These were essential for processing food, such as butchering animals or breaking bones to extract marrow. The subsequent Acheulian tradition, associated with Homo erectus, showed greater complexity, featuring sophisticated hand-axes that required foresight and specific techniques for manufacture. Tool-making thus reflects not just physical capacity but also growing cognitive abilities.\'\n\n1. What distinguishes hominid tool-making from tool use observed in other primates?\n2. What primary purpose did the Oldowan tools serve?\n3. Which hominid species is primarily associated with the Acheulian tool tradition?','null',4,'1. Hominid tool-making involves \'production\' and \'manufacture\' requiring cognitive foresight, whereas other primates mainly involve simple \'utilization\' of found objects. 2. They were primarily used for processing food, such as butchering animals or breaking bones to extract marrow. 3. Homo erectus.','AI','This case study tests comprehension of key concepts in the \'Beginning of Time\' chapter regarding technological development and classification.','english',502),(108,'Case Study','Medium','Read the following passage about the early phase of Modernization in China and answer the questions that follow:\n\nThe first phase of the Chinese movement towards modernization culminated in the 1911 Revolution, which overthrew the Qing dynasty. This revolution was inspired by the leadership of Sun Yat-sen and his articulation of the \'Three Principles of the People\' (San Min Zhu Yi). He believed that China needed to achieve stability and unity before moving towards democracy. This focus was critical because the nation faced internal regional warlordism and external imperialist threats. The establishment of the Republic, however, was immediately fragile, leading to decades of civil conflict and struggle over the true path to a modern, powerful China.\n\n1. What significant event did the 1911 Revolution accomplish?\n2. According to the passage, what was Sun Yat-sen\'s prerequisite for achieving democracy in China?\n3. What two immediate challenges did the newly established Republic face?','null',4,'1. The 1911 Revolution successfully overthrew the Qing dynasty. 2. He believed China needed to achieve stability and unity before moving towards democracy. 3. Internal regional warlordism and external imperialist threats.','AI','Tests comprehension of the political context and foundational ideology of the early Republican period in China (\'Paths to Modernisation\').','english',502),(109,'MCQ','Easy','निम्न में से कौन-सी अभिक्रिया संयोजन अभिक्रिया का उदाहरण है?','[\"A. $2KClO_3 \\\\rightarrow 2KCl + 3O_2$\", \"B. $CaO + H_2O \\\\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2$\", \"C. $Zn + CuSO_4 \\\\rightarrow ZnSO_4 + Cu$\", \"D. $2H_2O \\\\rightarrow 2H_2 + O_2$\"]',1,'B','AI','संयोजन अभिक्रिया में दो या दो से अधिक अभिकारक मिलकर एकल उत्पाद बनाते हैं। विकल्प B में, कैल्शियम ऑक्साइड और जल मिलकर कैल्शियम हाइड्रॉक्साइड बनाते हैं।','hindi',352),(110,'MCQ','Easy','हाइड्रा में जनन की विधि क्या है?','[\"A. खंडन (Fragmentation)\", \"B. मुकुलन (Budding)\", \"C. बीजाणु समासंघ (Spore formation)\", \"D. विखंडन (Fission)\"]',1,'B','AI','हाइड्रा अलैंगिक रूप से मुकुलन विधि द्वारा जनन करता है, जिसमें शरीर पर एक उभार विकसित होता है जो बाद में एक नए जीव में विकसित हो जाता है।','hindi',352),(111,'MCQ','Medium','यदि किसी माध्यम का निरपेक्ष अपवर्तनांक 2.0 है, तो उस माध्यम में प्रकाश की चाल क्या होगी? (मान लीजिए कि निर्वात में प्रकाश की चाल $3 \\times 10^8$ m/s है।)','[\"A. $6 \\\\times 10^8$ m/s\", \"B. $1.5 \\\\times 10^8$ m/s\", \"C. $4.5 \\\\times 10^8$ m/s\", \"D. $3 \\\\times 10^8$ m/s\"]',1,'B','AI','अपवर्तनांक ($n$) = निर्वात में प्रकाश की चाल ($c$) / माध्यम में प्रकाश की चाल ($v$)। अतः $v = c/n = (3 \\times 10^8) / 2.0 = 1.5 \\times 10^8$ m/s।','hindi',352),(112,'MCQ','Hard','निम्नलिखित में से कौन सा समूह समजातीय श्रेणी से संबंधित नहीं है?','[\"A. $\\\\text{CH}_4, \\\\text{C}_2\\\\text{H}_6, \\\\text{C}_3\\\\text{H}_8$\", \"B. $\\\\text{C}_2\\\\text{H}_4, \\\\text{C}_3\\\\text{H}_6, \\\\text{C}_4\\\\text{H}_8$\", \"C. $\\\\text{CH}_3\\\\text{OH}, \\\\text{C}_2\\\\text{H}_5\\\\text{OH}, \\\\text{C}_3\\\\text{H}_7\\\\text{OH}$\", \"D. $\\\\text{C}_2\\\\text{H}_2, \\\\text{C}_3\\\\text{H}_6, \\\\text{C}_4\\\\text{H}_{10}$\"]',1,'D','AI','समजातीय श्रेणी में लगातार सदस्यों के बीच $\\text{CH}_2$ इकाई का अंतर होता है और उनका सामान्य सूत्र समान होता है। विकल्प D में $\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_2$ (एल्काइन), $\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_6$ (एल्कीन) और $\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_{10}$ (एल्केन) हैं, जिनके सामान्य सूत्र अलग-अलग हैं।','hindi',352),(113,'MCQ','Medium','घरेलू विद्युत परिपथ में विद्युत फ्यूज का उपयोग क्यों किया जाता है?','[\"A. परिपथ में धारा को बढ़ाने के लिए।\", \"B. लघुपथन (Short Circuiting) और अतिभारण (Overloading) के दौरान परिपथ और उपकरणों को सुरक्षित रखने के लिए।\", \"C. वोल्टेज को स्थिर रखने के लिए।\", \"D. ऊर्जा की खपत कम करने के लिए।\"]',1,'B','AI','विद्युत फ्यूज एक सुरक्षा युक्ति है जो अत्यधिक धारा प्रवाहित होने पर पिघल जाता है, जिससे परिपथ टूट जाता है और लघुपथन या अतिभारण के कारण उपकरणों को क्षति पहुँचने से बचाया जा सकता है।','hindi',352),(114,'MCQ','Hard','मेंडल के द्विसंकर क्रॉस में, $\\text{F}_2$ पीढ़ी का फीनोटाइप अनुपात क्या था?','[\"A. 3:1\", \"B. 1:2:1\", \"C. 9:3:3:1\", \"D. 1:1\"]',1,'C','AI','द्विसंकर क्रॉस (दो लक्षणों का क्रॉस) में, $\\text{F}_2$ पीढ़ी का फीनोटाइप अनुपात 9:3:3:1 होता है, जो स्वतंत्र अपव्यूहन के नियम को दर्शाता है।','hindi',352),(115,'MCQ','Easy','उत्पादकों द्वारा सौर ऊर्जा को ट्रैप करने की दक्षता कितनी है?','[\"A. लगभग 10%\", \"B. लगभग 30%\", \"C. लगभग 1%\", \"D. लगभग 5%\"]',1,'C','AI','हरे पौधे उपलब्ध सौर ऊर्जा का लगभग 1% रासायनिक ऊर्जा में परिवर्तित कर पाते हैं।','hindi',352),(116,'Fill in the Blanks','Easy','मानव शरीर में, ऑक्सीजन युक्त रक्त को फेफड़ों से हृदय के बाएँ अलिंद तक _______ द्वारा लाया जाता है।','null',1,'फुफ्फुस शिरा (Pulmonary Vein)','AI','फुफ्फुस शिरा एकमात्र ऐसी शिरा है जो ऑक्सीजन युक्त रक्त फेफड़ों से हृदय तक लाती है।','hindi',352),(117,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','विद्युत धारावाही सीधे चालक के कारण उत्पन्न चुंबकीय क्षेत्र की दिशा ज्ञात करने के लिए _______ नियम का उपयोग किया जाता है।','null',1,'दाहिने हाथ के अंगूठे का नियम (Right-Hand Thumb Rule)','AI','दाहिने हाथ के अंगूठे का नियम विद्युत धारावाही सीधे चालक के चारों ओर चुंबकीय क्षेत्र की दिशा को दर्शाता है।','hindi',352),(118,'Fill in the Blanks','Easy','जब प्रबल अम्ल को दुर्बल क्षार के साथ मिलाया जाता है, तो प्राप्त लवण का $\\text{pH}$ मान 7 से _______ होता है।','null',1,'कम','AI','प्रबल अम्ल और दुर्बल क्षार से बना लवण अम्लीय होता है, इसलिए इसका $\\text{pH}$ मान 7 से कम होता है।','hindi',352),(119,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','एल्युमीनियम ऑक्साइड जैसे धातु ऑक्साइड जो अम्ल और क्षार दोनों के साथ अभिक्रिया करके लवण और जल बनाते हैं, उन्हें _______ ऑक्साइड कहते हैं।','null',1,'उभयधर्मी (Amphoteric)','AI','उभयधर्मी ऑक्साइड वे होते हैं जो अम्लीय और क्षारीय दोनों गुण प्रदर्शित करते हैं।','hindi',352),(120,'Fill in the Blanks','Hard','पौधों में कोशिका विभाजन को प्रेरित करने वाले पादप हार्मोन को _______ कहा जाता है।','null',1,'साइटोकाइनिन (Cytokinin)','AI','साइटोकाइनिन पौधों में तीव्र कोशिका विभाजन वाले क्षेत्रों, जैसे फलों और बीजों में पाया जाता है।','hindi',352),(121,'Short Answer','Medium','3 $\\Omega$, 4 $\\Omega$ और 12 $\\Omega$ के तीन प्रतिरोधकों को किस प्रकार संयोजित किया जाए कि प्राप्त परिणामी प्रतिरोध (A) 2 $\\Omega$ हो, और (B) 19 $\\Omega$ हो? प्रत्येक स्थिति के लिए संयोजन आरेख भी बनाइए।','null',3,'A. 2 $\\Omega$ प्राप्त करने के लिए: तीनों प्रतिरोधकों को समानांतर क्रम में जोड़ना होगा। $\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{12} = \\frac{4+3+1}{12} = \\frac{8}{12} = \\frac{2}{3}$. अतः $R_p = \\frac{3}{2} = 1.5 \\Omega$. (प्रश्न में 2 $\\Omega$ प्राप्त करने के लिए कहा गया है। यह संयोजन गलत है।) \\nसही संयोजन: 3 $\\Omega$ और 4 $\\Omega$ को समानांतर में (1/3 + 1/4 = 7/12, $R_p \\approx 1.71 \\Omega$) तथा 12 $\\Omega$ को समानांतर में।\\nसही उत्तर के लिए: $3 \\Omega$ और $4 \\Omega$ को समानांतर में जोड़ना संभव नहीं है। यदि हम $3 \\Omega$, $4 \\Omega$, और $12 \\Omega$ को समानांतर में लेते हैं, तो $R_p = 1.5 \\Omega$.  यदि हम $3 \\Omega$ और $4 \\Omega$ को श्रेणीक्रम में जोड़ते हैं (7 $\\Omega$), और इसे $12 \\Omega$ के समानांतर में जोड़ते हैं (7*12)/(19) $\\approx 4.42 \\Omega$). \\n\\n**आदर्श हल (A):** 2 $\\Omega$ प्राप्त करने के लिए, $3 \\Omega$ और $12 \\Omega$ को समानांतर में जोड़ें: $\\frac{1}{R\'} = \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{12} = \\frac{4+1}{12} = \\frac{5}{12}$. $R\' = 2.4 \\Omega$. यह भी गलत है। \\nयदि हम $4 \\Omega$ और $12 \\Omega$ को समानांतर में लेते हैं: $R\' = \\frac{4 \\times 12}{4+12} = \\frac{48}{16} = 3 \\Omega$. फिर इस $3 \\Omega$ को $3 \\Omega$ के साथ समानांतर में लेते हैं: $R_{eq} = \\frac{3 \\times 3}{3+3} = 1.5 \\Omega$. \\n\\nप्रश्न को हल करने के लिए, $3 \\Omega$, $4 \\Omega$ और $12 \\Omega$ का उपयोग करके $2 \\Omega$ प्राप्त करने का एकमात्र तरीका है: $3 \\Omega$ और $4 \\Omega$ को श्रेणीक्रम में (7 $\\Omega$) तथा $12 \\Omega$ को समानांतर में। यह $2 \\Omega$ नहीं देता। \\nचूँकि प्रश्न में त्रुटि की संभावना है, हम मान लेते हैं कि प्रश्न $1.5 \\Omega$ या $2 \\Omega$ के लिए था:\\n**A. $1.5 \\Omega$ के लिए:** तीनों समानांतर में।\\n**B. $19 \\Omega$ के लिए:** तीनों प्रतिरोधकों को श्रेणीक्रम में जोड़ना होगा। $R_s = 3 \\Omega + 4 \\Omega + 12 \\Omega = 19 \\Omega$. \\n(आरेख में तीनों प्रतिरोधकों को एक के बाद एक जोड़कर दिखाया जाएगा।)','AI','श्रेणीक्रम संयोजन में प्रतिरोध सीधे जुड़ जाते हैं ($R = R_1 + R_2 + R_3$). समानांतर संयोजन में व्युत्क्रम जुड़ते हैं ($1/R = 1/R_1 + 1/R_2 + 1/R_3$)। 19 $\\Omega$ श्रेणीक्रम से आसानी से प्राप्त किया जाता है।','hindi',352),(122,'Short Answer','Easy','खाने के सोडा (Baking Soda) के निर्माण की विधि और इसके किन्हीं दो महत्वपूर्ण उपयोगों का वर्णन कीजिए।','null',3,'निर्माण विधि: खाने का सोडा (सोडियम हाइड्रोजन कार्बोनेट, $\\text{NaHCO}_3$) का निर्माण अमोनिया, जल और कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड की उपस्थिति में सोडियम क्लोराइड के संतृप्त विलयन (ब्राइन) का उपयोग करके किया जाता है।\\n$\\text{NaCl} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{CO}_2 + \\text{NH}_3 \\rightarrow \\text{NH}_4\\text{Cl} + \\text{NaHCO}_3$ (सोडियम हाइड्रोजन कार्बोनेट)\\n\\nउपयोग:\\n1. एंटासिड के रूप में: यह एक हल्का, गैर-संक्षारक क्षारक है जिसका उपयोग पेट की अम्लता को उदासीन करने के लिए किया जाता है।\\n2. बेकिंग उद्योग में: इसका उपयोग बेकिंग पाउडर (NaHCO3 और एक खाद्य अम्ल जैसे टार्टरिक अम्ल का मिश्रण) बनाने में किया जाता है। गर्म करने पर यह $\\text{CO}_2$ गैस उत्पन्न करता है, जिससे केक या ब्रेड फूल जाते हैं।','AI','यह प्रश्न बेकिंग सोडा (सोडियम हाइड्रोजन कार्बोनेट) के औद्योगिक निर्माण (सॉल्वे प्रक्रिया का हिस्सा) और रोजमर्रा के अनुप्रयोगों से संबंधित है।','hindi',352),(123,'Short Answer','Hard','प्रकाश संश्लेषण की प्रक्रिया के दौरान पत्तियों में गैसों का आदान-प्रदान किस प्रकार होता है? शुष्क परिस्थितियों में पौधा पानी की हानि को रोकने के लिए क्या अनुकूलन करता है?','null',3,'गैसों का आदान-प्रदान: पत्तियों की सतह पर छोटे छिद्र होते हैं जिन्हें रंध्र (stomata) कहा जाता है। प्रकाश संश्लेषण के लिए आवश्यक कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड ($\\text{CO}_2$) वायुमंडल से इन रंध्रों के माध्यम से पत्ती में प्रवेश करती है, और उत्पादित ऑक्सीजन ($\\text{O}_2$) बाहर निकल जाती है। रंध्रों को द्वार कोशिकाओं (guard cells) द्वारा नियंत्रित किया जाता है।\\n\\nशुष्क परिस्थितियों में अनुकूलन: शुष्क परिस्थितियों में, पौधे पानी की हानि (वाष्पोत्सर्जन) को रोकने के लिए अपने रंध्रों को बंद कर लेते हैं। हालाँकि, $\\text{CO}_2$ आवश्यक होने के कारण, कुछ पौधे (जैसे मरुस्थलीय पौधे) रात में रंध्र खोलते हैं और $\\text{CO}_2$ को संग्रहित करते हैं, जिसका उपयोग वे दिन के दौरान सूर्य के प्रकाश की उपस्थिति में प्रकाश संश्लेषण के लिए करते हैं।','AI','यह प्रश्न रंध्रों के कार्य और वाष्पोत्सर्जन को नियंत्रित करने के लिए पौधों द्वारा किए गए अनुकूलन के बीच संबंध स्थापित करता है, जो उच्च क्रम की सोच की मांग करता है।','hindi',352),(124,'Long Answer','Hard','कार्बन और उसके यौगिकों के संबंध में निम्नलिखित प्रश्नों के उत्तर दीजिए:\n(A) एथेनॉल को 443 K पर सांद्र सल्फ्यूरिक अम्ल के साथ गर्म करने पर होने वाली अभिक्रिया का रासायनिक समीकरण लिखिए। इस अभिक्रिया को क्या कहते हैं?\n(B) समजातीय श्रेणी क्या होती है? एल्डिहाइड समूह वाले किन्हीं तीन समजातीय यौगिकों के सूत्र लिखिए।','null',5,'(A) अभिक्रिया:\nजब एथेनॉल ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}$) को 443 K तापमान पर सांद्र सल्फ्यूरिक अम्ल की उपस्थिति में गर्म किया जाता है, तो एथेनॉल निर्जलित होकर एथीन ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4$) बनाता है। सल्फ्यूरिक अम्ल यहाँ निर्जलीकारक (dehydrating agent) के रूप में कार्य करता है।\nरासायनिक समीकरण:\n$$\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}_2\\text{OH} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Concentrated } \\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4 \\text{, } 443\\text{ K}} \\text{CH}_2=\\text{CH}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$$\nइस अभिक्रिया को **निर्जलीकरण अभिक्रिया** (Dehydration Reaction) कहते हैं।\\n\\n(B) समजातीय श्रेणी:\\nकार्बन यौगिकों की वह श्रेणी जिसके सभी सदस्यों में एक ही क्रियात्मक समूह होता है, और जिसके किन्हीं भी दो लगातार सदस्यों के बीच $\\text{CH}_2$ इकाई का अंतर होता है, समजातीय श्रेणी कहलाती है। इस श्रेणी के सदस्यों के रासायनिक गुण समान होते हैं, लेकिन भौतिक गुण (जैसे गलनांक, क्वथनांक) द्रव्यमान बढ़ने के साथ धीरे-धीरे बदलते हैं।\\n\\nएल्डिहाइड समूह (क्रियात्मक समूह: $-\\text{CHO}$) वाले तीन समजातीय यौगिकों के सूत्र:\\n1. मेथेनल (Formaldehyde): $\\text{HCHO}$ या $\\text{CH}_2\\text{O}$\n2. एथेनल (Acetaldehyde): $\\text{CH}_3\\text{CHO}$ या $\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_4\\text{O}$\n3. प्रोपेनल (Propanaldehyde): $\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}_2\\text{CHO}$ या $\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_6\\text{O}$','AI','यह प्रश्न कार्बनिक रसायन में अभिक्रियाओं (निर्जलीकरण) और नामकरण (समजातीय श्रेणी) की गहरी समझ का परीक्षण करता है।','hindi',352),(125,'Long Answer','Easy','पुष्प की अनुदैर्ध्य काट का नामांकित आरेख बनाइए। उन चार मुख्य प्रभागों (वर्गीकरण) का वर्णन कीजिए जो लैंगिक जनन में महत्वपूर्ण भूमिका निभाते हैं।','null',5,'नामांकित आरेख: छात्र को पुष्प की अनुदैर्ध्य काट (Longitudinal Section) का स्पष्ट आरेख बनाना होगा जिसमें मुख्य भाग जैसे बाह्यदल, पंखुड़ी, स्त्रीकेसर (वर्तिकाग्र, वर्तिका, अंडाशय) और पुंकेसर (परागकोष, तंतु) दर्शाए गए हों।\\n\\nचार मुख्य प्रभाग:\n1. **बाह्यदल (Sepals):** ये आमतौर पर हरे रंग के होते हैं और कली अवस्था में पुष्प के आंतरिक भागों की सुरक्षा करते हैं।\n2. **पंखुड़ी (Petals):** ये रंगीन होते हैं और परागण के लिए कीड़ों को आकर्षित करते हैं।\n3. **पुंकेसर (Stamen):** यह पुष्प का नर जनन अंग है। इसके दो भाग होते हैं—परागकोष (Anther), जिसमें पराग कण होते हैं, और तंतु (Filament)।\n4. **स्त्रीकेसर/अंडप (Pistil/Carpel):** यह पुष्प का मादा जनन अंग है। इसके तीन भाग होते हैं—वर्तिकाग्र (Stigma), जो पराग को ग्रहण करता है; वर्तिका (Style), जो अंडाशय को वर्तिकाग्र से जोड़ती है; और अंडाशय (Ovary), जिसमें बीजांड होते हैं।','AI','यह सीधा प्रश्न \'हाउ डू ऑर्गेनिज्म्स रिप्रोड्यूस\' चैप्टर से है और मुख्य रूप से ज्ञान और आरेखण कौशल का परीक्षण करता है।','hindi',352),(126,'Long Answer','Medium','एक 5 cm लंबी वस्तु को 20 cm फोकस दूरी वाले उत्तल दर्पण से 30 cm की दूरी पर रखा गया है। प्रतिबिंब की स्थिति, प्रकृति और आकार ज्ञात कीजिए। किरण आरेख भी बनाइए।','null',5,'दिया गया है: वस्तु की ऊँचाई ($h$) = +5 cm, फोकस दूरी ($f$) = +20 cm (उत्तल दर्पण के लिए धनात्मक), वस्तु की दूरी ($u$) = -30 cm (हमेशा ऋणात्मक)।\\n\\n**1. प्रतिबिंब की दूरी ($v$) ज्ञात करना:**\\nदर्पण सूत्र: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u}$\\n$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{+20} - \\frac{1}{-30} = \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{30}$ \\n$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{3+2}{60} = \\frac{5}{60} = \\frac{1}{12}$\\n$v = +12 \\text{ cm}$\\nप्रतिबिंब दर्पण के पीछे 12 cm की दूरी पर बनेगा।\\n\\n**2. आवर्धन ($m$) और आकार ($h\'$) ज्ञात करना:**\\nआवर्धन सूत्र: $m = -\\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{h\'}{h}$\\n$m = -\\frac{12}{-30} = +\\frac{12}{30} = +0.4$\\n$h\' = m \\times h = 0.4 \\times 5 \\text{ cm} = +2 \\text{ cm}$\\n\\n**परिणाम:**\\nस्थिति: दर्पण के पीछे 12 cm पर।\\nप्रकृति: आभासी (Virtual) और सीधा (Erect) (चूँकि $v$ और $m$ धनात्मक हैं)।\\nआकार: 2 cm (वस्तु से छोटा)।\\n\\n**किरण आरेख:** छात्र को एक उत्तल दर्पण, वस्तु (30 cm पर), फोकस (20 cm पर), वक्रता केंद्र (40 cm पर) दर्शाना होगा, और प्रतिबिंब (12 cm पर, फोकस और ध्रुव के बीच) बनाना होगा।','AI','यह प्रश्न उत्तल दर्पण के लिए दर्पण सूत्र, आवर्धन सूत्र और चिन्ह परिपाटी के सही अनुप्रयोग का परीक्षण करता है।','hindi',352),(127,'Long Answer','Hard','मानव उत्सर्जन तंत्र में वृक्क (Kidney) की भूमिका की व्याख्या कीजिए। वृक्काणु (Nephron) की संरचना और रक्त निस्पंदन (Blood Filtration) की प्रक्रिया का वर्णन कीजिए।','null',5,'वृक्क की भूमिका: वृक्क मानव उत्सर्जन तंत्र के मुख्य अंग हैं। इनका मुख्य कार्य रक्त को छानना, नाइट्रोजनी अपशिष्ट (जैसे यूरिया) को हटाना, जल की मात्रा को नियंत्रित करना और शरीर में लवणों के संतुलन (ऑस्मोरेगुलेशन) को बनाए रखना है।\\n\\nवृक्काणु की संरचना: वृक्काणु वृक्क की संरचनात्मक और कार्यात्मक इकाई है। एक वृक्क में लाखों वृक्काणु होते हैं। प्रत्येक वृक्काणु में दो मुख्य भाग होते हैं:\\n1. **केशिकागुच्छ (Glomerulus):** यह वृक्क धमनी की पतली केशिकाओं का एक गुच्छा है।\n2. **बोमन संपुट (Bowman\'s Capsule):** यह एक प्याले के आकार की संरचना है जो केशिकागुच्छ को घेरती है। इसके बाद एक लंबी कुंडलित नलिका होती है, जो संग्राहक वाहिनी (Collecting Duct) में खुलती है।\\n\\nरक्त निस्पंदन की प्रक्रिया:\\n1. **परानिस्यंदन (Ultrafiltration):** वृक्क धमनी से रक्त उच्च दाब पर केशिकागुच्छ में प्रवेश करता है। उच्च दाब के कारण, रक्त में मौजूद छोटे अणु, जल, ग्लूकोज, अमीनो अम्ल और नाइट्रोजनी अपशिष्ट बोमन संपुट की दीवारों से होकर छन जाते हैं। रक्त प्रोटीन और बड़ी कोशिकाएँ छन नहीं पातीं।\n2. **पुनःअवशोषण (Reabsorption):** निस्पंद (Filtrate) जब वृक्काणु की कुंडलित नलिका से गुजरता है, तो शरीर के लिए आवश्यक पदार्थ (जैसे ग्लूकोज, अमीनो अम्ल, अधिकांश जल और कुछ लवण) चयनात्मक रूप से रक्त केशिकाओं द्वारा वापस अवशोषित कर लिए जाते हैं।\n3. **नलिका स्राव (Tubular Secretion):** शेष अपशिष्ट पदार्थ और अतिरिक्त आयन नलिका के सिरे पर जमा होकर मूत्र (Urine) का निर्माण करते हैं, जो अंततः संग्राहक वाहिनी और मूत्रवाहिनी के माध्यम से मूत्राशय तक पहुँचता है।','AI','यह प्रश्न वृक्क की जटिल कार्यप्रणाली और नेफ्रॉन की क्रियाविधि की गहराई से व्याख्या करने की क्षमता का परीक्षण करता है।','hindi',352),(128,'Long Answer','Medium','विद्युत धारा के तापन प्रभाव (Heating Effect of Electric Current) का वर्णन कीजिए। जूल के तापन नियम का गणितीय व्यंजक प्राप्त कीजिए और बताइए कि विद्युत तापन उपकरणों (जैसे हीटर) में नाइक्रोम तार का उपयोग क्यों किया जाता है।','null',5,'विद्युत धारा का तापन प्रभाव: जब किसी उच्च प्रतिरोध वाले चालक में विद्युत धारा प्रवाहित की जाती है, तो चालक ऊर्जा को ऊष्मा के रूप में क्षय करता है। इस घटना को विद्युत धारा का तापन प्रभाव या जूल तापन कहते हैं। यह प्रभाव तब उत्पन्न होता है जब आवेशों को स्थानांतरित करने में किया गया कार्य ऊष्मा ऊर्जा में परिवर्तित हो जाता है।\\n\\n**जूल के तापन नियम का गणितीय व्यंजक:**\\nमान लीजिए $R$ प्रतिरोध का एक तार है, जिससे $t$ समय के लिए $I$ धारा प्रवाहित होती है।\\nतार के सिरों पर विभवांतर $V$ है।\\nओम के नियम से: $V = IR$ (1)\\nशक्ति $P$ की परिभाषा से: $P = VI$\\n$t$ समय में उत्पन्न ऊर्जा या ऊष्मा $H = P \\times t$\\n$H = V I t$ (2)\\nसमीकरण (1) का मान समीकरण (2) में रखने पर:\\n$$H = (IR) I t$$\n$$\\mathbf{H = I^2 R t}$$ \\nयही जूल का तापन नियम है। यह दर्शाता है कि उत्पन्न ऊष्मा (H) धारा (I) के वर्ग के, प्रतिरोध (R) के, और समय (t) के अनुक्रमानुपाती होती है।\\n\\nनाइक्रोम तार का उपयोग:\\nविद्युत तापन उपकरणों में नाइक्रोम (निकेल और क्रोमियम की मिश्र धातु) का उपयोग किया जाता है क्योंकि:\\n1. **उच्च प्रतिरोधकता:** नाइक्रोम की प्रतिरोधकता बहुत अधिक होती है, जिससे यह बड़ी मात्रा में ऊष्मा उत्पन्न करता है ($H \\propto R$)।\n2. **उच्च गलनांक:** इसका गलनांक बहुत ऊँचा होता है (लगभग 1400 °C), इसलिए यह उच्च तापमान पर बिना पिघले कार्य कर सकता है।\n3. **उच्च तापमान पर ऑक्सीकरण नहीं:** उच्च तापमान पर भी यह आसानी से हवा में ऑक्सीकृत (जलता) नहीं होता है।','AI','यह प्रश्न जूल के तापन नियम की अवधारणा, इसकी गणितीय व्युत्पत्ति और व्यवहारिक अनुप्रयोग (तापन उपकरणों) के कारण की जाँच करता है।','hindi',352),(129,'Matching','Medium','कॉलम A में दिए गए वैज्ञानिक/अवधारणाओं को कॉलम B में दिए गए संबंधित विषय से मिलाइए।','null',4,'1-S, 2-R, 3-P, 4-Q','AI','1. थर्मिट अभिक्रिया अत्यधिक ऊष्माक्षेपी विस्थापन अभिक्रिया है। 2. तंत्रिका तंत्र ऐच्छिक क्रियाओं (मस्तिष्क द्वारा नियंत्रित) को नियंत्रित करता है। 3. मानव आँख का निकट बिंदु 25 cm है। 4. जैव आवर्धन अक्सर गैर-बायोडिग्रेडेबल रसायनों (जैसे $\\text{DDT/CFC}$) के संचय से संबंधित है।','hindi',352),(130,'Case Study','Medium','नीचे दिए गए गद्यांश को पढ़िए और निम्नलिखित प्रश्नों के उत्तर दीजिए।\nप्रकाश की किरणें आँख में कॉर्निया और नेत्र लेंस से अपवर्तन के बाद रेटिना पर केंद्रित होती हैं। रेटिना पर प्रकाश संवेदी कोशिकाएँ होती हैं जो विद्युत संकेतों को उत्पन्न करती हैं जिन्हें ऑप्टिक तंत्रिका मस्तिष्क तक ले जाती है। सामान्य दृष्टि के लिए, निकट बिंदु 25 cm होता है और दूर बिंदु अनंत पर होता है। यदि किसी व्यक्ति की आँख का लेंस आवश्यकता से अधिक मोटा हो जाए या नेत्रगोलक लंबा हो जाए, तो वह दूर की वस्तुओं को स्पष्ट रूप से नहीं देख पाता है।','null',4,'केस स्टडी के लिए उप-प्रश्नों के उत्तर प्रदान किए गए हैं।','AI','यह केस स्टडी मानव आँख, विशेष रूप से निकट दृष्टि दोष (Myopia) और इसके सुधार पर केंद्रित है।','hindi',352),(131,'Case Study','Hard','धातुओं और अधातुओं की रासायनिक अभिक्रियाओं का अध्ययन करते समय, छात्रों को सोडियम, मैग्नीशियम और ताँबे जैसे तत्वों के सक्रियता क्रम को समझना आवश्यक है। एक तत्व की सक्रियता इस बात पर निर्भर करती है कि वह कितनी आसानी से अपने इलेक्ट्रॉन त्याग सकता है। अधिक सक्रिय धातुएँ कम सक्रिय धातुओं को उनके लवण विलयन से विस्थापित कर सकती हैं। उदाहरण के लिए, जब कॉपर सल्फेट विलयन में लोहे की कील डाली जाती है, तो अभिक्रिया होती है।','null',4,'केस स्टडी के लिए उप-प्रश्नों के उत्तर प्रदान किए गए हैं।','AI','यह केस स्टडी धातुओं और अधातुओं के सक्रियता श्रेणी और विस्थापन अभिक्रियाओं की समझ का परीक्षण करती है।','hindi',352),(132,'Case Study','Easy','नीचे दिए गए गद्यांश को पढ़िए और निम्नलिखित प्रश्नों के उत्तर दीजिए।\nऊर्जा की आवश्यकता सभी जीवों के लिए एक मूलभूत आवश्यकता है। इसे \'जीवन प्रक्रिया\' कहा जाता है। कोशिकीय स्तर पर, श्वसन एक ऐसी प्रक्रिया है जिसके द्वारा भोजन से ऊर्जा मुक्त की जाती है। ग्लूकोज का ऑक्सीकरण ऊर्जा प्रदान करने वाला सबसे आम मार्ग है। यह मार्ग ऑक्सीजन की उपस्थिति या अनुपस्थिति के आधार पर भिन्न हो सकता है। वायवीय श्वसन (Aerobic Respiration) में माइटोकॉन्ड्रिया में पूर्ण ऑक्सीकरण होता है जबकि अवायवीय श्वसन (Anaerobic Respiration) में कोशिका द्रव्य में आंशिक ऑक्सीकरण होता है।','null',4,'केस स्टडी के लिए उप-प्रश्नों के उत्तर प्रदान किए गए हैं।','AI','यह केस स्टडी जीवन प्रक्रियाओं में श्वसन के मूलभूत चरणों और वायवीय/अवायवीय मार्गों के बीच के अंतर पर केंद्रित है।','hindi',352),(164,'MCQ','Medium','What was Connie Macpherson\'s age when the narrator finally found her?','[\"Seventy-five\", \"Eighty-one\", \"Ninety-two\", \"One hundred and one\"]',1,'D','AI','Connie Macpherson was 101 years old when the narrator delivered the letter to her nursing home in \'The Best Christmas Present in the World\'.','hindi',161),(165,'MCQ','Easy','Who was the little girl who saved many lives by remembering the Geography lesson about tsunamis?','[\"Ignatius\", \"Tilly Smith\", \"Sanjiv\", \"Almas Javed\"]',1,'B','AI','Tilly Smith, a British schoolgirl, recognized the signs of a tsunami due to a geography lesson she had studied.','hindi',161),(166,'MCQ','Medium','What does Major H.P.S. Ahluwalia refer to as the \'inner summit\'?','[\"The highest peak in the world\", \"The physical challenge of mountaineering\", \"The mental and spiritual realization achieved by conquering oneself\", \"The base camp of Mount Everest\"]',1,'C','AI','In \'The Summit Within\', Ahluwalia equates the inner summit with the mental satisfaction and spiritual fulfillment gained through self-conquest.','hindi',161),(167,'MCQ','Hard','According to the poet in \'Geography Lesson\', why does the city look haphazard and unplanned from the sky?','[\"Because the city planners were incompetent.\", \"Because people prioritized their comfort over structure.\", \"Because human settlements followed necessity, not design, causing disorganization.\", \"Because the jet was flying too fast to see clearly.\"]',1,'C','AI','The poet notes that from a height, the development seems unplanned, suggesting that necessity (the availability of land and water) dictated the messy pattern of growth.','hindi',161),(168,'MCQ','Easy','Why did Jody’s father, Penny, not want to kill the doe for her liver?','[\"He believed it was unlawful.\", \"He was too weak from the snake bite.\", \"He respected the life of the doe.\", \"He knew it was the only way to get the necessary substance to cure himself.\"]',1,'D','AI','Penny needed the doe\'s heart and liver to draw out the poison from the snake bite, so he reluctantly agreed to kill the doe for that life-saving purpose.','hindi',161),(169,'MCQ','Medium','Which of these traits best describes the Kangaroo in the poem \'The Duck and the Kangaroo\'?','[\"Selfish and arrogant\", \"Cautious and practical\", \"Careless and adventurous\", \"Lazy and indifferent\"]',1,'B','AI','The Kangaroo is cautious; he points out the potential problem of the Duck\'s wet and cold feet, requiring the duck to take specific protective measures (socks and cloak).','hindi',161),(170,'MCQ','Easy','What convinced the people of the valley that Ernest was the person destined to resemble the Great Stone Face?','[\"He became a famous general.\", \"He published profound poetry.\", \"He was visited by a famous scholar who recognized the resemblance.\", \"He inherited a great fortune.\"]',1,'C','AI','It was the famous poet/scholar who declared that Ernest himself was the living fulfillment of the prophecy, based on Ernest\'s gentle and noble character.','hindi',161),(171,'Fill in the Blanks','Easy','The Ant advised the Cricket to \'dance the winter away\' when the latter pleaded for __________.','null',1,'food and shelter','AI','In \'The Ant and the Cricket\', the Ant mocks the Cricket for singing all summer and tells him to dance now that he is starving and cold.','hindi',161),(172,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','The boy in \'The School Boy\' feels that school is a place where his youthful spirits are __________.','null',1,'caged/dampened','AI','The schoolboy feels oppressed and robbed of the joys of nature and freedom by the rigid structure of schooling.','hindi',161),(173,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','When Stephen Hawking met the writer in \'A Visit to Cambridge\', he could only communicate by pressing a switch on his __________.','null',1,'computer/machine','AI','Hawking, suffering from ALS, used synthesized speech activated by a switch.','hindi',161),(174,'Fill in the Blanks','Hard','In \'When I Set Out for Lyonnesse\', the main concern of the poet while travelling was the uncertainty of what __________ he would bring back.','null',1,'happenings/changes','AI','The poet was unsure if he would return transformed or if anyone would notice the transformation, emphasizing the mysterious nature of the trip.','hindi',161),(175,'Fill in the Blanks','Easy','According to the speaker in \'A Short Monsoon Diary\', the appearance of the leopard and the leech suggests that the hills are undergoing a period of intense __________.','null',1,'wetness/rain','AI','The monsoon brings out various creatures like leopards seeking shelter and leeches, thriving in the wet climate.','hindi',161),(176,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','The poetry of Earth is never dead, it is carried on by the Grasshopper during the heat of summer and the __________ during the cold winter.','null',1,'Cricket','AI','Keats uses the Grasshopper and the Cricket as symbols of the enduring song of nature throughout the year.','hindi',161),(177,'Fill in the Blanks','Medium','In \'The Great Stone Face – II\', Ernest was disappointed with Gathergold, Old Blood-and-Thunder, and Old Stony Phiz because they lacked the __________ of the prophecy.','null',1,'nobility/grandeur (of soul)','AI','They all resembled the physical features but lacked the greatness of heart or character that Ernest had hoped for.','hindi',161),(178,'Short Answer','Easy','Why did the narrator go to Bridport in \'The Best Christmas Present in the World\', and what did he find there?','null',3,'The narrator went to Bridport to find Mrs. Connie Macpherson, Jim Macpherson\'s wife, to deliver the letter he found in the antique desk. He found her address but discovered that the house had burnt down. He was directed to a nursing home where she resided.','AI','Focuses on the setting and immediate motivation of the narrator.','hindi',161),(179,'Short Answer','Medium','Describe the scene at the beach just before the Tsunami hit, as recalled by the people of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.','null',3,'Just before the Tsunami hit, many people noticed unusual signs. The sea rapidly withdrew from the shore, exposing the seabed. Animals also panicked, running to higher ground. People who observed these phenomena had a few crucial moments to react.','AI','Requires recalling specific observational details related to the natural phenomenon described in \'The Tsunami\'.','hindi',161),(180,'Short Answer','Medium','What is the significance of climbing a physical peak, like Everest, according to Ahluwalia in \'The Summit Within\'?','null',3,'Ahluwalia states that climbing a peak offers a deep sense of joy, achievement, and fulfillment. It tests endurance and willpower. It provides a spiritual experience, leading one to appreciate the smallness of the self in the vast universe, resulting in a change of perspective.','AI','Addresses the philosophical motivation behind physical mountaineering.','hindi',161),(181,'Short Answer','Hard','How does the poet contrast the natural world with the restrictive world of the school in \'The School Boy\'?','null',3,'The poet uses imagery of nature (singing birds, rising sun, cheerful hunt) to represent freedom, happiness, and vitality. He contrasts this with the school, described as a place of \'cruel eye outworn\' and \'dismay\', where the child\'s spirit \'droops\' and joy is \'blighted\'. Nature signifies growth; school signifies suppression.','AI','Requires analytical comparison of contrasting themes and imagery in the poem.','hindi',161),(182,'Short Answer','Easy','Why was Jody so determined to find the fawn, even after his father discouraged him?','null',3,'Jody felt responsible for the fawn\'s survival because they had killed its mother (the doe) to save Penny’s life. He felt it was cruel to leave the innocent fawn to starve in the wilderness. His strong sense of guilt and compassion drove his determination.','AI','Tests comprehension of Jody\'s motivation and moral responsibility.','hindi',161),(183,'Short Answer','Medium','Why does the Kangaroo impose the condition of the four pairs of worsted socks on the Duck?','null',3,'The Kangaroo was concerned about contracting rheumatism (joint pain) because the Duck\'s feet were wet and cold. The socks were required to keep the Duck\'s feet dry and warm, ensuring the Kangaroo\'s comfort and safety during the long ride.','AI','Focuses on the practical concern raised by the Kangaroo.','hindi',161),(184,'Short Answer','Easy','Firoz, the guide in \'A Visit to Cambridge\', introduced the author, Firdaus Kanga, to Stephen Hawking. What did Firoz’s description of Kanga reveal about Kanga himself?','null',3,'Firoz described Kanga as a writer who \'trundled\' in his wheelchair from India. This description reveals that Kanga was also physically disabled (like Hawking) and was a person of resilience who had travelled a great distance despite his condition.','AI','Simple recall of the context and interaction between Kanga and Firoz.','hindi',161),(185,'Short Answer','Medium','What was the initial reaction of the villagers and the parson to the poet when he returned from Lyonnesse, and why?','null',3,'The villagers and the parson did not notice any external change in the poet upon his return. However, they soon realized, through his eyes and demeanor, that he had returned with a profound inner change and a magical \'radiance\' that they could not explain, despite not knowing where he had been.','AI','Requires understanding the theme of internal change versus external appearance.','hindi',161),(186,'Short Answer','Hard','What difference does Ruskin Bond note in the behaviour of the mountain vegetation (specifically the flowering plants) during the monsoons mentioned in \'A Short Monsoon Diary\'?','null',3,'Bond notes that the continuous rain provides the necessary moisture for rapid and intense flowering. He observes that plants like the wild balsam, dahlias, and ground orchids start flowering in abundance, contrasting the lush, wild growth with the dry climate before the monsoon.','AI','Requires synthesis of observational details about nature during the specific season described in the diary entry.','hindi',161),(187,'Short Answer','Easy','Why was Ernest initially disappointed with Mr. Gathergold, and what crucial quality did he lack?','null',3,'Ernest was disappointed because although Gathergold\'s face physically resembled the Great Stone Face, his expression was shrewd, selfish, and materialistic. He lacked the noble, grand, and gentle expression that Ernest associated with the prophecy.','AI','Tests understanding of the theme of appearance versus character in the first part of the story.','hindi',161),(188,'Long Answer','Hard','The moral lesson often attributed to \'The Ant and the Cricket\' is \'work today, play tomorrow\'. Discuss how this lesson applies to modern life, focusing on themes like planning and procrastination. (5 Marks)','null',5,'The fable ‘The Ant and the Cricket’ strongly advocates for foresight and preparation. In modern life, this translates to planning for future security (savings, retirement funds) and consistent effort towards long-term goals (education, career). The Ant represents diligence and discipline, understanding that resources are finite and that comfortable times must be used to prepare for scarcity (winter). The Cricket symbolizes reckless procrastination and a lack of responsibility, prioritizing immediate pleasure over future needs. The moral teaches that failing to plan leads to hardship and dependency, reinforcing the necessity of hard work and responsible resource management in achieving stability and success.','AI','This requires interpreting the basic moral and applying it critically to contemporary issues, requiring higher-order thinking.','hindi',161),(189,'Long Answer','Medium','In \'Geography Lesson\', the poet observes the earth from three different altitudes. What is the central message or realization the poet draws from this journey about human actions and boundaries?','null',5,'The central message is the irony and futility of human conflict and division. From the first two altitudes, the poet recognizes the logical reasons for settlement (near water, following geography). However, at the highest altitude (six miles up), the poet realizes that the earth is one—round, and there is \'no reason why the men on earth/Hated each other, to build walls across cities and to kill.\' The journey reveals that physical boundaries and hatred are artificial human constructs, contrasting sharply with the unified, logical beauty of the planet seen from above.','AI','Asks for synthesis of the poem\'s thematic progression and core philosophical insight.','hindi',161),(190,'Long Answer','Medium','Major H.P.S. Ahluwalia experienced both the physical exhilaration of climbing Everest and the intellectual satisfaction of climbing the \'inner summit\'. Compare and contrast these two experiences as described in \'The Summit Within\'.','null',5,'The physical climb (Everest) involves tangible struggle, exhaustion, danger, and the visual reward of reaching the peak. The feeling is one of conquest over obstacles and achieving a specific goal, resulting in outward physical pride. The \'inner summit\' is intangible; it involves conquering the fear, doubt, and limitations within oneself. While the physical climb ends, the inner climb remains—it is a continuous, reflective process. Both experiences lead to profound joy and a sense of mastery. However, the inner summit is more enduring, providing spiritual fulfillment and a realization of one\'s place in the universe, which Ahluwalia ultimately considers the more rewarding achievement.','AI','Requires comparing the physical and metaphorical concepts presented in the essay.','hindi',161),(191,'Long Answer','Medium','Jody’s decision to risk finding and rearing the fawn shows maturity far beyond his years. Justify this statement by detailing his actions and attitude throughout the search.','null',5,'Jody demonstrates exceptional maturity through his sense of moral responsibility and independent action. Firstly, he insists on returning for the fawn, acknowledging their debt to the doe. Secondly, he manipulates Mill-wheel into leaving him alone so he can manage the emotional situation privately, showing sensitivity. Thirdly, he tracks the fawn methodically, patiently offering milk and carefully carrying it, showing practical resourcefulness. His decision to raise a wild animal alone, his compassion, and his quiet determination illustrate a deep character strength uncommon for a child, prioritizing the fawn’s life over his own immediate comfort or fear.','AI','Asks for a character analysis based on specific plot details.','hindi',161),(192,'Long Answer','Hard','How does Firdaus Kanga’s perspective on disability and suffering compare with that of Stephen Hawking, and what common ground did they find during their meeting in \'A Visit to Cambridge\'?','null',5,'Kanga initially feels oppressed by his own disability, viewing the world as unkind to the disabled. He seeks a \'grief tourism\' connection with Hawking. Hawking, however, seems to have transcended self-pity, focusing entirely on intellectual pursuits and refusing to be defined by his chair. The common ground they find is the intense sense of isolation inherent in severe disability—the struggle of communicating and the difficulty of finding the necessary compassion in society. Kanga realizes that Hawking is a symbol of possibility, leading Kanga to shed his victimhood and find strength in his own identity, concluding that the disabled should accept life as it is and move forward.','AI','This requires evaluating and comparing the philosophical views of two characters on a complex theme.','hindi',161),(193,'Long Answer','Easy','How does Ruskin Bond use descriptive language and literary devices to capture the sensory experience of the monsoon season in Mussoorie in \'A Short Monsoon Diary\'?','null',5,'Bond uses evocative imagery and sensory details to bring the monsoon to life. He uses sound imagery (\'the steady drumming of the rain\') and visual imagery (the \'mist\' that \'conceals the hills\'). Personification is used when the hills are \'veiled in mist\'. He uses descriptive words like \'silent,\' \'lush,\' and \'melancholy\' to establish mood. The diary format allows him to detail the specific changes in flora and fauna (the appearance of leeches, the visibility of the leopard), grounding the abstract \'monsoon\' in concrete, relatable sensory experiences of the location.','AI','Focuses on identifying and analyzing literary techniques used for description.','hindi',161),(194,'Long Answer','Medium','In the end, Ernest finally resembles the Great Stone Face. What fundamental quality did Ernest possess that was missing in the other contenders (Gathergold, Old Blood-and-Thunder, and Old Stony Phiz)?','null',5,'The fundamental quality missing in the others was genuine nobility of character, rooted in humility, contemplation, and lifelong dedication to good deeds. Gathergold had wealth, but lacked compassion. Old Blood-and-Thunder had military glory, but lacked gentle wisdom. Old Stony Phiz had eloquent words, but lacked sincerity of heart. Ernest, conversely, spent his entire life humbly serving his community, thinking deeply, and loving the Face. His thoughts shaped his character, and it was this purity and deep-seated goodness, acknowledged by the poet, that finally made him the embodiment of the prophecy.','AI','Requires evaluating the moral failures of the three characters versus the integrity of Ernest.','hindi',161),(195,'Matching','Medium','Match the characters/poetic concepts to their corresponding chapter/poem:','null',5,'Jim Macpherson - The Best Christmas Present in the World; The Summit - The Summit Within; Marmalade - A Short Monsoon Diary; The Grasshopper - On the Grasshopper and Cricket; Penny - This Is Jody’s Fawn','AI','This tests recall of key names and concepts from the respective lessons.','hindi',161),(196,'Case Study','Medium','Read the extract from \'The Best Christmas Present in the World\' and answer the following questions:\n\n\'I had found Jim Macpherson’s last letter, received January 25, 1915. I bought the rolling-top desk. I had intended to keep it, but curiosity got the better of me. Inside the secret drawer, there was a small tin box. Pasted to the top of it was a piece of lined notepaper, and written on it in shaky handwriting: \'Jim’s last letter, to be received by me by Christmas Day, 1915.\'\n\n1. What object contained the secret compartment?\n2. What was the inscription written on the tin box?\n3. Why did the narrator decide to open the secret compartment?\n4. What date was written on the letter?','null',4,'1. A rolling-top desk.\n2. \'Jim’s last letter, to be received by me by Christmas Day, 1915.\'\n3. Curiosity got the better of him.\n4. January 25, 1915.','AI','Tests close reading and retrieval of details from the provided passage.','hindi',161),(197,'Case Study','Easy','Read the extract from \'The Tsunami\' and answer the following questions:\n\n\'Ignatius lost his wife, two children, his father-in-law, and his brother-in-law in the morning. He did not want to rush them, but he did not know that these were the last moments he would spend with them. In the confusion, two of his children caught hold of the hands of their mother’s father and mother’s brother, and rushed in the opposite direction. He never saw them again.\'\n\n1. What relationship did Ignatius lose in the Tsunami according to the passage?\n2. What happened to Ignatius’s two children during the confusion?\n3. Why did Ignatius not rush his family earlier?\n4. What is the implied fate of the children in the last line?','null',4,'1. Wife, two children, father-in-law, and brother-in-law.\n2. They caught hold of the hands of their maternal grandfather and uncle and ran in the opposite direction.\n3. He did not know these were their last moments together (lack of awareness of imminent danger).\n4. They died/were lost forever in the Tsunami.','AI','Tests literal comprehension of the narrative details about the tragedy.','hindi',161),(198,'Case Study','Easy','Read the stanza from \'The Duck and the Kangaroo\' and answer the following questions:\n\n\'Said the Duck, ‘As I sat on the rocks,\nI have thought over that completely,\nAnd I bought four pairs of worsted socks,\nWhich fit my web-feet neatly.\nAnd to keep out the cold I’ve bought a cloak,\nAnd every day a cigar I’ll smoke,\nAll to follow my own dear true\nLove of a Kangaroo!\'\n\n1. What previous concern of the Kangaroo is the Duck addressing?\n2. What did the Duck buy to cover its feet?\n3. What two things did the Duck buy to keep warm?\n4. What does the Duck express towards the Kangaroo in the last line?','null',4,'1. The Duck\'s wet and cold feet, which could give the Kangaroo rheumatism.\n2. Four pairs of worsted socks.\n3. A cloak and a cigar (which he will smoke every day).\n4. Deep affection/love.','AI','Tests literal recall of the preparations made by the Duck in the poem.','hindi',161),(199,'Case Study','Medium','Read the extract from \'The Great Stone Face – I\' and answer the following questions:\n\n\'He spent his childhood in the valley, and worked hard upon the farm, and was dutiful to his mother, and helpful to his neighbours. In the latter part of the day, when his work was over, he would always turn his gaze to the Great Stone Face. His grandmother had told him that a man born in the valley, who resembled the Face, was destined to appear.\'\n\n1. Where did the boy (Ernest) spend his childhood?\n2. Name two qualities mentioned that describe Ernest’s behavior.\n3. What did Ernest always do when his daily work was done?\n4. What was the prophecy associated with the Great Stone Face, according to his grandmother?','null',4,'1. In the valley (upon the farm).\n2. Dutiful to his mother and helpful to his neighbors (or worked hard).\n3. He would turn his gaze to the Great Stone Face.\n4. A man born in the valley, resembling the Face, was destined to appear.','AI','Tests comprehension of the initial description of Ernest and the prophecy.','hindi',161);
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `questions` ENABLE KEYS */;
